window.HM={"built":"2026-10-01","sessions":[{"id":"countdown","phase":1,"num":1,"title":"Countdown to your first hunt","minutes":10,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Countdown to your first hunt","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>About 2 weeks to go. Gear and practice take longer than you think. This is the order that works.</p></aside>"},{"title":"The big picture","html":"<ul><li><strong>Week 1 (now):</strong> licences, buy the shotgun, buy and mount the scope.</li><li><strong>Week 2:</strong> sight in the rifle, pattern the shotgun, buy clothing, scout on the map.</li><li><strong>Hunt week:</strong> final checks, pack, go.</li><li>Dates are a plan, not a rule. Move them to fit your life.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Licences and the scope come first. Without a sighted in rifle there is no deer hunt. Without a federal permit there is no duck hunt.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Week 1: paperwork and the big buys","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"cd-w1\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:0\"><span>Find my FWID (Fish and Wildlife ID) in WILD online</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:1\"><span>Buy BC hunting licence online</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:2\"><span>Buy mule deer and white tailed deer licences IN PERSON</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:3\"><span>Buy the federal Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit online</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:4\"><span>Pick a shotgun (Session 3) and buy it</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:5\"><span>Buy steel shot for ducks and lead or steel for quail (Session 6)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:6\"><span>Pick a scope and Picatinny rings (Session 4) and buy them</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:7\"><span>Have the shop mount the scope, or mount it with a torque wrench</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:8\"><span>Buy .308 ammo: 3 to 4 boxes of the SAME load</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w1:9\"><span>Ask your brother: is his own house on the 20 acres? Where are the neighbours?</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Week 2: practice and kit","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"cd-w2\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:0\"><span>Bore sight, then zero the rifle at 100 m (Session 5)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:1\"><span>Shoot 3 shot groups from a rest until they are tight</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:2\"><span>Shoot from sitting and kneeling with shooting sticks</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:3\"><span>Pattern the shotgun on paper with your duck load (Session 5)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:4\"><span>Practice on clay targets, at least 1 session</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:5\"><span>Buy clothing, boots, waders (Session 7)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:6\"><span>Build the day pack and car kit (Session 8)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:7\"><span>Download offline maps of the area to your phone</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:8\"><span>Scout on the map: slopes, benches, water, roads (Sessions 9 to 11)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w2:9\"><span>Practice the legal to shoot flowchart in Field Mode</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Hunt week","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"cd-w3\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w3:0\"><span>Check in season regulation changes and closures</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w3:1\"><span>Check fire restrictions and road closures</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w3:2\"><span>Check legal light times in Field Mode</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w3:3\"><span>Leave a trip plan with someone</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w3:4\"><span>Pack: licences, ID, permit, ammo, knife, game bags, first aid</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w3:5\"><span>Cooler and ice ready for meat</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"cd-w3:6\"><span>Go slow. Watch more than you walk.</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Time savers","html":"<ul><li>Buy where you can handle the gun and get help mounting the scope. (Tip)</li><li>Ask the store about a range near Kamloops. Many have sighting in days. (Tip)</li><li>One ammo load for the rifle. Do not mix brands after zeroing. (Tip, 85% the zero changes between loads)</li><li>Practice loading and unloading at the car until it is boring. (Tip)</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Buy once, practice twice. The rifle you shot 60 times beats the fancy one you shot 6 times.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Mounting the scope the night before the hunt.</li><li>Buying 5 kinds of ammo and never zeroing any of them.</li><li>Spending every evening shopping and none on the range.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Tonight: put your hunt date in More. Then tick off the first 3 boxes above.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li>Licence facts: see Session 2 sources.</li><li>Everything else in this session is a plan and tips (opinion), not rules.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What should you do first this week?","options":["Buy clothing","Sort licences and buy the scope","Scout on the map"],"answer":1,"why":"Deer tags can take time to get and the rifle must be zeroed before you can hunt deer."},{"q":"You zeroed with Brand A. The store is out, so you buy Brand B. What now?","options":["Nothing, .308 is .308","Check the zero again with Brand B","Aim a bit higher"],"answer":1,"why":"Different loads hit different spots. Confirm the zero with the exact ammo you will hunt with."},{"q":"Why pattern the shotgun before duck season?","options":["It is required by law","To see where and how tight your shot hits with your load and choke","To break in the barrel"],"answer":1,"why":"Patterning shows if the gun shoots where you look and how the shot spreads at real distances."}],"checklists":["cd-w1","cd-w2","cd-w3"],"text":" !why about 2 weeks to go. gear and practice take longer than you think. this is the order that works. the big picture - week 1 (now): licences, buy the shotgun, buy and mount the scope. - week 2: sight in the rifle, pattern the shotgun, buy clothing, scout on the map. - hunt week: final checks, pack, go. - dates are a plan, not a rule. move them to fit your life. !lean licences and the scope come first. without a sighted in rifle there is no deer hunt. without a federal permit there is no duck hunt. week 1: paperwork and the big buys week 2: practice and kit hunt week time savers - buy where you can handle the gun and get help mounting the scope. (tip) - ask the store about a range near kamloops. many have sighting in days. (tip) - one ammo load for the rifle. do not mix brands after zeroing. (tip, 85% the zero changes between loads) - practice loading and unloading at the car until it is boring. (tip) !rule buy once, practice twice. the rifle you shot 60 times beats the fancy one you shot 6 times. !mistake - mounting the scope the night before the hunt. - buying 5 kinds of ammo and never zeroing any of them. - spending every evening shopping and none on the range. !field tonight: put your hunt date in more. then tick off the first 3 boxes above. sources - licence facts: see session 2 sources. - everything else in this session is a plan and tips (opinion), not rules. "},{"id":"licences","phase":1,"num":2,"title":"Licences and tags","minutes":12,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Licences and tags","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>No licence, no hunt. Some tags take 14 days by mail. Buy in person now.</p></aside>"},{"title":"What you need, by animal","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hunting</th><th>You need</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deer</td><td>BC hunting licence + deer species licence (tag) for that species</td></tr><tr><td>Ducks and geese</td><td>BC hunting licence + federal bird permit</td></tr><tr><td>Quail and grouse</td><td>BC hunting licence only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Residents need no species licence for quail or grouse <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Ducks and geese need no BC species licence, only the federal permit <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Mule deer and white tailed deer are <strong>separate</strong> species licences <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Your hunter number (FWID)","html":"<ul><li>Your old hunter number is now the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"FWID\">FWID</button> (Fish and Wildlife ID) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>It is 9 digits: your old number with 3 digits added at the front <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>There is no physical card.</strong> You do not need to carry a printout <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>You must carry <strong>photo ID</strong> and be able to give your FWID number <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Not sure of yours? Log in to WILD (Wildlife Information and Licensing Data) and tap <strong>Find my FWID</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>No luck? FrontCounter BC: 1 877 855 3222 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The hunting licence","html":"<ul><li>One per licence year. Licence year runs 1 April to 31 March <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Adult resident cost: $25 + $7 surcharge <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. GST (Goods and Services Tax): <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>It is <strong>electronic only</strong>. No paper copy exists <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Buy online in WILD, or at Service BC or FrontCounter BC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Deer tags (species licences)","html":"<ul><li>Mule deer: $12 + $3 surcharge. White tailed deer: $12 + $3 surcharge <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Cannot be printed at home.</strong> They are special waterproof paper <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Online orders are mailed. About 14 days <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Hunting in the next 2 weeks? <strong>Buy in person</strong> at Service BC, FrontCounter BC or a licence vendor <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Some species have a 2 day wait after purchase. For deer: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Carry <strong>all</strong> your species licences for the year, used and unused <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Buy both a mule deer and a white tailed deer licence. $30 total. You will not know which deer walks out first.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Federal bird permit (ducks and geese)","html":"<ul><li>Name: Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit. The habitat stamp is included <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Cost: $17 + tax <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>2026 e permit valid 1 August 2026 to 30 June 2027 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Buy online any time with a credit card. Save it on your phone <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Carry it every time you hunt ducks or geese, paper or phone <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Your brother","html":"<ul><li>He has CORE (Conservation and Outdoor Recreation Education), a PAL (Possession and Acquisition Licence) and an FWID. He can hunt too.</li><li>He needs his <strong>own</strong> BC hunting licence, his <strong>own</strong> deer species licences and his <strong>own</strong> federal bird permit <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Each hunter fills <strong>their own</strong> tags. You cannot notch your tag for his deer (80%, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> party hunting rules).</li><li>Anyone without a licence can still come along to watch and help pack out, if they carry no firearm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Buy it this week","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s2-buy\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s2-buy:0\"><span>Find my FWID in WILD</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s2-buy:1\"><span>Buy BC hunting licence (online is fine, it is electronic)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s2-buy:2\"><span>Buy mule deer licence IN PERSON</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s2-buy:3\"><span>Buy white tailed deer licence IN PERSON</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s2-buy:4\"><span>Buy federal bird permit online, save to phone</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s2-buy:5\"><span>Photo ID and PAL card in wallet</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Licences go in a zip bag in the same pocket every time. Check the pocket before you leave the truck.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Ordering deer tags online a week before the hunt. They arrive too late.</li><li>Thinking the hunting licence covers ducks. You also need the federal permit.</li><li>Leaving used tags at home. Carry them all.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Open WILD tonight. Find your FWID. Write it on the back of your PAL card in pencil.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/wild-system/fish-wildlife-id\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Fish and Wildlife ID page</a>, checked 2026-10-01</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC species licences page</a>, checked 2026-10-01</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/frequently-asked-questions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC hunting FAQ</a>, checked 2026-10-01</li><li><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/permit.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ECCC permit page</a>, checked 2026-10-01</li><li>Note: read through search previews of these official pages. Capped at 85% until the full pages are opened.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Do you need to carry a hunter number card?","options":["Yes, always","No. Carry photo ID and know your FWID number","Only for deer"],"answer":1,"why":"BC replaced the card with the FWID. Carry photo ID and be able to give the number."},{"q":"You hunt deer in 10 days. How do you get the species licence?","options":["Order online, it prints at home","Buy in person","You do not need one"],"answer":1,"why":"Species licences cannot be printed at home and online orders are mailed, about 14 days."},{"q":"What do you need to shoot a mallard?","options":["BC hunting licence only","BC hunting licence and the federal Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit","A duck species licence"],"answer":1,"why":"Ducks are federal. You need the BC hunting licence plus the federal permit."},{"q":"Can an unlicensed friend carry your rifle on the walk in?","options":["Yes if it is unloaded","No. Having a firearm while out after game can count as hunting","Yes on Crown land"],"answer":1,"why":"Under the Wildlife Act, searching for wildlife while in possession of a firearm is hunting. They would need a licence."}],"checklists":["s2-buy"],"text":" !why no licence, no hunt. some tags take 14 days by mail. buy in person now. what you need, by animal hunting you need --- --- deer bc hunting licence + deer species licence (tag) for that species ducks and geese bc hunting licence + federal bird permit quail and grouse bc hunting licence only - residents need no species licence for quail or grouse (80%). - ducks and geese need no bc species licence, only the federal permit (80%). - mule deer and white tailed deer are separate species licences (80%). your hunter number (fwid) - your old hunter number is now the fwid (fish and wildlife id) (85%). - it is 9 digits: your old number with 3 digits added at the front (85%). - there is no physical card. you do not need to carry a printout (85%). - you must carry photo id and be able to give your fwid number (85%). - not sure of yours? log in to wild (wildlife information and licensing data) and tap find my fwid (85%). - no luck? frontcounter bc: 1 877 855 3222 (80%). the hunting licence - one per licence year. licence year runs 1 april to 31 march (85%). - adult resident cost: $25 + $7 surcharge (80%). gst (goods and services tax): verify. - it is electronic only . no paper copy exists (85%). - buy online in wild, or at service bc or frontcounter bc (85%). deer tags (species licences) - mule deer: $12 + $3 surcharge. white tailed deer: $12 + $3 surcharge (80%). - cannot be printed at home. they are special waterproof paper (85%). - online orders are mailed. about 14 days (85%). - hunting in the next 2 weeks? buy in person at service bc, frontcounter bc or a licence vendor (85%). - some species have a 2 day wait after purchase. for deer: verify. - carry all your species licences for the year, used and unused (85%). !lean buy both a mule deer and a white tailed deer licence. $30 total. you will not know which deer walks out first. federal bird permit (ducks and geese) - name: migratory game bird hunting permit. the habitat stamp is included (80%). - cost: $17 + tax (80%). - 2026 e permit valid 1 august 2026 to 30 june 2027 (80%). - buy online any time with a credit card. save it on your phone (85%). - carry it every time you hunt ducks or geese, paper or phone (85%). your brother - he has core (conservation and outdoor recreation education), a pal (possession and acquisition licence) and an fwid. he can hunt too. - he needs his own bc hunting licence, his own deer species licences and his own federal bird permit (85%). - each hunter fills their own tags. you cannot notch your tag for his deer (80%, verify party hunting rules). - anyone without a licence can still come along to watch and help pack out, if they carry no firearm (80%). buy it this week !rule licences go in a zip bag in the same pocket every time. check the pocket before you leave the truck. !mistake - ordering deer tags online a week before the hunt. they arrive too late. - thinking the hunting licence covers ducks. you also need the federal permit. - leaving used tags at home. carry them all. !field open wild tonight. find your fwid. write it on the back of your pal card in pencil. sources - bc fish and wildlife id page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/wild-system/fish-wildlife-id), checked 2026-10-01 - bc species licences page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences), checked 2026-10-01 - bc hunting faq (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/frequently-asked-questions), checked 2026-10-01 - eccc permit page (https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/permit.html), checked 2026-10-01 - note: read through search previews of these official pages. capped at 85% until the full pages are opened. "},{"id":"first-duck-hunt","phase":1,"num":9,"title":"Your first duck hunt","minutes":15,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Your first duck hunt","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Ducks are the fastest way to get real shooting, real ID practice and real meat this October. A simple plan beats a fancy spread.</p></aside><ul><li>Two hunters: you and your brother. He has CORE (Conservation and Outdoor Recreation Education), a PAL (Possession and Acquisition Licence) and an FWID (Fish and Wildlife ID).</li><li>He still needs his own BC hunting licence and federal <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MGBHP\">MGBHP</button> (Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit) before he carries a shotgun <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Assumption: no dog, a 12 gauge with steel shot, a 10 ft boat and maybe a kayak.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Season, hours, limits","html":"<ul><li>Seasons are set by <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"ECCC\">ECCC</button> (Environment and Climate Change Canada), not the BC synopsis tables. Kamloops area duck dates for 2026 to 2027: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>ECCC dates are listed by MU (Management Unit). Find the MU for each pond on the map. Your exact MU and dates: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Daily bag: 8 ducks. Inside that, max 4 pintails, 4 canvasbacks, 2 goldeneyes, 2 harlequins <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Possession: 24 ducks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Legal hours: half an hour before sunrise to half an hour after sunset <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Deer and quail hours are different (1 hour each side). Do not mix them up <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Season dates near Kamloops are blank on purpose. Read the ECCC BC summary for 2026 to 2027 before your first hunt.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Ducks you will see near Kamloops","html":"<ul><li><strong>Mallard.</strong> Big. Drake has a green head. Blue wing patch with white edges. Slow, steady wingbeats <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Green winged teal.</strong> Small and very fast. Tight flocks twist as one unit. Often early migrants <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>American wigeon.</strong> Drake has a white crown stripe. Big white shoulder patch shows in flight. Whistled call <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Goldeneye (diver).</strong> Black and white. Wings whistle in flight. Bag limit only 2 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Bufflehead.</strong> Small black and white diver. Mostly arrives later, into winter <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Mallards and Canada geese are common on the Thompson River all year <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Which species and how many in mid October near Heffley Creek: Tip, varies by year.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Flight silhouettes","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 440\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four simple duck flight silhouettes: mallard large with broad wings, teal small and fast in tight flocks, wigeon with white wing patch, goldeneye diver with wings set far back and white sides.\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"440\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"430\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <line x1=\"10\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <!-- Mallard: big body, broad wings -->\n  <g>\n    <path d=\"M30 95 L100 70 L170 95 L100 88 Z\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"38\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"56\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <rect x=\"66\" y=\"84\" width=\"16\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#4a5fa8\"/>\n    <rect x=\"118\" y=\"84\" width=\"16\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#4a5fa8\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"168\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mallard</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"188\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Big. Slow wingbeats.</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"205\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Loose groups.</text>\n  </g>\n  <!-- Teal: small, flock of three -->\n  <g fill=\"#23261f\">\n    <path d=\"M240 80 L268 68 L296 80 L268 77 Z\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"268\" cy=\"84\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"17\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"268\" cy=\"64\" r=\"5\"/>\n    <path d=\"M300 110 L328 98 L356 110 L328 107 Z\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"328\" cy=\"114\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"17\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"328\" cy=\"94\" r=\"5\"/>\n    <path d=\"M250 130 L278 118 L306 130 L278 127 Z\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"278\" cy=\"134\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"17\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"278\" cy=\"114\" r=\"5\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"168\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Green winged teal</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"188\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Small. Very fast.</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"205\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Tight flock twists.</text>\n  <!-- Wigeon: white shoulder patch, pointed tail -->\n  <g>\n    <path d=\"M40 305 L100 282 L160 305 L100 298 Z\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"308\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"32\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"94,338 106,338 100,352\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"270\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <rect x=\"96\" y=\"264\" width=\"8\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"72,293 90,288 90,296 72,300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"128,293 110,288 110,296 128,300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"378\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wigeon</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"398\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">White wing patch.</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"415\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Whistle call.</text>\n  </g>\n  <!-- Goldeneye / diver: wings set back, short neck, white sides -->\n  <g>\n    <ellipse cx=\"300\" cy=\"300\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"34\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <path d=\"M244 318 L300 300 L356 318 L300 312 Z\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"262,312 282,307 282,314 262,318\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"338,312 318,307 318,314 338,318\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"262\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"305\" cy=\"266\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"378\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Goldeneye (diver)</text>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"398\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wings set far back.</text>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"415\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wings whistle.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Puddle ducks (mallard, teal, wigeon) jump straight up off the water <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Divers (goldeneye, bufflehead) run along the water to take off. Wings sit far back on the body <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hen ducks look alike. If you cannot tell a goldeneye from a mallard hen, do not shoot until it is identified (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Three ways to hunt","html":"<h3>Decoys over water</h3><ul><li>Pros: birds come to you, slow and close. Best shots. Best way to learn.</li><li>Cons: need decoys (6 to 12 is enough to start), a hide, and a way to fetch birds.</li></ul><h3>Jump shooting</h3><ul><li>Sneak along a creek, slough or pond edge. Ducks flush close.</li><li>Pros: cheap, active, no decoys. Good with 2 hunters.</li><li>Cons: hard fast shots. Birds may fall into deep or fast water. Safety needs a plan.</li></ul><h3>Pass shooting</h3><ul><li>Stand under a flight line between roost and food.</li><li>Pros: no gear.</li><li>Cons: long, crossing shots. More wounded birds. Hard for beginners.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Jump shoot small calm water first. Then a small decoy spread on a pond with a shallow shore. Skip pass shooting until you are hitting clay targets well. Pick water where any bird you drop can be reached by wading or by boat.</p></aside>"},{"title":"The J hook spread","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 440\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"J hook decoy spread: wind blows from left to right, the main bunch of decoys sits upwind beside the blind, a tail line trails downwind, ducks fly in against the wind and land in the open pocket in front of the blind.\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"440\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"60\" width=\"380\" height=\"290\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"85\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Open water</text>\n  <g>\n    <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"32\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"32\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"6\" fill=\"none\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"150,20 174,32 150,44\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n    <text x=\"190\" y=\"38\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\">WIND</text>\n  </g>\n  <g fill=\"#6b4f35\">\n    <ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"280\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"62\" cy=\"255\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"78\" cy=\"232\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"96\" cy=\"210\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"50\" cy=\"228\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"72\" cy=\"196\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"102\" cy=\"180\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"130\" cy=\"166\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"156\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"192\" cy=\"150\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"224\" cy=\"146\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"256\" cy=\"142\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"288\" cy=\"138\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"320\" cy=\"134\" rx=\"11\" ry=\"6\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"312\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Main bunch</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"328\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">(the hook)</text>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"118\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Tail line</text>\n  <ellipse cx=\"190\" cy=\"240\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"40\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 6\"/>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"236\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Landing zone</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"254\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">20 to 25 m out</text>\n  <g>\n    <path d=\"M370 210 Q 320 200 262 228\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"262,228 280,214 282,234\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <text x=\"268\" y=\"300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Ducks come in</text>\n    <text x=\"268\" y=\"317\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">against the wind</text>\n  </g>\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"350\" width=\"380\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#c9b79c\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"420\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Shore</text>\n  <rect x=\"150\" y=\"352\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"372\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">You (blind)</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"404\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Wind at your side or back.</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"422\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Never in your face.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Ducks land into the wind <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Main bunch upwind near you. Tail line trailing downwind <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Leave an open pocket 20 to 25 m (22 to 27 yd) out. That is where they land and where you shoot (Tip).</li><li>Wind at your side or back. Never in your face.</li><li>Keep shots inside about 35 m (38 yd) with steel (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Two hunters, one hide","html":"<ul><li>Agree on zones of fire before first light. You take left of centre, he takes right (Tip).</li><li>Never swing past your partner. Never shoot across the other person (Tip).</li><li>One person calls the shot: \"Take them.\" Then both stand or sit up, shoot your own side.</li><li>In the boat, <strong>one shooter at a time</strong>. The other runs the boat and watches (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Boat and kayak safety","html":"<ul><li><strong>Wear the PFD (Personal Flotation Device), zipped, every minute on the water.</strong> Carrying one is the legal minimum. Wearing it saves you <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Cold water: 1 minute to control your breathing, about 10 minutes of useful movement, up to 1 hour before you pass out from cold <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Never stand to shoot</strong> from a 10 ft boat or a kayak. Sit, or shoot from shore (Tip).</li><li>Load the gun only once the boat is stopped and you are in position. Unload before moving (Tip).</li><li>Kayak: use it to fetch birds, not to shoot from (Tip).</li><li>Waders in a boat can fill if you go in. Wear a belt on them (Tip).</li><li>Leave a trip plan. Shore return time.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Boat rules","html":"<ul><li>No hunting migratory birds from a boat moving under motor or sail power <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Retrieving a downed bird from a moving boat is allowed <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>BC: shooting from a power boat is allowed only beached, anchored, or tied to a fixed blind. A motor tilted up and not ready to use does not count as power <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Do not use a boat to chase or herd ducks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Motor on the boat? The driver needs a PCOC (Pleasure Craft Operator Card) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Motor size and boat registration: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Required kit for a small boat: a PFD for each person, a 15 m (50 ft) buoyant heaving line, a reboarding device if needed <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. Full list: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Fetching birds without a dog","html":"<ul><li><strong>The law:</strong> you must have a way to retrieve birds with you before you shoot. A bird you kill must be retrieved as soon as you can. A wounded bird must be killed and retrieved <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Plan the shot around the fetch: only shoot birds that will fall where you can reach them (Tip).</li><li>Tools: chest waders, the boat or kayak, a long handled landing net (Tip).</li><li>Wounded duck on the water: shoot again right away, on the water, while it is close (Tip).</li><li>Mark the fall: pick a tree or rock behind it. Go straight there (Tip).</li><li>Fast river current: do not wade. Let it go to shore downstream only if safe (Tip).</li><li>Keep one fully feathered wing or the head on each bird until home <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Steel shot","html":"<ul><li><strong>Lead shot is banned for duck hunting in Canada.</strong> Do not even carry it while duck hunting <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Legal types include steel, bismuth and tungsten <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Shotgun: 10 gauge or smaller, max 3 shells in the gun. Magazine must be plugged <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>No rifles for ducks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Starter load: 12 gauge, steel, size 2 or 3 (Tip). Pattern it first (Session 5).</li><li>Steel needs a choke rated for steel. Check the gun manual (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Find water you can legally hunt","html":"<ul><li>Do not hunt city water. There is a Kamloops no shooting and hunting area that includes river water near the city. Boundaries: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on map C7 in the Region 3 synopsis <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Is the North Thompson at Heffley Creek inside that area? <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> before you shoot there.</li><li>There is a provincial park on the North Thompson at Heffley Creek <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. Hunting allowed there? <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Skip it until confirmed.</li><li>Private ponds: written permission from the owner (Tip).</li><li>Crown land ponds: check the map for private land, parks and no shooting areas (Tip).</li><li>100 m (109 yd) from any occupied house or farm building, including your brother's <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"First hunt plan, step by step","html":"<ol><li>Night before: check sunrise, legal start (half an hour before), and the wind forecast.</li><li>Pack: licences, ID, both federal permits, steel shells, PFDs, waders, net, game bag, headlamps.</li><li>Arrive 1 hour before legal light. Set 6 to 12 decoys in a J hook with flashlights only.</li><li>Hide on shore with the wind at your side or back. Unload guns on the walk in until set.</li><li>Zones of fire agreed. Phone on silent. Watch the clock: no shot before legal time.</li><li>Let ducks commit to the pocket. Call the shot. Shoot your own side only.</li><li>Fetch each bird before the next flock if you can. Count birds by species.</li><li>Done by 10:00 or at your bag. Pick up every decoy and every empty shell.</li></ol><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s9-duckday\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s9-duckday:0\"><span>Season open today in my MU (checked ECCC summary)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s9-duckday:1\"><span>Both hunters: BC licence and federal permit on phone</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s9-duckday:2\"><span>Steel shot only in my pockets</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s9-duckday:3\"><span>Magazine plug in</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s9-duckday:4\"><span>PFDs worn, trip plan left</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s9-duckday:5\"><span>Zones of fire agreed</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s9-duckday:6\"><span>Legal start time written down</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s9-duckday:7\"><span>Way to fetch birds ready (waders, boat, net)</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>If you would not swim for it, do not shoot it. Shoot only where you can fetch.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Standing up in a small boat to swing on a passing bird.</li><li>Shooting a duck that falls in fast, deep water with no way to reach it.</li><li>Shooting before legal light because \"the others started.\"</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>This week: drive to two candidate ponds at dawn. Watch where ducks land and which way the wind blows. Count shots you would take that you could also fetch.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ECCC BC migratory bird hunting summary</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, bag limits 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/frequently-asked-questions.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ECCC hunting FAQ</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, lead shot and boats 80 to 85%.</li><li><a href=\"https://laws.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-2022-105/FullText.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Migratory Birds Regulations, 2022 (Justice Laws)</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, retrieval, boats, wing attached 75 to 80%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/regulations-synopsis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, power boat rules 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Region 3 synopsis</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, Kamloops no shooting area 70%.</li><li><a href=\"https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/sor-2010-91/page-4.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Small Vessel Regulations (Justice Laws)</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 70%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.lifesavingsociety.com/water-safety/cold-water-and-ice.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lifesaving Society cold water</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.ducks.ca/assets/2021/10/waterfowl-ID-guide-2020-EN-reader-spreads.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited Canada waterfowl ID guide</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 80%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.themeateater.com/hunt/waterfowl/a-primer-on-decoys-and-decoy-spreads-for-puddle-ducks\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MeatEater decoy spread primer</a>, checked 2026-10-01, tactics, 65%.</li><li>Note: official pages read through search previews only. Capped at 85%.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"When can you take your first shot at ducks?","options":["1 hour before sunrise","Half an hour before sunrise","At sunrise only"],"answer":1,"why":"Migratory bird hours are half an hour before sunrise to half an hour after sunset. Deer and quail are 1 hour."},{"q":"A duck drops in deep water and you have no waders, boat or net. What went wrong?","options":["Nothing, leave it","You took a shot you could not fetch. The law says you need a way to retrieve birds with you","You should have used lead"],"answer":1,"why":"You must have a way to retrieve birds before you hunt, and must retrieve them as soon as you can."},{"q":"Which shot can you carry while duck hunting?","options":["Lead or steel","Steel, bismuth or tungsten only","Any shot under size 4"],"answer":1,"why":"Lead shot is banned for most migratory game birds in Canada, even in your pocket."},{"q":"Where should the open landing pocket be in a J hook?","options":["Upwind of all decoys","In front of you, 20 to 25 m out, where ducks land into the wind","Behind you on shore"],"answer":1,"why":"Ducks land into the wind and like an open hole. Put it where you want to shoot."},{"q":"A flock comes in while you are in the 10 ft boat with your brother. What do you do?","options":["Both stand up and shoot","One shooter, seated, motor off and boat stopped","Chase them with the motor"],"answer":1,"why":"Stay seated in small boats. One shooter at a time. Never hunt from a boat moving under power."}],"checklists":["s9-duckday"],"text":" !why ducks are the fastest way to get real shooting, real id practice and real meat this october. a simple plan beats a fancy spread. - two hunters: you and your brother. he has core (conservation and outdoor recreation education), a pal (possession and acquisition licence) and an fwid (fish and wildlife id). - he still needs his own bc hunting licence and federal mgbhp (migratory game bird hunting permit) before he carries a shotgun (80%). - assumption: no dog, a 12 gauge with steel shot, a 10 ft boat and maybe a kayak. season, hours, limits - seasons are set by eccc (environment and climate change canada), not the bc synopsis tables. kamloops area duck dates for 2026 to 2027: verify. - eccc dates are listed by mu (management unit). find the mu for each pond on the map. your exact mu and dates: verify. - daily bag: 8 ducks. inside that, max 4 pintails, 4 canvasbacks, 2 goldeneyes, 2 harlequins (75%). - possession: 24 ducks (70%). - legal hours: half an hour before sunrise to half an hour after sunset (85%). - deer and quail hours are different (1 hour each side). do not mix them up (80%). !warn season dates near kamloops are blank on purpose. read the eccc bc summary for 2026 to 2027 before your first hunt. ducks you will see near kamloops - mallard. big. drake has a green head. blue wing patch with white edges. slow, steady wingbeats (80%). - green winged teal. small and very fast. tight flocks twist as one unit. often early migrants (80%). - american wigeon. drake has a white crown stripe. big white shoulder patch shows in flight. whistled call (80%). - goldeneye (diver). black and white. wings whistle in flight. bag limit only 2 (80%). - bufflehead. small black and white diver. mostly arrives later, into winter (65%). - mallards and canada geese are common on the thompson river all year (70%). - which species and how many in mid october near heffley creek: tip, varies by year. flight silhouettes - puddle ducks (mallard, teal, wigeon) jump straight up off the water (75%). - divers (goldeneye, bufflehead) run along the water to take off. wings sit far back on the body (75%). - hen ducks look alike. if you cannot tell a goldeneye from a mallard hen, do not shoot until it is identified (tip). three ways to hunt decoys over water - pros: birds come to you, slow and close. best shots. best way to learn. - cons: need decoys (6 to 12 is enough to start), a hide, and a way to fetch birds. jump shooting - sneak along a creek, slough or pond edge. ducks flush close. - pros: cheap, active, no decoys. good with 2 hunters. - cons: hard fast shots. birds may fall into deep or fast water. safety needs a plan. pass shooting - stand under a flight line between roost and food. - pros: no gear. - cons: long, crossing shots. more wounded birds. hard for beginners. !lean jump shoot small calm water first. then a small decoy spread on a pond with a shallow shore. skip pass shooting until you are hitting clay targets well. pick water where any bird you drop can be reached by wading or by boat. the j hook spread - ducks land into the wind (75%). - main bunch upwind near you. tail line trailing downwind (65%). - leave an open pocket 20 to 25 m (22 to 27 yd) out. that is where they land and where you shoot (tip). - wind at your side or back. never in your face. - keep shots inside about 35 m (38 yd) with steel (tip). two hunters, one hide - agree on zones of fire before first light. you take left of centre, he takes right (tip). - never swing past your partner. never shoot across the other person (tip). - one person calls the shot: \"take them.\" then both stand or sit up, shoot your own side. - in the boat, one shooter at a time . the other runs the boat and watches (tip). boat and kayak safety - wear the pfd (personal flotation device), zipped, every minute on the water. carrying one is the legal minimum. wearing it saves you (85%). - cold water: 1 minute to control your breathing, about 10 minutes of useful movement, up to 1 hour before you pass out from cold (75%). - never stand to shoot from a 10 ft boat or a kayak. sit, or shoot from shore (tip). - load the gun only once the boat is stopped and you are in position. unload before moving (tip). - kayak: use it to fetch birds, not to shoot from (tip). - waders in a boat can fill if you go in. wear a belt on them (tip). - leave a trip plan. shore return time. boat rules - no hunting migratory birds from a boat moving under motor or sail power (80%). - retrieving a downed bird from a moving boat is allowed (75%). - bc: shooting from a power boat is allowed only beached, anchored, or tied to a fixed blind. a motor tilted up and not ready to use does not count as power (75%). - do not use a boat to chase or herd ducks (75%). - motor on the boat? the driver needs a pcoc (pleasure craft operator card) (75%). motor size and boat registration: verify. - required kit for a small boat: a pfd for each person, a 15 m (50 ft) buoyant heaving line, a reboarding device if needed (70%). full list: verify. fetching birds without a dog - the law: you must have a way to retrieve birds with you before you shoot. a bird you kill must be retrieved as soon as you can. a wounded bird must be killed and retrieved (80%). - plan the shot around the fetch: only shoot birds that will fall where you can reach them (tip). - tools: chest waders, the boat or kayak, a long handled landing net (tip). - wounded duck on the water: shoot again right away, on the water, while it is close (tip). - mark the fall: pick a tree or rock behind it. go straight there (tip). - fast river current: do not wade. let it go to shore downstream only if safe (tip). - keep one fully feathered wing or the head on each bird until home (80%). steel shot - lead shot is banned for duck hunting in canada. do not even carry it while duck hunting (85%). - legal types include steel, bismuth and tungsten (85%). - shotgun: 10 gauge or smaller, max 3 shells in the gun. magazine must be plugged (80%). - no rifles for ducks (80%). - starter load: 12 gauge, steel, size 2 or 3 (tip). pattern it first (session 5). - steel needs a choke rated for steel. check the gun manual (tip). find water you can legally hunt - do not hunt city water. there is a kamloops no shooting and hunting area that includes river water near the city. boundaries: verify on map c7 in the region 3 synopsis (70%). - is the north thompson at heffley creek inside that area? verify before you shoot there. - there is a provincial park on the north thompson at heffley creek (70%). hunting allowed there? verify. skip it until confirmed. - private ponds: written permission from the owner (tip). - crown land ponds: check the map for private land, parks and no shooting areas (tip). - 100 m (109 yd) from any occupied house or farm building, including your brother's (75%). first hunt plan, step by step 1. night before: check sunrise, legal start (half an hour before), and the wind forecast. 2. pack: licences, id, both federal permits, steel shells, pfds, waders, net, game bag, headlamps. 3. arrive 1 hour before legal light. set 6 to 12 decoys in a j hook with flashlights only. 4. hide on shore with the wind at your side or back. unload guns on the walk in until set. 5. zones of fire agreed. phone on silent. watch the clock: no shot before legal time. 6. let ducks commit to the pocket. call the shot. shoot your own side only. 7. fetch each bird before the next flock if you can. count birds by species. 8. done by 10:00 or at your bag. pick up every decoy and every empty shell. !rule if you would not swim for it, do not shoot it. shoot only where you can fetch. !mistake - standing up in a small boat to swing on a passing bird. - shooting a duck that falls in fast, deep water with no way to reach it. - shooting before legal light because \"the others started.\" !field this week: drive to two candidate ponds at dawn. watch where ducks land and which way the wind blows. count shots you would take that you could also fetch. sources - eccc bc migratory bird hunting summary (https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, bag limits 75%. - eccc hunting faq (https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/frequently-asked-questions.html), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, lead shot and boats 80 to 85%. - migratory birds regulations, 2022 (justice laws) (https://laws.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/sor-2022-105/fulltext.html), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, retrieval, boats, wing attached 75 to 80%. - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028 (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/regulations-synopsis), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, power boat rules 75%. - region 3 synopsis (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, kamloops no shooting area 70%. - small vessel regulations (justice laws) (https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/sor-2010-91/page-4.html), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 70%. - lifesaving society cold water (https://www.lifesavingsociety.com/water-safety/cold-water-and-ice.aspx), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%. - ducks unlimited canada waterfowl id guide (https://www.ducks.ca/assets/2021/10/waterfowl-id-guide-2020-en-reader-spreads.pdf), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 80%. - meateater decoy spread primer (https://www.themeateater.com/hunt/waterfowl/a-primer-on-decoys-and-decoy-spreads-for-puddle-ducks), checked 2026-10-01, tactics, 65%. - note: official pages read through search previews only. capped at 85%. "},{"id":"first-deer-hunt","phase":1,"num":10,"title":"Your first deer hunt","minutes":15,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Your first deer hunt","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Late October near Heffley Creek is a real chance at a deer. A safe, simple plan you can repeat beats a long shot at a far ridge.</p></aside><ul><li>Two hunters: you and your brother. He has CORE (Conservation and Outdoor Recreation Education), a PAL (Possession and Acquisition Licence) and an FWID (Fish and Wildlife ID).</li><li>He needs his own BC hunting licence and deer <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Species licence\">species licences</button> before he hunts <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Assumption: your Tikka .308 is zeroed (Session 5). His rifle: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> what he will carry.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Mule deer or white tail?","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Mule deer</th><th>White tailed deer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Antlers</td><td>Fork, then fork again</td><td>One main beam, single tines up</td></tr><tr><td>Tail</td><td>Thin, rope like, black tip</td><td>Wide, brown on top, flags up white</td></tr><tr><td>Ears</td><td>Very big</td><td>Smaller</td></tr><tr><td>Runs</td><td>Bounces on all 4 feet</td><td>Gallops to cover</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>These field marks are reliable on clear looks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Mule deer often stop and look back at 150 to 300 m. Hunter lore <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>They are <strong>separate licences and separate rules</strong>. Know which one you are looking at before the safety comes off <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Rules you must check first","html":"<ul><li>Your MU (Management Unit) for the property and the Crown land behind it: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the official map.</li><li>Mule deer season dates and antler rules (for example a 4 point rule) in your MU: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>White tail season and any antlerless days in your MU: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Region 3 deer bag limit: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. A search preview said 3 deer combined, 1 of them mule deer <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Legal hours: 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>No MU, no season dates, no hunt. Read the Region 3 synopsis table for your exact MU before opening day.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Where deer are in late October","html":"<ul><li>Mule deer are the main deer in the southern Interior <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Mule deer use steeper, higher, open broken ground. White tails use valley bottoms, creeks and farm fields <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Local reading: mule deer on grassland and open Douglas fir benches above the valley. White tails in North Thompson hayfields and cottonwood bottoms <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Many mule deer move toward winter range in October to November. Snow and cold push them, not the rut <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Late October is early pre rut. Bucks start sparring. Bachelor groups break up <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Deer move most around dawn and dusk <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Day beds: timber edges, benches, draws, out of the wind. Sunny south and west slopes on cold days <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Glassing","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 420\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A hillside split into a 3 by 3 grid. Scan order runs 1 to 9 in a snake pattern: bottom row left to right, middle row right to left, top row left to right. Start near, finish far.\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"420\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <path d=\"M10 330 L10 120 Q 90 40 170 80 Q 240 30 310 70 Q 360 90 390 80 L390 330 Z\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <g fill=\"#2f3a2b\">\n    <polygon points=\"60,150 70,120 80,150\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"78,160 88,128 98,160\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"250,110 260,82 270,110\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"268,118 278,90 288,118\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"330,250 340,222 350,250\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\" fill=\"none\">\n    <line x1=\"137\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"137\" y2=\"330\"/>\n    <line x1=\"263\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"263\" y2=\"330\"/>\n    <line x1=\"10\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"160\"/>\n    <line x1=\"10\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"245\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"22\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <circle cx=\"73\" cy=\"290\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"73\" y=\"298\">1</text>\n    <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"290\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"298\">2</text>\n    <circle cx=\"326\" cy=\"290\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"326\" y=\"298\">3</text>\n    <circle cx=\"326\" cy=\"203\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"326\" y=\"211\">4</text>\n    <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"203\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"211\">5</text>\n    <circle cx=\"73\" cy=\"203\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"73\" y=\"211\">6</text>\n    <circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"125\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"133\">7</text>\n    <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"125\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"133\">8</text>\n    <circle cx=\"326\" cy=\"125\" r=\"17\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"326\" y=\"133\">9</text>\n  </g>\n  <g stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M92 290 L180 290 M219 290 L306 290\"/>\n    <path d=\"M343 290 Q 370 250 343 210\"/>\n    <path d=\"M306 203 L219 203 M180 203 L92 203\"/>\n    <path d=\"M56 203 Q 30 160 92 130\"/>\n    <path d=\"M128 125 L180 125 M219 125 L306 125\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"40\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Far: ridges, benches, timber edges</text>\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"330\" width=\"380\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#c9b79c\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"358\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n  <rect x=\"186\" y=\"370\" width=\"28\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"406\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">You, sitting, binoculars on a rest</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"352\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Start near</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"352\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">1 to 2 min</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"368\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">per square</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Glassing\">Glassing</button> finds more deer than walking. Sit, rest the binoculars, go slow (Tip).</li><li>Split the hillside into squares. Scan near first, then far, in a snake pattern (Tip).</li><li>1 to 2 minutes per square. Then do it again. Deer appear (Tip).</li><li>Look for parts, not whole deer: an ear flick, a white rump, a leg line, a horizontal back (Tip).</li><li>Best times: first light and the last 2 hours of light <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Binoculars find. The rifle scope is never used to look at people or unknown shapes (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Wind and thermals","html":"<ul><li>Smell is a deer's best sense. Keep the wind in your face or across you <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Morning: air warms and flows <strong>uphill</strong> later in the morning. Evening: air cools and flows <strong>downhill</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>So: hunt from above in the evening only if the wind lets you. Come from below in late morning (Tip).</li><li>Check wind with a puff bottle or a bit of milkweed fluff every 15 minutes (Tip).</li><li>Wind wrong? Leave that spot for today. Do not push it (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Scenario A: the property plus Crown behind it","html":"<ul><li>Your brother's house is on the 20 acres. Neighbours are 100 to 200 m away.</li><li><strong>No shooting within 100 m (109 yd) of an occupied house, farm or ranch building</strong> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. That includes his house.</li><li>So the usable area on the 20 acres may be small or zero. The Crown land behind is the real option.</li><li>Map it first: draw a 100 m circle around every house and barn. Mark safe shooting lanes and earth <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Backstop\">backstops</button> (Tip).</li><li>Mark the property line and the Crown edge. Walk it in daylight with a GPS (Global Positioning System) app (Tip).</li><li>Assign sides: you shoot left of the line, he shoots right. No shots toward any house, road or each other (Tip).</li><li>Pros: close to home, short pack out, easy to repeat dawn and dusk.</li><li>Cons: small area, people and pets nearby, deer may be few.</li><li>Odds: fair if deer cross the Crown edge. Opinion.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Scenario B: ATV on Forest Service Roads","html":"<ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"FSR\">FSR</button> (Forest Service Road) networks reach benches and cutblocks fast. Use them to get to glassing spots, then walk.</li><li><strong>No loaded firearm in or on a motor vehicle, and that includes an <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"ATV\">ATV</button> (All Terrain Vehicle)</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Loaded means a round in the chamber <strong>or</strong> in an attached magazine <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>No shooting from a motor vehicle</strong>, ATV included <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>No hunting or shooting on or across numbered highways and 2 lane public roads kept by the province, out to 15 m from the road centre <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>FSRs are not usually treated as those highways. Still never shoot along or across any road (60%, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>).</li><li>ATV on Crown land: needs registration and a plate. Helmet required <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Insurance on FSRs: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Motor vehicle closures in Region 3: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the synopsis maps.</li><li>Pros: covers ground, easy meat haul, beats walking in the dark.</li><li>Cons: road hunters push deer back from roads. Noise. Easy to break rules by accident.</li><li>Odds: good for finding deer, fair for a shot. Opinion.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Scenario C: walk in 5 to 10 km","html":"<ul><li>Quiet country away from roads. Fewer hunters (Tip).</li><li>Meat math for a mule deer (US agency figures):<ul><li>Live weight about 68 to 113 kg (150 to 250 lb) for a buck <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Field dressed about 54 to 86 kg (120 to 190 lb) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>A forkhorn or doe can give only about 23 kg (50 lb) of boned meat <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul></li><li>Bone in quarters for a mature buck: about 41 to 48 kg (90 to 105 lb) total. Hunter reports <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Two of you, about 20 to 25 kg each per trip: 1 trip boned out, 2 trips bone in (Tip).</li><li>Loaded and off trail you may do 2 km an hour. 8 km out is about 4 hours per trip (Tip).</li><li><strong>You must bring out the edible meat: neck, ribs, 4 quarters and loins</strong> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Pros: best chance at a calm, unpressured deer. Great skill builder.</li><li>Cons: a late shot means packing in the dark. Meat must cool fast.</li><li>Odds: good chance to see deer, hard first kill. Opinion.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Start with A and B together. Map the 100 m buffers and hunt the Crown edge behind the property at dawn. Use the ATV mid day to glass new benches. Save C for after your first deer, when you know the pack out and the cutting.</p></aside>"},{"title":"A hunt day","html":"<ul><li><strong>Night before:</strong> check legal light, wind, MU (Management Unit) rules, closures. Pack and charge everything.</li><li><strong>Sunrise minus 90 min:</strong> leave the house. Rifles unloaded and cased in the vehicle.</li><li><strong>Sunrise minus 60 min (legal light):</strong> sit at the first glassing spot. Load only when sitting and ready.</li><li><strong>First 3 hours:</strong> glass. Do not walk much.</li><li><strong>Mid morning:</strong> walk slowly into the wind, still hunting or checking sign. Eat, drink.</li><li><strong>Midday:</strong> move spots, scout benches, check trail cameras if you use them.</li><li><strong>3 hours before sunset:</strong> back on a glassing spot that faces where deer feed.</li><li><strong>Sunset plus 60 min:</strong> legal light ends. Unload. Walk out with headlamps.</li><li>Shot taken late? Mark it, wait, track carefully. See Field Mode \"After the shot\".</li></ul><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s10-deerday\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s10-deerday:0\"><span>MU and season checked for today</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s10-deerday:1\"><span>Both hunters: licences, species licences, ID on me</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s10-deerday:2\"><span>100 m buffer map on phone</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s10-deerday:3\"><span>Sides and shooting lanes agreed</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s10-deerday:4\"><span>Wind checker in pocket</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s10-deerday:5\"><span>Knife, game bags, headlamp, rope</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s10-deerday:6\"><span>Trip plan left</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s10-deerday:7\"><span>Rifle unloaded on ATV and in vehicle</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Look at the deer, then look past it. If you cannot see what the bullet will hit after the deer, you do not have a shot.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Shooting toward a house you forgot was there. Map the 100 m buffers first.</li><li>Keeping a round in the chamber on the ATV \"for a quick shot\".</li><li>Walking fast all morning instead of sitting and glassing.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>This week: walk the property line and the Crown edge with your brother. Drop a GPS pin at every house and barn. Draw 100 m circles on the map tonight.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/regulations-synopsis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, loaded firearm, vehicles, roads, edible portions 75 to 80%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Region 3 synopsis</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, bag limit 60%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting Regulation, 100 m rule</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/industry/crown-land-water/crown-land/crown-land-uses/off-road-vehicles/registration\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC off road vehicle registration</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://wgfd.wyo.gov/how-much-meat-can-i-get-deer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wyoming Game and Fish: how much meat from a deer</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://rokslide.com/forums/threads/weight-of-a-bone-in-mule-deer-hind-quarter.107100/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rokslide: weight of bone in mule deer quarters</a>, checked 2026-10-01, hunter reports, 60%.</li><li><a href=\"https://bcwf.bc.ca/initiatives/mule-deer-project/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Wildlife Federation mule deer project</a>, checked 2026-10-01, migration, 85%.</li><li>Research notes: <code>research/2026-10-01-deer.md</code> (ID, habitat, daily pattern, senses).</li><li>Note: official pages read through search previews only. Capped at 85%.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Your brother's house is on the property. How close to it can you shoot?","options":["Anywhere on his land with his OK","No closer than 100 m to an occupied house or farm building","50 m if the shot is away from it"],"answer":1,"why":"BC bans hunting and shooting within 100 m of an occupied dwelling or farm building. His permission does not change that."},{"q":"On the ATV, when may a round be in the magazine attached to the rifle?","options":["Always, if the chamber is empty","Never. A loaded magazine attached counts as a loaded firearm","Only on FSRs"],"answer":1,"why":"Loaded means a round in the chamber or in an attached magazine. No loaded firearm in or on a motor vehicle, ATV included."},{"q":"The deer bounces away on all 4 feet with a thin black tipped tail. Which licence?","options":["White tailed deer","Mule deer","Either one"],"answer":1,"why":"Stotting and a rope tail with a black tip are mule deer signs. Check the mule deer rules for your MU."},{"q":"It is a cold late afternoon. Which way is air moving on the slope?","options":["Uphill","Downhill","No movement"],"answer":1,"why":"Cooling air sinks. Evening thermals flow downhill, so deer below you can smell you."},{"q":"You shot a deer 8 km in. What must you bring out?","options":["Just the backstraps and antlers","The edible meat: neck, ribs, 4 quarters and loins","Whatever fits in one trip"],"answer":1,"why":"BC requires the edible portions to be removed. Plan 2 trips or bone it out."}],"checklists":["s10-deerday"],"text":" !why late october near heffley creek is a real chance at a deer. a safe, simple plan you can repeat beats a long shot at a far ridge. - two hunters: you and your brother. he has core (conservation and outdoor recreation education), a pal (possession and acquisition licence) and an fwid (fish and wildlife id). - he needs his own bc hunting licence and deer species licence species licences before he hunts (80%). - assumption: your tikka .308 is zeroed (session 5). his rifle: verify what he will carry. mule deer or white tail? mule deer white tailed deer --- --- --- antlers fork, then fork again one main beam, single tines up tail thin, rope like, black tip wide, brown on top, flags up white ears very big smaller runs bounces on all 4 feet gallops to cover - these field marks are reliable on clear looks (85%). - mule deer often stop and look back at 150 to 300 m. hunter lore (65%). - they are separate licences and separate rules . know which one you are looking at before the safety comes off (80%). rules you must check first - your mu (management unit) for the property and the crown land behind it: verify on the official map. - mule deer season dates and antler rules (for example a 4 point rule) in your mu: verify. - white tail season and any antlerless days in your mu: verify. - region 3 deer bag limit: verify. a search preview said 3 deer combined, 1 of them mule deer (60%). - legal hours: 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset (80%). !warn no mu, no season dates, no hunt. read the region 3 synopsis table for your exact mu before opening day. where deer are in late october - mule deer are the main deer in the southern interior (85%). - mule deer use steeper, higher, open broken ground. white tails use valley bottoms, creeks and farm fields (85%). - local reading: mule deer on grassland and open douglas fir benches above the valley. white tails in north thompson hayfields and cottonwood bottoms (75%). - many mule deer move toward winter range in october to november. snow and cold push them, not the rut (85%). - late october is early pre rut. bucks start sparring. bachelor groups break up (70%). - deer move most around dawn and dusk (85%). - day beds: timber edges, benches, draws, out of the wind. sunny south and west slopes on cold days (80%). glassing - glassing finds more deer than walking. sit, rest the binoculars, go slow (tip). - split the hillside into squares. scan near first, then far, in a snake pattern (tip). - 1 to 2 minutes per square. then do it again. deer appear (tip). - look for parts, not whole deer: an ear flick, a white rump, a leg line, a horizontal back (tip). - best times: first light and the last 2 hours of light (80%). - binoculars find. the rifle scope is never used to look at people or unknown shapes (tip). wind and thermals - smell is a deer's best sense. keep the wind in your face or across you (85%). - morning: air warms and flows uphill later in the morning. evening: air cools and flows downhill (80%). - so: hunt from above in the evening only if the wind lets you. come from below in late morning (tip). - check wind with a puff bottle or a bit of milkweed fluff every 15 minutes (tip). - wind wrong? leave that spot for today. do not push it (tip). scenario a: the property plus crown behind it - your brother's house is on the 20 acres. neighbours are 100 to 200 m away. - no shooting within 100 m (109 yd) of an occupied house, farm or ranch building (75%). that includes his house. - so the usable area on the 20 acres may be small or zero. the crown land behind is the real option. - map it first: draw a 100 m circle around every house and barn. mark safe shooting lanes and earth backstop backstops (tip). - mark the property line and the crown edge. walk it in daylight with a gps (global positioning system) app (tip). - assign sides: you shoot left of the line, he shoots right. no shots toward any house, road or each other (tip). - pros: close to home, short pack out, easy to repeat dawn and dusk. - cons: small area, people and pets nearby, deer may be few. - odds: fair if deer cross the crown edge. opinion. scenario b: atv on forest service roads - fsr (forest service road) networks reach benches and cutblocks fast. use them to get to glassing spots, then walk. - no loaded firearm in or on a motor vehicle, and that includes an atv (all terrain vehicle) (80%). - loaded means a round in the chamber or in an attached magazine (80%). - no shooting from a motor vehicle , atv included (80%). - no hunting or shooting on or across numbered highways and 2 lane public roads kept by the province, out to 15 m from the road centre (75%). - fsrs are not usually treated as those highways. still never shoot along or across any road (60%, verify). - atv on crown land: needs registration and a plate. helmet required (75%). insurance on fsrs: verify. - motor vehicle closures in region 3: verify on the synopsis maps. - pros: covers ground, easy meat haul, beats walking in the dark. - cons: road hunters push deer back from roads. noise. easy to break rules by accident. - odds: good for finding deer, fair for a shot. opinion. scenario c: walk in 5 to 10 km - quiet country away from roads. fewer hunters (tip). - meat math for a mule deer (us agency figures): - live weight about 68 to 113 kg (150 to 250 lb) for a buck (75%). - field dressed about 54 to 86 kg (120 to 190 lb) (75%). - a forkhorn or doe can give only about 23 kg (50 lb) of boned meat (75%). - bone in quarters for a mature buck: about 41 to 48 kg (90 to 105 lb) total. hunter reports (60%). - two of you, about 20 to 25 kg each per trip: 1 trip boned out, 2 trips bone in (tip). - loaded and off trail you may do 2 km an hour. 8 km out is about 4 hours per trip (tip). - you must bring out the edible meat: neck, ribs, 4 quarters and loins (75%). - pros: best chance at a calm, unpressured deer. great skill builder. - cons: a late shot means packing in the dark. meat must cool fast. - odds: good chance to see deer, hard first kill. opinion. !lean start with a and b together. map the 100 m buffers and hunt the crown edge behind the property at dawn. use the atv mid day to glass new benches. save c for after your first deer, when you know the pack out and the cutting. a hunt day - night before: check legal light, wind, mu (management unit) rules, closures. pack and charge everything. - sunrise minus 90 min: leave the house. rifles unloaded and cased in the vehicle. - sunrise minus 60 min (legal light): sit at the first glassing spot. load only when sitting and ready. - first 3 hours: glass. do not walk much. - mid morning: walk slowly into the wind, still hunting or checking sign. eat, drink. - midday: move spots, scout benches, check trail cameras if you use them. - 3 hours before sunset: back on a glassing spot that faces where deer feed. - sunset plus 60 min: legal light ends. unload. walk out with headlamps. - shot taken late? mark it, wait, track carefully. see field mode \"after the shot\". !rule look at the deer, then look past it. if you cannot see what the bullet will hit after the deer, you do not have a shot. !mistake - shooting toward a house you forgot was there. map the 100 m buffers first. - keeping a round in the chamber on the atv \"for a quick shot\". - walking fast all morning instead of sitting and glassing. !field this week: walk the property line and the crown edge with your brother. drop a gps pin at every house and barn. draw 100 m circles on the map tonight. sources - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028 (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/regulations-synopsis), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, loaded firearm, vehicles, roads, edible portions 75 to 80%. - region 3 synopsis (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, bag limit 60%. - bc hunting regulation, 100 m rule (https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%. - bc off road vehicle registration (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/industry/crown-land-water/crown-land/crown-land-uses/off-road-vehicles/registration), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%. - wyoming game and fish: how much meat from a deer (https://wgfd.wyo.gov/how-much-meat-can-i-get-deer), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%. - rokslide: weight of bone in mule deer quarters (https://rokslide.com/forums/threads/weight-of-a-bone-in-mule-deer-hind-quarter.107100/), checked 2026-10-01, hunter reports, 60%. - bc wildlife federation mule deer project (https://bcwf.bc.ca/initiatives/mule-deer-project/), checked 2026-10-01, migration, 85%. - research notes: research/2026-10-01-deer.md (id, habitat, daily pattern, senses). - note: official pages read through search previews only. capped at 85%. "},{"id":"first-quail-trip","phase":1,"num":11,"title":"Your first quail trip","minutes":12,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Your first quail trip","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Quail are fast, fun and live close to farms and homes. Most of the skill is getting permission and keeping every shot safe.</p></aside><ul><li>Trip: the Okanagan, Region 8. Two hunters with shotguns.</li><li>Assumption: no landowner permission yet. No dog.</li><li>Residents need only the BC hunting licence for quail. No species licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Season and limits","html":"<ul><li>Region 8 California quail: 1 October to 30 November <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Daily bag 10. Possession 30 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Open in MUs (Management Units) 8-1 to 8-15 and 8-21 to 8-26 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Read from a search preview of the Region 8 synopsis. Confirm in the full table before you go: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Legal hours: 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Federal lead shot ban covers ducks and geese, not quail <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Any BC rule on lead for quail: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Steel works fine (Tip).</li><li>Each hunter has their own bag. No filling your partner's limit (Tip, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> the exact wording).</li></ul>"},{"title":"California quail: habitat and habits","html":"<ul><li>In BC, mostly in the Okanagan and Kettle valleys and on southeast Vancouver Island <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>They live in farm and suburban edges with lots of cover, and in brushy creeks, grassland and open forest <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Fall and winter flocks are called coveys. Often family groups, sometimes very large <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>They feed mornings and evenings. They rest in shrubs midday and roost in trees <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>A lookout bird, often a male, watches while the rest feed <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>They would rather <strong>run</strong> than fly. When pushed, they burst up with loud whirring wings <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Where to look","html":"<ul><li>Brushy draws, blackberry and rose thickets, creek edges near grain or orchards (Tip).</li><li>Listen at first light. Their call carries far (Tip).</li><li>Look for dusting spots and tracks on dirt roads near cover (Tip).</li><li>After the first flush, singles hold tighter. Mark where they landed and follow up (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Best time, my opinion: the first 2 hours after legal light and the last 2 hours before sunset, on calm days. Late October and November, after leaves drop, you can see them better.</p></aside>"},{"title":"The covey flush","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 440\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Top view of a covey flush. You and a partner walk in a line. Birds fan out ahead. Safe shooting is the wedge straight ahead of you. No shots toward your partner, toward the building, low over the brush, or behind you.\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"440\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <path d=\"M200 330 L80 90 A 135 135 0 0 1 300 90 Z\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"118\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">SAFE ZONE</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"137\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Birds above head height,</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"154\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">open ground beyond</text>\n  <g fill=\"#6b4f35\">\n    <ellipse cx=\"190\" cy=\"250\" rx=\"70\" ry=\"22\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"255\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Brush with covey</text>\n  <g stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M190 232 L120 175\"/>\n    <path d=\"M190 232 L165 170\"/>\n    <path d=\"M190 232 L215 170\"/>\n    <path d=\"M190 232 L262 178\"/>\n    <path d=\"M190 232 L330 220\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g fill=\"#23261f\">\n    <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"175\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"170\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"215\" cy=\"170\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"262\" cy=\"178\" r=\"5\"/><circle cx=\"330\" cy=\"220\" r=\"5\"/>\n  </g>\n  <rect x=\"332\" y=\"150\" width=\"56\" height=\"44\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <polygon points=\"328,150 360,124 392,150\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"212\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Building</text>\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"246\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">NO SHOT</text>\n  <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"345\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"378\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">You</text>\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"345\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"378\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Partner</text>\n  <line x1=\"80\" y1=\"345\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"345\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"330\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">NO SHOT</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"408\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Behind you and low birds: NO SHOT</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"428\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stay in line. Know where your partner is.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Walk in a straight line, side by side, 15 to 25 m (16 to 27 yd) apart (Tip).</li><li>Each hunter takes birds on their own side of centre (Tip).</li><li>Shoot only birds above head height with open ground or sky beyond (Tip).</li><li><strong>Never swing through your partner.</strong> Stop the swing, gun up, safety on (Tip).</li><li>Blaze orange hat and vest on both of you in brush (Tip).</li><li>Pick one bird. Shoot it. Then pick another. Shooting at the whole covey misses (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Near orchards and buildings","html":"<ul><li><strong>No shooting within 100 m (109 yd) of an occupied house, farm or ranch building</strong> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>That includes barns with animals, packing sheds and worker housing <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Cultivated land, orchards included, needs the owner's or occupant's permission <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Orchards have ladders, irrigation, workers and pickers. Ask where people are today (Tip).</li><li>Shot falls back to earth. Never shoot toward a road, house or orchard crew, even far away (Tip).</li><li>Local no shooting areas and regional district bylaws are common in the Okanagan: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the Region 8 synopsis maps and the local bylaw.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Asking permission","html":"<ul><li>Go in person, daytime, clean clothes, no guns showing (Tip).</li><li>Short script:<ul><li>\"Hi, I'm (name). My brother and I hunt quail. Could we walk your fence lines and brush on a weekday morning?\"</li><li>\"We stay 100 m from every building. We close gates. We'll show you our licences.\"</li><li>\"Where should we not go? When are workers out?\"</li><li>\"Can I write down your OK and a phone number?\"</li></ul></li><li>Get it written: name, date, area, your names. Keep it on you (60%, legal advice from secondary sources).</li><li>Bring back a thank you: cleaned birds, a card, or help with a job (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Crown land options","html":"<ul><li>Crown land is public land owned by the province. Hunting is often allowed unless closed (Tip).</li><li>How to find it: an offline map app with a land ownership layer, or iMapBC. Check before you drive (Tip).</li><li>Grazing lease land needs the lease holder's OK while livestock are on it <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>First Nations reserve land is not Crown land. Hunting there needs the Nation's permission: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Parks, ecological reserves and no shooting areas: check each one in the synopsis. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Specific Okanagan Crown spots that hold quail: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> with the local rod and gun club or a BC Wildlife Federation member.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Chukar note","html":"<ul><li>Chukar live on dry, steep, rocky slopes and talus. In the Thompson, mostly 250 to 500 m elevation <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Best numbers are on low slopes of the Thompson valley from Kamloops to Lytton <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>They run uphill and flush downhill. Hard work for 2 hunters (Tip).</li><li>Region 8 and Region 3 chukar: a search preview said 1 October to 30 November, bag 5 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> both regions in the synopsis.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Quail at Heffley Creek?","html":"<ul><li>Region 3 California quail season: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. A search did not find one <span class=\"cert c-tip\" title=\"Certainty\">50%</span>. Do not hunt quail there until confirmed.</li><li>Heffley Creek is outside the main BC quail range <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>See quail at the property? Note it in your journal. Do not shoot.</li></ul><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s11-quail\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s11-quail:0\"><span>Quail season open today in my exact MU (full synopsis table)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s11-quail:1\"><span>Written permission in my pocket, or Crown land confirmed on the map</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s11-quail:2\"><span>100 m buffers from every building marked on the map</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s11-quail:3\"><span>Both hunters: blaze orange, eye and ear protection</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s11-quail:4\"><span>Sides agreed. No shots at low birds</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s11-quail:5\"><span>Shells for quail and a game vest or bag</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s11-quail:6\"><span>Cooler with ice for the drive home</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>A low bird is a free bird. Let it go.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Hunting an orchard edge without asking because \"it is not fenced.\"</li><li>Swinging on a bird that flies between you and your partner.</li><li>Shooting into the covey instead of picking one bird.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Before the trip: list 5 Okanagan farms with brushy edges on the map. Plan to knock on doors in person on day 1, and hunt day 2.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-8-okanagan.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Region 8 synopsis</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, quail season 70% (from <code>data/regs.json</code>), chukar 60%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting Regulation, 100 m rule</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC species licences</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 80%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/regulations-synopsis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, cultivated land and grazing lease 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/California_Quail/lifehistory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cornell All About Birds: California quail</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.birdatlas.bc.ca/accounts/speciesaccount.jsp?lang=en&amp;sp=CAQU\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Breeding Bird Atlas: California quail</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.wildlifebc.org/pdfs/WA%2014_2ChukarWeb.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wildlife Afield: chukar in the Thompson</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%.</li><li><a href=\"https://peaceforage.bc.ca/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/Trespass-to-Private-Land.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Peace Region Forage Association: trespass to private land</a>, checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 60%.</li><li>Note: official pages read through search previews only. Capped at 85%.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do you need to hunt quail as a BC resident?","options":["A quail species licence","The BC hunting licence only","The federal bird permit"],"answer":1,"why":"Residents need no species licence for quail. The BC hunting licence covers it."},{"q":"A quail flushes low between you and your partner. What do you do?","options":["Quick shot before it gets away","No shot. Gun up, safety on","Shoot once it passes your partner"],"answer":1,"why":"Never swing through your partner, and never shoot low birds. Let it go."},{"q":"The orchard is not fenced or posted. Can you hunt it?","options":["Yes, it is open","No. Cultivated land needs the owner's or occupant's permission","Only before 8 am"],"answer":1,"why":"BC requires consent to hunt cultivated land, posted or not."},{"q":"Can you hunt quail on your brother's land at Heffley Creek this October?","options":["Yes, same as the Okanagan","Not until a Region 3 quail season is confirmed in the synopsis","Yes, private land has no seasons"],"answer":1,"why":"Seasons are set by region and MU. No confirmed Region 3 quail season means no hunt."}],"checklists":["s11-quail"],"text":" !why quail are fast, fun and live close to farms and homes. most of the skill is getting permission and keeping every shot safe. - trip: the okanagan, region 8. two hunters with shotguns. - assumption: no landowner permission yet. no dog. - residents need only the bc hunting licence for quail. no species licence (80%). season and limits - region 8 california quail: 1 october to 30 november (70%). - daily bag 10. possession 30 (70%). - open in mus (management units) 8-1 to 8-15 and 8-21 to 8-26 (70%). - read from a search preview of the region 8 synopsis. confirm in the full table before you go: verify. - legal hours: 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset (80%). - federal lead shot ban covers ducks and geese, not quail (75%). any bc rule on lead for quail: verify. steel works fine (tip). - each hunter has their own bag. no filling your partner's limit (tip, verify the exact wording). california quail: habitat and habits - in bc, mostly in the okanagan and kettle valleys and on southeast vancouver island (75%). - they live in farm and suburban edges with lots of cover, and in brushy creeks, grassland and open forest (75%). - fall and winter flocks are called coveys. often family groups, sometimes very large (75%). - they feed mornings and evenings. they rest in shrubs midday and roost in trees (75%). - a lookout bird, often a male, watches while the rest feed (75%). - they would rather run than fly. when pushed, they burst up with loud whirring wings (75%). where to look - brushy draws, blackberry and rose thickets, creek edges near grain or orchards (tip). - listen at first light. their call carries far (tip). - look for dusting spots and tracks on dirt roads near cover (tip). - after the first flush, singles hold tighter. mark where they landed and follow up (tip). !tip best time, my opinion: the first 2 hours after legal light and the last 2 hours before sunset, on calm days. late october and november, after leaves drop, you can see them better. the covey flush - walk in a straight line, side by side, 15 to 25 m (16 to 27 yd) apart (tip). - each hunter takes birds on their own side of centre (tip). - shoot only birds above head height with open ground or sky beyond (tip). - never swing through your partner. stop the swing, gun up, safety on (tip). - blaze orange hat and vest on both of you in brush (tip). - pick one bird. shoot it. then pick another. shooting at the whole covey misses (tip). near orchards and buildings - no shooting within 100 m (109 yd) of an occupied house, farm or ranch building (75%). - that includes barns with animals, packing sheds and worker housing (75%). - cultivated land, orchards included, needs the owner's or occupant's permission (75%). - orchards have ladders, irrigation, workers and pickers. ask where people are today (tip). - shot falls back to earth. never shoot toward a road, house or orchard crew, even far away (tip). - local no shooting areas and regional district bylaws are common in the okanagan: verify on the region 8 synopsis maps and the local bylaw. asking permission - go in person, daytime, clean clothes, no guns showing (tip). - short script: - \"hi, i'm (name). my brother and i hunt quail. could we walk your fence lines and brush on a weekday morning?\" - \"we stay 100 m from every building. we close gates. we'll show you our licences.\" - \"where should we not go? when are workers out?\" - \"can i write down your ok and a phone number?\" - get it written: name, date, area, your names. keep it on you (60%, legal advice from secondary sources). - bring back a thank you: cleaned birds, a card, or help with a job (tip). crown land options - crown land is public land owned by the province. hunting is often allowed unless closed (tip). - how to find it: an offline map app with a land ownership layer, or imapbc. check before you drive (tip). - grazing lease land needs the lease holder's ok while livestock are on it (75%). - first nations reserve land is not crown land. hunting there needs the nation's permission: verify. - parks, ecological reserves and no shooting areas: check each one in the synopsis. verify. - specific okanagan crown spots that hold quail: verify with the local rod and gun club or a bc wildlife federation member. chukar note - chukar live on dry, steep, rocky slopes and talus. in the thompson, mostly 250 to 500 m elevation (75%). - best numbers are on low slopes of the thompson valley from kamloops to lytton (75%). - they run uphill and flush downhill. hard work for 2 hunters (tip). - region 8 and region 3 chukar: a search preview said 1 october to 30 november, bag 5 (60%). verify both regions in the synopsis. quail at heffley creek? - region 3 california quail season: verify. a search did not find one (50%). do not hunt quail there until confirmed. - heffley creek is outside the main bc quail range (60%). - see quail at the property? note it in your journal. do not shoot. !rule a low bird is a free bird. let it go. !mistake - hunting an orchard edge without asking because \"it is not fenced.\" - swinging on a bird that flies between you and your partner. - shooting into the covey instead of picking one bird. !field before the trip: list 5 okanagan farms with brushy edges on the map. plan to knock on doors in person on day 1, and hunt day 2. sources - region 8 synopsis (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-8-okanagan.pdf), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, quail season 70% (from data/regs.json ), chukar 60%. - bc hunting regulation, 100 m rule (https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%. - bc species licences (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 80%. - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/regulations-synopsis), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, cultivated land and grazing lease 75%. - cornell all about birds: california quail (https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/california_quail/lifehistory), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%. - bc breeding bird atlas: california quail (https://www.birdatlas.bc.ca/accounts/speciesaccount.jsp?lang=en&sp=caqu), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%. - wildlife afield: chukar in the thompson (https://www.wildlifebc.org/pdfs/wa%2014_2chukarweb.pdf), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 75%. - peace region forage association: trespass to private land (https://peaceforage.bc.ca/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/trespass-to-private-land.pdf), checked 2026-10-01, search preview, 60%. - note: official pages read through search previews only. capped at 85%. "},{"id":"legal-to-shoot","phase":1,"num":12,"title":"Is it legal to shoot?","minutes":10,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Is it legal to shoot?","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>You get about 3 seconds. The 9 questions must be a habit before the animal shows up.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><ul><li>Same 9 steps as the \"Is it legal to shoot?\" card in the Field tab.</li><li>Every answer must be <strong>yes</strong>. One no, or one \"not sure\", and you do not shoot.</li><li>Practise saying them in order on every walk until they are automatic.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The flowchart","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 790\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"790\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Is it legal to shoot?</text>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#23261f\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"ahr\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#c92a2a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <!-- red stop column -->\n  <rect x=\"312\" y=\"48\" width=\"80\" height=\"618\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#c92a2a\"/>\n  <text transform=\"translate(360 357) rotate(-90)\" font-size=\"26\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">DO NOT SHOOT</text>\n  <!-- question boxes: y = 48 + i*70, height 44 -->\n  <g font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\">\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"48\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">1. Licences on me?</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"118\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"145\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">2. Legal hours right now?</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"188\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">3. Season open, MU, sex?</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"258\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">4. 100% sure what it is?</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"328\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"355\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">5. Off road, out of vehicle?</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"398\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"425\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">6. Over 100 m from buildings?</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"468\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"495\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">7. Allowed on this land?</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"538\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"565\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">8. Safe backstop?</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"608\" width=\"252\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e9eedd\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"134\" y=\"635\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#23261f\">9. Clean shot, in my range?</text>\n  </g>\n  <!-- yes arrows down -->\n  <g stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#ah)\">\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"116\"/>\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"186\"/>\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"232\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"256\"/>\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"302\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"326\"/>\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"372\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"396\"/>\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"442\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"466\"/>\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"512\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"536\"/>\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"582\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"606\"/>\n    <line x1=\"134\" y1=\"652\" x2=\"134\" y2=\"690\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"320\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"390\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"460\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"530\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"600\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n    <text x=\"142\" y=\"676\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Yes</text>\n  </g>\n  <!-- no arrows right -->\n  <g stroke=\"#c92a2a\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#ahr)\">\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"70\"/>\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"140\"/>\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"210\"/>\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"280\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"280\"/>\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"350\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"350\"/>\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"420\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"420\"/>\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"490\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"490\"/>\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"560\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"560\"/>\n    <line x1=\"260\" y1=\"630\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"630\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"63\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"133\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"203\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"273\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"343\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"413\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"483\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"553\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n    <text x=\"270\" y=\"623\" fill=\"#c92a2a\">No</text>\n  </g>\n  <!-- final -->\n  <rect x=\"8\" y=\"692\" width=\"384\" height=\"86\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"726\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">All 9 yes: you may shoot</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"756\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Any doubt at any step means no.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Yes goes down. No goes right, to DO NOT SHOOT.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Steps 1 to 3: paper, clock, season","html":"<ol><li><strong>Licences on me?</strong> Photo ID, <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"FWID\">FWID</button> (Fish and Wildlife ID) number, the deer species licence, or the federal bird permit for ducks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Legal hours?</strong> Deer and quail: 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Ducks: 30 minutes before sunrise to 30 minutes after sunset <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Season open for this species, sex and antler type, in this <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MU\">MU</button> (Management Unit), today?</strong> Check the synopsis table for your exact MU. Dates: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the Rules tab.</li></ol><ul><li>Antler rules like \"4 point\" mean you count points <strong>before</strong> the shot. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Steps 4 to 6: target, road, buildings","html":"<ol><li><strong>100% sure what it is?</strong> Species, sex, antler points. For ducks: species before you shoot. Not sure means no. Tip.</li><li><strong>Off the road, out of the vehicle?</strong> No shooting from a motor vehicle or motorboat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. No shooting across a road or on the road allowance <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. No loaded firearm in or on a vehicle or <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"ATV\">ATV</button> (all terrain vehicle) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Over 100 m (109 yd) from any occupied house, farm or ranch building?</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. This includes your brother's house on the property (80%, my reading).</li></ol>"},{"title":"Steps 7 to 9: land, backstop, shot","html":"<ol><li><strong>Allowed on this land?</strong> <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Crown land\">Crown land</button> that is open, or owner's permission. Not a park closed to hunting, an ecological reserve or a No Shooting Area <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Safe <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Backstop\">Backstop</button>?</strong> Earth behind the animal. No skyline. No people, road, livestock or building beyond. Tip, and your safety course rule.</li><li><strong>Clean shot inside your tested range?</strong> Broadside or quartering away, still animal, steady rest. Tip.</li></ol><ul><li>Steps 8 and 9 are about safety and ethics. They are not optional.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>When in doubt, don't. The deer will be there next year. A bad shot follows you forever.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Counting antler points after the shot.</li><li>Forgetting that ducks start 30 minutes later than deer in the morning.</li><li>Shooting from the ATV seat \"because the engine is off\".</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Walking in, say the 9 steps out loud once. At the stand, rangefind the nearest building and the road.</p></aside><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s12-legal\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s12-legal:0\"><span>I can say all 9 steps from memory</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s12-legal:1\"><span>I know today's legal start and end for deer and for ducks</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s12-legal:2\"><span>I know my MU and today's open seasons</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s12-legal:3\"><span>I rangefinded the house and the road from my stand</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hours, vehicles, roads, 100 m, parks.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting Regulation, B.C. Reg. 190/84</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hours.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ECCC (Environment and Climate Change Canada) BC migratory bird summary</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: duck hours.</li><li>Steps 4, 8 and 9 are safety and ethics habits (Tip), not quoted law.</li><li>Full rule detail and certainty: Phase 2 Session 5, BC rules you must know.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Sunrise is in 40 minutes. A buck and a flock of ducks show up. What is legal right now?","options":["Both","The buck only. Duck hours start 30 minutes before sunrise, which is 10 minutes away","Neither"],"answer":1,"why":"Deer hours start 1 hour before sunrise. Duck hours start 30 minutes before sunrise. At 40 minutes before, only the deer is legal (if its season is open)."},{"q":"You think it is a 4 point buck but can only see one side. What now?","options":["Shoot, it is probably legal","Wait. Not sure means no","Shoot and check after"],"answer":1,"why":"Step 4: you must be 100% sure of species, sex and antler points before the shot."},{"q":"A deer stands 80 m from your brother's house. He says go ahead. Can you shoot?","options":["Yes, he gave permission","No. Inside 100 m of an occupied dwelling","Yes if the backstop is safe"],"answer":1,"why":"The 100 m rule protects occupied houses. Owner permission does not lift it."},{"q":"What happens if any one answer is no?","options":["Shoot carefully","Do not shoot","Ask your brother"],"answer":1,"why":"Every step must be yes. One no ends it."}],"checklists":["s12-legal"],"text":" !why you get about 3 seconds. the 9 questions must be a habit before the animal shows up. !warn study aid only. the official regulations are the law. - same 9 steps as the \"is it legal to shoot?\" card in the field tab. - every answer must be yes . one no, or one \"not sure\", and you do not shoot. - practise saying them in order on every walk until they are automatic. the flowchart - yes goes down. no goes right, to do not shoot. steps 1 to 3: paper, clock, season 1. licences on me? photo id, fwid (fish and wildlife id) number, the deer species licence, or the federal bird permit for ducks (85%). 2. legal hours? deer and quail: 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset (85%). ducks: 30 minutes before sunrise to 30 minutes after sunset (85%). 3. season open for this species, sex and antler type, in this mu (management unit), today? check the synopsis table for your exact mu. dates: verify in the rules tab. - antler rules like \"4 point\" mean you count points before the shot. tip. steps 4 to 6: target, road, buildings 4. 100% sure what it is? species, sex, antler points. for ducks: species before you shoot. not sure means no. tip. 5. off the road, out of the vehicle? no shooting from a motor vehicle or motorboat (85%). no shooting across a road or on the road allowance (80%). no loaded firearm in or on a vehicle or atv (all terrain vehicle) (85%). 6. over 100 m (109 yd) from any occupied house, farm or ranch building? (85%). this includes your brother's house on the property (80%, my reading). steps 7 to 9: land, backstop, shot 7. allowed on this land? crown land that is open, or owner's permission. not a park closed to hunting, an ecological reserve or a no shooting area (85%). 8. safe backstop ? earth behind the animal. no skyline. no people, road, livestock or building beyond. tip, and your safety course rule. 9. clean shot inside your tested range? broadside or quartering away, still animal, steady rest. tip. - steps 8 and 9 are about safety and ethics. they are not optional. !rule when in doubt, don't. the deer will be there next year. a bad shot follows you forever. !mistake - counting antler points after the shot. - forgetting that ducks start 30 minutes later than deer in the morning. - shooting from the atv seat \"because the engine is off\". !field walking in, say the 9 steps out loud once. at the stand, rangefind the nearest building and the road. sources - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028 (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hours, vehicles, roads, 100 m, parks. - hunting regulation, b.c. reg. 190/84 (https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hours. - eccc (environment and climate change canada) bc migratory bird summary (https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: duck hours. - steps 4, 8 and 9 are safety and ethics habits (tip), not quoted law. - full rule detail and certainty: phase 2 session 5, bc rules you must know. "},{"id":"after-the-shot","phase":1,"num":13,"title":"After the shot","minutes":15,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"After the shot","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>The shot is 1 second. The next 3 hours decide if you find the deer and if the meat is good.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><ul><li>Fast order: <strong>wait, mark, track, approach, notch, evidence, dress, cool, transport, report.</strong></li><li>Tagging and evidence rules are law. Tracking and dressing steps are skills (Tip).</li><li>Your brother hunts too. Each of you notches only your own licence. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"1. Wait and mark","html":"<ul><li>Stay still. Watch where it runs. Listen for a crash. Tip.</li><li>Rifle reloaded and on safe. Watch for 5 minutes in case it stands. Tip.</li><li>Drop a phone map pin where you shot from and where the deer stood. Tip.</li><li>Note a landmark at the last place you saw it (a tree, a rock). Tip.</li><li>Wait about 30 minutes before you follow. Longer if you think the hit was far back. Tip, common hunter practice, not law.</li></ul>"},{"title":"2. Track","html":"<ul><li>Go to where it stood. Look for blood, hair, scuffed ground. Tip.</li><li>Bright pink, bubbly blood often means a lung hit. Dark blood or gut matter: back out and wait hours. Tip.</li><li>Mark each blood spot with tape or a phone pin. Tip.</li><li>Lost the trail? Go back to the last blood. Search in slow circles. Get your brother to help. Tip.</li><li>Trail goes into posted private land, a park or a No Shooting Area: stop. Ask the owner or call a conservation officer <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. See Phase 2 Session 5.</li><li>You must make a real effort to recover wounded game <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> the exact rule.</li></ul>"},{"title":"3. Approach and notch","html":"<ul><li>Come from behind the back, rifle ready. Touch the eye with a stick. No blink means dead. Tip.</li><li><strong>Notch (cancel) the species licence immediately after the kill and before you handle the animal</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Follow the notch instructions printed on the licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Exact fields: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on your licence.</li><li>Keep the notched licence with you. Carry all licences for the year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Take photos: the deer, the notched licence, the spot. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"4. Evidence of sex","html":"<ul><li>Evidence of sex stays <strong>naturally attached</strong> to the carcass until home, the meat cutter, cold storage or inspection (75%, secondary).</li><li>Buck: the part of the head with antlers, <strong>or</strong> testicles or penis plus the tail or a patch of hide (75%, secondary).</li><li>Doe: the head part where antlers would be, <strong>or</strong> udder or teats plus the tail or a patch of hide (75%, secondary).</li><li>Antler restricted season (like 4 point): antlers stay on part of the skull <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Do <strong>not</strong> cut these off during field dressing. Plan your cuts around them. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"5. Field dress (gutted method)","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 492\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"492\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Field dressing: gutted method</text>\n  <g transform=\"translate(0,24)\">\n  <!-- deer lying on its back is hard to read; simple standing side outline, head left -->\n  <g stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">\n    <!-- legs -->\n    <rect x=\"120\" y=\"150\" width=\"14\" height=\"70\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n    <rect x=\"142\" y=\"150\" width=\"14\" height=\"66\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n    <rect x=\"262\" y=\"150\" width=\"14\" height=\"70\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n    <rect x=\"284\" y=\"150\" width=\"14\" height=\"66\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n    <!-- body -->\n    <ellipse cx=\"210\" cy=\"125\" rx=\"110\" ry=\"42\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n    <!-- neck -->\n    <path d=\"M118 112 L82 62 L104 52 L142 100 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n    <!-- head -->\n    <ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"56\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n    <!-- ear -->\n    <path d=\"M96 44 L112 24 L106 48 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n    <!-- antler -->\n    <path d=\"M90 42 L94 14 M94 26 L84 16 M94 20 L104 8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <!-- tail -->\n    <path d=\"M318 108 L334 100 L330 116 Z\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  </g>\n  <!-- belly cut line -->\n  <line x1=\"150\" y1=\"164\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 6\"/>\n  <!-- diaphragm line -->\n  <line x1=\"178\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"178\" y2=\"164\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 6\"/>\n  <!-- numbered markers -->\n  <g font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"84\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#fffaf0\">1</text>\n    <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"140\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"350\" y=\"146\" fill=\"#fffaf0\">2</text>\n    <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"186\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"192\" fill=\"#fffaf0\">3</text>\n    <circle cx=\"225\" cy=\"190\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"225\" y=\"196\" fill=\"#fffaf0\">4</text>\n    <circle cx=\"178\" cy=\"66\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"178\" y=\"72\" fill=\"#fffaf0\">5</text>\n    <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"80\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#fffaf0\">6</text>\n    <circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"125\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"210\" y=\"131\" fill=\"#fffaf0\">7</text>\n  </g>\n  <!-- legend -->\n  <g font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\">\n    <text x=\"16\" y=\"256\" fill=\"#23261f\">1  Confirm dead. Notch licence first.</text>\n    <text x=\"16\" y=\"284\" fill=\"#23261f\">2  Gloves on. Cut around the anus, tie off.</text>\n    <text x=\"16\" y=\"312\" fill=\"#23261f\">3  Open hide at the pelvis, blade up.</text>\n    <text x=\"16\" y=\"340\" fill=\"#23261f\">4  Cut belly to ribs. Fingers guard blade.</text>\n    <text x=\"16\" y=\"368\" fill=\"#23261f\">5  Cut the diaphragm along the ribs.</text>\n    <text x=\"16\" y=\"396\" fill=\"#23261f\">6  Reach up, cut windpipe and gullet.</text>\n    <text x=\"16\" y=\"424\" fill=\"#23261f\">7  Roll guts out. Prop cavity open to cool.</text>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"16\" y=\"456\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\">Orange dashes: cut lines. Do not cut the gut.</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Gloves on. Do not cut the stomach or guts (Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife food safety page, 75%).</li><li>If gut contents touch meat, wipe them off right away with a clean cloth <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Do it fast. Remove the guts as quickly as you can <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Leave the evidence of sex attached. Check before each cut. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"6. Gutless method (overview)","html":"<ul><li>Skin one side down the back. Take the hind quarter, front quarter, back strap. Tip.</li><li>Reach in behind the last rib for the tenderloin. Tip.</li><li>Flip and repeat. Then neck meat and rib meat. Tip.</li><li>You must still take neck, ribs, all four quarters and loins <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. These are the legal edible portions.</li><li>Keep evidence of sex with the meat, naturally attached to one piece. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> how this works with boned meat.</li><li>Good for a deer far from the truck. Leave guts and bones at the site <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"7. Cool the meat","html":"<ul><li>Cool it below 4°C (40°F) as fast as you can (75%, Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife).</li><li>Best: in a cooler or cold room the same day <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Prop the chest open. Air must move. Shade, off the ground. Tip.</li><li><strong>No plastic or tarp</strong> on a warm carcass. It holds the heat <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Light game bags keep flies off <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Warm October afternoon in the valley: get it to a cooler fast. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"8. Transport legally","html":"<ul><li>Carry your notched species licence with the meat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Anyone moving your deer <strong>without you</strong> needs a <strong>Record of Receipt</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Record of Receipt: date and place, your name and address, who gets it, your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"FWID\">FWID</button> (Fish and Wildlife ID), species licence number, species and sex <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Evidence of sex stays attached in transport <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Rifle unloaded in the truck or on the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"ATV\">ATV</button> (all terrain vehicle): chamber and magazine empty <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Region 3 is not in the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) Management Zone, so its parts rules do not apply here <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"9. Report","html":"<ul><li>Compulsory reporting for deer in your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MU\">MU</button> (Management Unit): <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the synopsis.</li><li>Where it applies: report within 30 days with location, date, days hunted, weapon and more <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>2026 mandatory hunter reports cover elk, moose and caribou licences, not deer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Write it in your journal tonight. Tip.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Notch first, knife second. And never let a warm deer sit in a closed truck.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Following too soon and pushing a hit deer out of the country.</li><li>Cutting off the evidence of sex while gutting.</li><li>Wrapping a warm carcass in a tarp for the drive home.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Pack: knife, spare blade, gloves, game bags, 10 m of rope, flagging tape, pen, Record of Receipt notes.</p></aside><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s13-after\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s13-after:0\"><span>Waited and marked the spot</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s13-after:1\"><span>Licence notched before touching</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s13-after:2\"><span>Evidence of sex still attached</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s13-after:3\"><span>Guts out, no gut contents on meat</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s13-after:4\"><span>Cavity open and cooling, no plastic</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s13-after:5\"><span>Record of Receipt filled if someone else moves it</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s13-after:6\"><span>Compulsory reporting checked (<span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>)</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC species licences page</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: notch immediately, before handling.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: edible portions, Record of Receipt, CWD zone.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/compulsory-reporting\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC compulsory reporting page</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: 30 day reporting.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/2026_hunting-trapping_regulation_changes_summary.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2026 regulation changes summary</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: mandatory hunter report species.</li><li><a href=\"https://wdfw.wa.gov/hunting/requirements/food-safety\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife, food safety for game meat</a> (search preview, not BC law, 75% max), checked 2026-10-01: cooling, gloves, no plastic.</li><li>Secondary, 60 to 79% max: <a href=\"https://bcfirearmsacademy.ca/possession-transportation-big-game/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Firearms Academy, evidence of sex and transport</a>; <a href=\"https://www.huntingbc.ca/forum/archive/index.php/t-92535.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HuntingBC forum, wounded game on private land</a>. Checked 2026-10-01.</li><li>Tracking and dressing steps: common hunter practice (Tip).</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"The deer is down. What is the first legal step?","options":["Field dress it fast","Notch the species licence before you handle it","Drag it to the truck"],"answer":1,"why":"The licence must be cancelled immediately after the kill and before handling the animal."},{"q":"Why not wrap the carcass in a tarp for the drive?","options":["It is illegal","It holds heat and the meat can spoil","It hides the evidence of sex"],"answer":1,"why":"Plastic and tarps trap heat. Meat needs to drop below 4°C fast."},{"q":"Your friend drives your deer home while you stay at camp. What does he need?","options":["Nothing","A Record of Receipt from you","His own deer licence"],"answer":1,"why":"Anyone carrying wildlife killed by another person needs a Record of Receipt."},{"q":"Using the gutless method, what must you take out?","options":["Back straps and hind quarters","Neck, ribs, all four quarters and the loins","Whatever fits in the pack"],"answer":1,"why":"Those are the legal edible portions of big game. Leaving them is wastage."}],"checklists":["s13-after"],"text":" !why the shot is 1 second. the next 3 hours decide if you find the deer and if the meat is good. !warn study aid only. the official regulations are the law. - fast order: wait, mark, track, approach, notch, evidence, dress, cool, transport, report. - tagging and evidence rules are law. tracking and dressing steps are skills (tip). - your brother hunts too. each of you notches only your own licence. tip. 1. wait and mark - stay still. watch where it runs. listen for a crash. tip. - rifle reloaded and on safe. watch for 5 minutes in case it stands. tip. - drop a phone map pin where you shot from and where the deer stood. tip. - note a landmark at the last place you saw it (a tree, a rock). tip. - wait about 30 minutes before you follow. longer if you think the hit was far back. tip, common hunter practice, not law. 2. track - go to where it stood. look for blood, hair, scuffed ground. tip. - bright pink, bubbly blood often means a lung hit. dark blood or gut matter: back out and wait hours. tip. - mark each blood spot with tape or a phone pin. tip. - lost the trail? go back to the last blood. search in slow circles. get your brother to help. tip. - trail goes into posted private land, a park or a no shooting area: stop. ask the owner or call a conservation officer (70%). see phase 2 session 5. - you must make a real effort to recover wounded game (70%). verify the exact rule. 3. approach and notch - come from behind the back, rifle ready. touch the eye with a stick. no blink means dead. tip. - notch (cancel) the species licence immediately after the kill and before you handle the animal (85%). - follow the notch instructions printed on the licence (85%). exact fields: verify on your licence. - keep the notched licence with you. carry all licences for the year (85%). - take photos: the deer, the notched licence, the spot. tip. 4. evidence of sex - evidence of sex stays naturally attached to the carcass until home, the meat cutter, cold storage or inspection (75%, secondary). - buck: the part of the head with antlers, or testicles or penis plus the tail or a patch of hide (75%, secondary). - doe: the head part where antlers would be, or udder or teats plus the tail or a patch of hide (75%, secondary). - antler restricted season (like 4 point): antlers stay on part of the skull (75%). - do not cut these off during field dressing. plan your cuts around them. tip. 5. field dress (gutted method) - gloves on. do not cut the stomach or guts (washington department of fish and wildlife food safety page, 75%). - if gut contents touch meat, wipe them off right away with a clean cloth (75%). - do it fast. remove the guts as quickly as you can (75%). - leave the evidence of sex attached. check before each cut. tip. 6. gutless method (overview) - skin one side down the back. take the hind quarter, front quarter, back strap. tip. - reach in behind the last rib for the tenderloin. tip. - flip and repeat. then neck meat and rib meat. tip. - you must still take neck, ribs, all four quarters and loins (85%). these are the legal edible portions. - keep evidence of sex with the meat, naturally attached to one piece. verify how this works with boned meat. - good for a deer far from the truck. leave guts and bones at the site (85%). 7. cool the meat - cool it below 4°c (40°f) as fast as you can (75%, washington department of fish and wildlife). - best: in a cooler or cold room the same day (75%). - prop the chest open. air must move. shade, off the ground. tip. - no plastic or tarp on a warm carcass. it holds the heat (75%). - light game bags keep flies off (75%). - warm october afternoon in the valley: get it to a cooler fast. tip. 8. transport legally - carry your notched species licence with the meat (85%). - anyone moving your deer without you needs a record of receipt (85%). - record of receipt: date and place, your name and address, who gets it, your fwid (fish and wildlife id), species licence number, species and sex (85%). - evidence of sex stays attached in transport (75%). - rifle unloaded in the truck or on the atv (all terrain vehicle): chamber and magazine empty (85%). - region 3 is not in the cwd (chronic wasting disease) management zone, so its parts rules do not apply here (85%). 9. report - compulsory reporting for deer in your mu (management unit): verify in the synopsis. - where it applies: report within 30 days with location, date, days hunted, weapon and more (85%). - 2026 mandatory hunter reports cover elk, moose and caribou licences, not deer (80%). - write it in your journal tonight. tip. !rule notch first, knife second. and never let a warm deer sit in a closed truck. !mistake - following too soon and pushing a hit deer out of the country. - cutting off the evidence of sex while gutting. - wrapping a warm carcass in a tarp for the drive home. !field pack: knife, spare blade, gloves, game bags, 10 m of rope, flagging tape, pen, record of receipt notes. sources - bc species licences page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: notch immediately, before handling. - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028 (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: edible portions, record of receipt, cwd zone. - bc compulsory reporting page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/compulsory-reporting) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: 30 day reporting. - 2026 regulation changes summary (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/2026_hunting-trapping_regulation_changes_summary.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: mandatory hunter report species. - washington department of fish and wildlife, food safety for game meat (https://wdfw.wa.gov/hunting/requirements/food-safety) (search preview, not bc law, 75% max), checked 2026-10-01: cooling, gloves, no plastic. - secondary, 60 to 79% max: bc firearms academy, evidence of sex and transport (https://bcfirearmsacademy.ca/possession-transportation-big-game/); huntingbc forum, wounded game on private land (https://www.huntingbc.ca/forum/archive/index.php/t-92535.html). checked 2026-10-01. - tracking and dressing steps: common hunter practice (tip). "},{"id":"firearm-safety","phase":2,"num":1,"title":"Firearm safety refresher","minutes":12,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Firearm safety refresher","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>You both passed the course. Now there are two loaded guns, two new hunters, cold hands and excitement. That is when habits matter.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside>"},{"title":"ACTS: the Vital Four","html":"<p>The CFSC (Canadian Firearms Safety Course) teaches these as the \"Vital Four ACTS\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</p><ul><li><strong>A</strong>ssume every firearm is loaded <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>C</strong>ontrol the muzzle direction at all times <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>T</strong>rigger finger off the trigger and out of the trigger guard <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>S</strong>ee that the firearm is unloaded: PROVE it safe <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Wording read in search previews of the course handbook and course provider pages, not the full handbook (see Sources).</li></ul>"},{"title":"PROVE it safe","html":"<ul><li><strong>P</strong>oint the firearm in the safest available direction <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>R</strong>emove all cartridges <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>O</strong>bserve the chamber <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>V</strong>erify the feeding path <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>E</strong>xamine the bore for obstructions <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Do ACTS and PROVE every time you pick up, put down, hand over or receive a firearm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Also when you clean it, and before you store or transport it <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Say each letter out loud when you PROVE it. Have your brother do the same. You each catch the other skipping a step.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Zones of fire","html":"<ul><li>A zone of fire is the only slice of ground where you may shoot <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Hunter education teaches about 45 degrees straight ahead for each hunter <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Never swing the muzzle outside it, even tracking a running deer <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Every shot also needs a safe <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Backstop\">Backstop</button>. Sky is never one.</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 560\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"560\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Walking side by side</text>\n  <path d=\"M120,230 L66,100 A140,140 0 0 1 174,100 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" fill-opacity=\"0.55\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M280,230 L226,100 A140,140 0 0 1 334,100 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" fill-opacity=\"0.55\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A shoots</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">here only</text>\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">B shoots</text>\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">here only</text>\n  <path d=\"M174,100 L200,60 L226,100 L200,215 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\" fill-opacity=\"0.25\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">NO SHOT</text>\n  <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"238\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"238\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"273\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text>\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"273\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">B</text>\n  <path d=\"M140,262 L260,262\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"256\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">in sight, in line</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Each arc about 45 degrees, straight ahead</text>\n  <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"314\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"314\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"344\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Small boat, back to back</text>\n  <path d=\"M300,450 L370,400 A86,86 0 0 1 370,500 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" fill-opacity=\"0.55\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M100,450 L30,400 A86,86 0 0 0 30,500 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" fill-opacity=\"0.55\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M95,425 L280,425 Q330,425 345,450 Q330,475 280,475 L95,475 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" fill-opacity=\"0.35\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"270\" cy=\"450\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"450\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"415\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">bow shooter</text>\n  <text x=\"130\" y=\"415\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">stern shooter</text>\n  <text x=\"372\" y=\"390\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">bow arc</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"390\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">stern arc</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"456\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">NO SHOT</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"512\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sit to shoot. Never swing past</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"530\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">your partner. Motor off, anchored.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure>"},{"title":"Two hunters walking side by side","html":"<ul><li>Walk in a line, side by side, always in sight of each other <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Each covers the arc in front. The middle and behind are no shot zones <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>One gets ahead or drops back? Both stop. Fix the line first.</li><li>Lose sight of each other? Muzzles up or down, no shooting, call out.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Bright orange on hat and vest helps you see each other in timber. My lean: wear it. Whether BC requires it: not covered here.</p></aside>"},{"title":"In a blind and in a boat or canoe","html":"<h3>In a blind</h3><ul><li>You and your brother agree who takes the left side and who takes the right side before first light.</li><li>Shoot only birds or deer on your side. Muzzles out the front, never across your partner.</li><li>Guns rest muzzle up or out the front when not shooting. Never leaned on the wall behind you.</li></ul><h3>In a boat or canoe</h3><ul><li>Two hunters sit back to back: bow shooter faces forward, stern shooter faces back (70%, hunter education guidance).</li><li>Shoot sitting. Never stand. See the water safety session.</li><li>Only one person moves at a time. Unload before anyone changes seats.</li><li>Loading in a boat is a legal question too. See the water safety session.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Fences, logs and ditches","html":"<ul><li>Alone: unload, PROVE it, lay the firearm under or through the fence, muzzle pointed away from you <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Cross well away from the muzzle, then pick it up by the stock <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>With a partner: one unloads and holds both firearms, actions open. The other crosses, then takes both. Then the first crosses (Tip, common hunter education method).</li><li>Never climb a fence, log or bank with a loaded firearm in your hands.</li></ul>"},{"title":"At the car","html":"<ul><li>Load only where you may lawfully shoot. Federal rules allow loading only in such a place <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>In BC, you must not transport, carry or store a loaded firearm in or on a vehicle or boat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>\"Unloaded\" means no cartridge in the chamber, and none in a magazine attached to or inserted in the firearm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>So: magazine out, chamber empty, before the firearm goes in the car or on the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"ATV\">ATV</button> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Leaving the car? Non restricted firearms go locked in the trunk or a lockable compartment. No trunk: out of sight, car locked <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Unload facing away from the car, partner and road. Load the same way, at the start of the walk.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>BC's Firearm Violence Prevention Act (FVPA) came into force 1 October 2026, with hunting exemptions made by regulation <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Exact exemption wording: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> before you hunt.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Two armed hunters: you and your brother","html":"<ul><li>Both of you hold <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CORE\">CORE</button> and a <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"PAL\">PAL</button>. Each carries your own licences and tags (owner confirmed).</li><li>Before every walk, agree three things: who covers left, who covers right, and the stop signal.</li><li>Only one of you shoots at a deer unless you agreed a backup plan first (Tip).</li><li>Neither one steps in front of the other's muzzle. Ever.</li><li>After a shot, both unload, then walk up together. The shooter leads.</li><li>First hunt for him? Swap roles at midday so each practises spotter and shooter (Tip).</li></ul><h3>General case: a non hunting companion</h3><ul><li>A person who only watches carries no firearm (80%, see Licences session).</li><li>They walk behind or beside you, never ahead, and freeze on your signal.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): The safety is a mechanical device. Your muzzle and your finger are the real safety.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Pointing the muzzle at your partner while you unload at the tailgate.</li><li>Leaving a round in the magazine \"because it is not in the chamber\". Attached magazine counts as loaded.</li><li>Following a running deer with the muzzle past your shooting arc.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>At the car, each of you runs PROVE out loud while the other watches. Then walk 50 m (55 yd) side by side, unloaded, and point out each other's arc with your arms.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2015/grc-rcmp/PS99-2-2-1-2014-eng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Firearms Safety Course handbook, RCMP (Royal Canadian Mounted Police), Government of Canada publications</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.firearmstraining.ca/actsprove.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ACTS and PROVE, Canadian Firearms Training</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://rcmp.ca/en/firearms/firearms-safety-training-transport-and-storage/storing-transporting-and-displaying-firearms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RCMP: Storing, transporting and displaying firearms</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/sor-98-209/page-1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Storage, Display, Transportation and Handling of Firearms by Individuals Regulations, SOR/98-209</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/justice/criminal-justice/policing-in-bc/fvpa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Firearm Violence Prevention Act page</a>, section 4, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://news.gov.bc.ca/releases/2026PSSG0037-000460\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC news release on FVPA in force 1 October 2026</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.hunter-ed.com/national/studyGuide/Determining-Your-Safe-Zone-of-Fire/201099_93020/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunter Ed: Determining your safe zone of fire</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, United States course material)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.hunter-ed.com/national/studyGuide/Crossing-Obstacles-Alone/201099_93010/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunter Ed: Crossing obstacles alone</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li>Note: all pages read through search previews. Capped at 85% until the full pages are opened.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the S in ACTS stand for?","options":["Safety on","See that the firearm is unloaded: PROVE it safe","Sight your target"],"answer":1,"why":"The Vital Four ACTS end with: See that the firearm is unloaded, PROVE it safe."},{"q":"Your magazine is in the rifle but the chamber is empty. Is it unloaded for transport?","options":["Yes","No. A cartridge in an attached or inserted magazine means loaded","Only if the safety is on"],"answer":1,"why":"The federal definition of unloaded covers the chamber and any magazine attached to or inserted in the firearm."},{"q":"A deer runs between you and your partner walking side by side. What do you do?","options":["Swing and shoot quickly","Hold fire. It is outside both arcs","Shoot only if it stops"],"answer":1,"why":"The space between two hunters walking abreast is a no shot zone. Never swing past your arc."},{"q":"Crossing a barbed wire fence alone, what comes first?","options":["Hold the rifle high and step over","Unload and PROVE it, then pass it under the fence muzzle away","Put the safety on and climb"],"answer":1,"why":"Never cross an obstacle with a loaded firearm. Unload, lay it through, cross away from the muzzle."},{"q":"You and your brother both carry rifles. What do you agree before the walk?","options":["Nothing, just be careful","Who covers left, who covers right, and a stop signal","Who gets the first deer"],"answer":1,"why":"Agreed arcs and a stop signal keep each muzzle off the other hunter."}],"checklists":[],"text":" !why you both passed the course. now there are two loaded guns, two new hunters, cold hands and excitement. that is when habits matter. !warn study aid only. the official regulations are the law. acts: the vital four the cfsc (canadian firearms safety course) teaches these as the \"vital four acts\" (85%). - a ssume every firearm is loaded (85%). - c ontrol the muzzle direction at all times (85%). - t rigger finger off the trigger and out of the trigger guard (85%). - s ee that the firearm is unloaded: prove it safe (85%). - wording read in search previews of the course handbook and course provider pages, not the full handbook (see sources). prove it safe - p oint the firearm in the safest available direction (85%). - r emove all cartridges (85%). - o bserve the chamber (85%). - v erify the feeding path (85%). - e xamine the bore for obstructions (85%). - do acts and prove every time you pick up, put down, hand over or receive a firearm (80%). - also when you clean it, and before you store or transport it (80%). !tip say each letter out loud when you prove it. have your brother do the same. you each catch the other skipping a step. zones of fire - a zone of fire is the only slice of ground where you may shoot (75%). - hunter education teaches about 45 degrees straight ahead for each hunter (75%). - never swing the muzzle outside it, even tracking a running deer (75%). - every shot also needs a safe backstop . sky is never one. two hunters walking side by side - walk in a line, side by side, always in sight of each other (75%). - each covers the arc in front. the middle and behind are no shot zones (75%). - one gets ahead or drops back? both stop. fix the line first. - lose sight of each other? muzzles up or down, no shooting, call out. !tip bright orange on hat and vest helps you see each other in timber. my lean: wear it. whether bc requires it: not covered here. in a blind and in a boat or canoe in a blind - you and your brother agree who takes the left side and who takes the right side before first light. - shoot only birds or deer on your side. muzzles out the front, never across your partner. - guns rest muzzle up or out the front when not shooting. never leaned on the wall behind you. in a boat or canoe - two hunters sit back to back: bow shooter faces forward, stern shooter faces back (70%, hunter education guidance). - shoot sitting. never stand. see the water safety session. - only one person moves at a time. unload before anyone changes seats. - loading in a boat is a legal question too. see the water safety session. fences, logs and ditches - alone: unload, prove it, lay the firearm under or through the fence, muzzle pointed away from you (75%). - cross well away from the muzzle, then pick it up by the stock (75%). - with a partner: one unloads and holds both firearms, actions open. the other crosses, then takes both. then the first crosses (tip, common hunter education method). - never climb a fence, log or bank with a loaded firearm in your hands. at the car - load only where you may lawfully shoot. federal rules allow loading only in such a place (80%). - in bc, you must not transport, carry or store a loaded firearm in or on a vehicle or boat (80%). - \"unloaded\" means no cartridge in the chamber, and none in a magazine attached to or inserted in the firearm (80%). - so: magazine out, chamber empty, before the firearm goes in the car or on the atv (80%). - leaving the car? non restricted firearms go locked in the trunk or a lockable compartment. no trunk: out of sight, car locked (80%). - unload facing away from the car, partner and road. load the same way, at the start of the walk. !warn bc's firearm violence prevention act (fvpa) came into force 1 october 2026, with hunting exemptions made by regulation (75%). exact exemption wording: verify before you hunt. two armed hunters: you and your brother - both of you hold core and a pal . each carries your own licences and tags (owner confirmed). - before every walk, agree three things: who covers left, who covers right, and the stop signal. - only one of you shoots at a deer unless you agreed a backup plan first (tip). - neither one steps in front of the other's muzzle. ever. - after a shot, both unload, then walk up together. the shooter leads. - first hunt for him? swap roles at midday so each practises spotter and shooter (tip). general case: a non hunting companion - a person who only watches carries no firearm (80%, see licences session). - they walk behind or beside you, never ahead, and freeze on your signal. !rule grandpa's rule (wisdom): the safety is a mechanical device. your muzzle and your finger are the real safety. !mistake - pointing the muzzle at your partner while you unload at the tailgate. - leaving a round in the magazine \"because it is not in the chamber\". attached magazine counts as loaded. - following a running deer with the muzzle past your shooting arc. !field at the car, each of you runs prove out loud while the other watches. then walk 50 m (55 yd) side by side, unloaded, and point out each other's arc with your arms. sources - canadian firearms safety course handbook, rcmp (royal canadian mounted police), government of canada publications (https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2015/grc-rcmp/ps99-2-2-1-2014-eng.pdf), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - acts and prove, canadian firearms training (https://www.firearmstraining.ca/actsprove.htm), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - rcmp: storing, transporting and displaying firearms (https://rcmp.ca/en/firearms/firearms-safety-training-transport-and-storage/storing-transporting-and-displaying-firearms), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - storage, display, transportation and handling of firearms by individuals regulations, sor/98-209 (https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/sor-98-209/page-1.html), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - bc firearm violence prevention act page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/justice/criminal-justice/policing-in-bc/fvpa), section 4, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - bc news release on fvpa in force 1 october 2026 (https://news.gov.bc.ca/releases/2026pssg0037-000460), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - hunter ed: determining your safe zone of fire (https://www.hunter-ed.com/national/studyguide/determining-your-safe-zone-of-fire/201099_93020/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, united states course material) - hunter ed: crossing obstacles alone (https://www.hunter-ed.com/national/studyguide/crossing-obstacles-alone/201099_93010/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - note: all pages read through search previews. capped at 85% until the full pages are opened. "},{"id":"water-safety","phase":2,"num":2,"title":"Water safety for hunters","minutes":10,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Water safety for hunters","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>October water in the Interior is cold. A small boat or kayak tips fast. The cold, not swimming skill, is what kills.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Cold water shock and 1 10 1","html":"<ul><li>The \"1 10 1 principle\" is a name, not exact timing. It was made by physiologist Dr. Gordon Giesbrecht <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>1 minute:</strong> cold shock. You gasp and breathe hard. Get your breathing under control <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>10 minutes:</strong> of useful muscle movement before hands and arms stop working <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>1 hour:</strong> before hypothermia makes you unconscious <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Real times vary with water temperature, body and clothing. Some experts say it is too neat <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>The first gasp is the killer. If your head is under water when you gasp, you inhale water <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Use the 10 minutes for one job: get out of the water or onto the hull. Do not swim for shore unless it is very close.</p></aside>"},{"title":"PFD worn, not stowed","html":"<ul><li>A <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"PFD\">PFD</button> (personal flotation device) only works if you are wearing it when you go in.</li><li>Canadian law requires an approved PFD or lifejacket of the right size <strong>on board</strong> for each person <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Wearing it is not required by law for adults on a private trip <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. My lean: wear it, zipped, every minute on the water.</li><li>About 87% of boaters who drowned were not wearing one, per the Lifesaving Society <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Pick one that fits over your hunting jacket. Try it on with all layers before the trip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The boat and the kayak","html":"<ul><li>Any boat fitted with a motor, even a small electric, needs the operator to carry a PCOC (Pleasure Craft Operator Card) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>10 ft boat: check the capacity plate. Count you, your brother, gear, guns and a deer (<span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> plate numbers on your own boat).</li><li>Load low and in the middle. Heavy items on the floor, centred side to side.</li><li>Kayak: one person, guns cased and strapped, dry bag clipped in. Hard to shoot from and easy to tip.</li><li>No dog? Retrieve birds with the boat, motor off and gun unloaded, or with a long handled landing net (Tip).</li><li>Never lean out over the side to grab a bird. Bring it along the hull to the middle.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Guns in the boat","html":"<ul><li>BC: you must not transport, carry or store a <strong>loaded</strong> firearm in or on a vehicle or boat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>BC: no shooting from a boat <strong>propelled by a motor</strong>. A motor tilted up or disengaged so it is not ready for use does not count as propelling <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Federal, ducks and geese: no hunting from a <strong>moving</strong> boat with a motor or sail. Drifting on momentum from the motor still counts as moving <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>BC synopsis, ducks and geese: power boat only if beached, anchored, or tied alongside a fixed blind <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>The FVPA (Firearm Violence Prevention Act) came into force 1 October 2026. Hunting exemptions are set by regulation <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Exact wording: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Safe habit: gun unloaded and in a case while moving. Load only once you are stopped and set (Tip). A case is not required by federal law for a non restricted firearm <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Use the boat as a ride to a shore blind or a point. Shoot from land. It removes most of the legal and tipping risk.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Never stand to shoot","html":"<ul><li>Small boats and canoes tip when weight goes high or to one side.</li><li>Recoil pushes you back. Standing turns that into a fall over the side.</li><li>Sit square, feet flat, shoot only inside your own arc. See the firearm safety session.</li><li>One person moves at a time. Everyone else sits still.</li></ul>"},{"title":"If you capsize","html":"<ol><li>Expect the gasp. Mouth closed if you can. Float in your PFD and get breathing under control for about 1 minute <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Stay with the boat. It is bigger to see and helps you float <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Climb onto the hull to get as much of your body out of the water as you can <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Can't get out? Use the HELP (Heat Escape Lessening Posture): arms crossed on chest, knees up, ankles crossed <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Two people? Huddle, chests together <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Once out, stay out. Water pulls heat away about 25 times faster than air <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Let the gun go. It is replaceable.</li></ol><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): The PFD in the bow locker is a cushion. The PFD on your chest is a lifejacket.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>PFD stowed under the seat \"to put on if we tip\". You will not get it on in cold water.</li><li>Running the motor to chase a crippled duck with a loaded gun.</li><li>Standing up to shoot or to pull in a bird.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Before launching, both wear PFDs over your hunting layers. In shallow water near shore, practise sitting back to back and swapping who leans for a bird, with the gun unloaded.</p></aside><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"ws-launch\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"ws-launch:0\"><span>Both PFDs on and zipped</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"ws-launch:1\"><span>PCOC on board if the motor is fitted</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"ws-launch:2\"><span>Guns unloaded and cased</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"ws-launch:3\"><span>Weight low and centred</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"ws-launch:4\"><span>Trip plan left with someone</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"ws-launch:5\"><span>Dry bag: spare warm layer, lighter, phone</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.lifesavingsociety.com/water-safety/boating-and-lifejackets.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lifesaving Society, boating and lifejackets</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.mcgill.ca/oss/article/medical-did-you-know/professor-popsicles-physiological-proof\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">McGill Office for Science and Society, Professor Popsicle (Giesbrecht, 1 10 1)</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordon_Giesbrecht\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gordon Giesbrecht, Wikipedia</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.coldwatersafety.org/1-10-1-myth\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cold Water Safety: the 1 10 1 critique</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heat_escape_lessening_position\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Heat escape lessening position, Wikipedia</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/justice/criminal-justice/policing-in-bc/fvpa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Firearm Violence Prevention Act page</a>, section 4 and 5, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/loo62/loo62/190_84\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting Regulation, B.C. Reg. 190/84</a>, power boat definition, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-2022-105/section-41.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Migratory Birds Regulations, 2022, section 41</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026</a>, migratory birds section, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, page <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>)</li><li><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pleasure_Craft_Operator_Card\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pleasure Craft Operator Card, Wikipedia</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, secondary)</li><li><a href=\"https://paddlebc.ca/law-of-lifejackets-in-canada-and-finding-the-right-one-for-you/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PaddleBC: the law of lifejackets</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, secondary)</li><li>Note: Transport Canada pages not opened. Capped at 85% and lower for secondary sources.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the first 1 in the 1 10 1 principle warn about?","options":["1 hour until hypothermia","About 1 minute of cold shock: control your breathing","1 metre to the boat"],"answer":1,"why":"Phase one is cold shock. Gasping and fast breathing last about a minute. Calm your breathing before you act."},{"q":"Where should your PFD be while the boat is moving?","options":["Under the seat, easy to reach","On you, zipped","In the dry bag"],"answer":1,"why":"In cold water you will not manage to put one on. Only a worn PFD helps."},{"q":"Can you carry a loaded shotgun while motoring to the blind?","options":["Yes if the safety is on","No. BC bans loaded firearms in or on a boat, and you may not hunt ducks from a moving motor boat","Yes on a lake"],"answer":1,"why":"BC law bans transporting a loaded firearm in a boat. Federal rules ban hunting migratory birds from a moving boat with a motor."},{"q":"You capsize 200 m from shore in October. Best first move after the gasp?","options":["Swim for shore","Stay with the boat and climb onto the hull","Take off your boots and PFD"],"answer":1,"why":"Stay with the boat and get as much of your body out of the water as you can. Swimming in cold water drains you fast."}],"checklists":["ws-launch"],"text":" !why october water in the interior is cold. a small boat or kayak tips fast. the cold, not swimming skill, is what kills. !warn study aid only. the official regulations are the law. cold water shock and 1 10 1 - the \"1 10 1 principle\" is a name, not exact timing. it was made by physiologist dr. gordon giesbrecht (80%). - 1 minute: cold shock. you gasp and breathe hard. get your breathing under control (80%). - 10 minutes: of useful muscle movement before hands and arms stop working (80%). - 1 hour: before hypothermia makes you unconscious (80%). - real times vary with water temperature, body and clothing. some experts say it is too neat (70%). - the first gasp is the killer. if your head is under water when you gasp, you inhale water (75%). !tip use the 10 minutes for one job: get out of the water or onto the hull. do not swim for shore unless it is very close. pfd worn, not stowed - a pfd (personal flotation device) only works if you are wearing it when you go in. - canadian law requires an approved pfd or lifejacket of the right size on board for each person (75%). - wearing it is not required by law for adults on a private trip (75%). my lean: wear it, zipped, every minute on the water. - about 87% of boaters who drowned were not wearing one, per the lifesaving society (75%). - pick one that fits over your hunting jacket. try it on with all layers before the trip. the boat and the kayak - any boat fitted with a motor, even a small electric, needs the operator to carry a pcoc (pleasure craft operator card) (75%). - 10 ft boat: check the capacity plate. count you, your brother, gear, guns and a deer (verify plate numbers on your own boat). - load low and in the middle. heavy items on the floor, centred side to side. - kayak: one person, guns cased and strapped, dry bag clipped in. hard to shoot from and easy to tip. - no dog? retrieve birds with the boat, motor off and gun unloaded, or with a long handled landing net (tip). - never lean out over the side to grab a bird. bring it along the hull to the middle. guns in the boat - bc: you must not transport, carry or store a loaded firearm in or on a vehicle or boat (80%). - bc: no shooting from a boat propelled by a motor . a motor tilted up or disengaged so it is not ready for use does not count as propelling (75%). - federal, ducks and geese: no hunting from a moving boat with a motor or sail. drifting on momentum from the motor still counts as moving (80%). - bc synopsis, ducks and geese: power boat only if beached, anchored, or tied alongside a fixed blind (75%). - the fvpa (firearm violence prevention act) came into force 1 october 2026. hunting exemptions are set by regulation (75%). exact wording: verify. - safe habit: gun unloaded and in a case while moving. load only once you are stopped and set (tip). a case is not required by federal law for a non restricted firearm (70%). !lean use the boat as a ride to a shore blind or a point. shoot from land. it removes most of the legal and tipping risk. never stand to shoot - small boats and canoes tip when weight goes high or to one side. - recoil pushes you back. standing turns that into a fall over the side. - sit square, feet flat, shoot only inside your own arc. see the firearm safety session. - one person moves at a time. everyone else sits still. if you capsize 1. expect the gasp. mouth closed if you can. float in your pfd and get breathing under control for about 1 minute (80%). 2. stay with the boat. it is bigger to see and helps you float (75%). 3. climb onto the hull to get as much of your body out of the water as you can (75%). 4. can't get out? use the help (heat escape lessening posture): arms crossed on chest, knees up, ankles crossed (75%). 5. two people? huddle, chests together (75%). 6. once out, stay out. water pulls heat away about 25 times faster than air (70%). 7. let the gun go. it is replaceable. !rule grandpa's rule (wisdom): the pfd in the bow locker is a cushion. the pfd on your chest is a lifejacket. !mistake - pfd stowed under the seat \"to put on if we tip\". you will not get it on in cold water. - running the motor to chase a crippled duck with a loaded gun. - standing up to shoot or to pull in a bird. !field before launching, both wear pfds over your hunting layers. in shallow water near shore, practise sitting back to back and swapping who leans for a bird, with the gun unloaded. sources - lifesaving society, boating and lifejackets (https://www.lifesavingsociety.com/water-safety/boating-and-lifejackets.aspx), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - mcgill office for science and society, professor popsicle (giesbrecht, 1 10 1) (https://www.mcgill.ca/oss/article/medical-did-you-know/professor-popsicles-physiological-proof), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - gordon giesbrecht, wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/gordon_giesbrecht), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - cold water safety: the 1 10 1 critique (https://www.coldwatersafety.org/1-10-1-myth), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - heat escape lessening position, wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/heat_escape_lessening_position), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - bc firearm violence prevention act page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/justice/criminal-justice/policing-in-bc/fvpa), section 4 and 5, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - bc hunting regulation, b.c. reg. 190/84 (https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/loo62/loo62/190_84), power boat definition, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - migratory birds regulations, 2022, section 41 (https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/sor-2022-105/section-41.html), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf), migratory birds section, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, page verify) - pleasure craft operator card, wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/pleasure_craft_operator_card), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, secondary) - paddlebc: the law of lifejackets (https://paddlebc.ca/law-of-lifejackets-in-canada-and-finding-the-right-one-for-you/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, secondary) - note: transport canada pages not opened. capped at 85% and lower for secondary sources. "},{"id":"bush-safety","phase":2,"num":3,"title":"Bush safety","minutes":15,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Bush safety","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Most bad days in the bush start small: a wrong turn, wet socks, dark at 6 pm. A plan and a few tools turn them back into small problems.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside>"},{"title":"STOP when lost","html":"<ul><li>AdventureSmart teaches STOP: Stop, Think, Observe, Plan <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Some AdventureSmart material uses Sit instead of Stop. Same idea <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Stop:</strong> do not keep walking. Most people make it worse by pushing on.</li><li><strong>Think:</strong> where did you last know where you were?</li><li><strong>Observe:</strong> map app, compass, sun, creeks, the sound of a road.</li><li><strong>Plan:</strong> stay put, or move only if you are sure. Shelter and fire before dark.</li><li>Use it when lost, disoriented or hurt <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The trip plan","html":"<ul><li>AdventureSmart's 3 Ts: Trip planning, Training, Taking the essentials <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Always fill out a trip plan and leave it with someone at home <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>AdventureSmart has a free trip plan app that sends your plan to a contact <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Write: who, vehicle and plate, where you park, route, return time, when to call for help.</li><li>Say who to call if you are late: 911, and police will start a search (Tip: confirm with your local police).</li><li>Assumption: you hunt from the car, the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"ATV\">ATV</button> and the boat. List all three in the plan.</li></ul><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"bs-tripplan\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"bs-tripplan:0\"><span>Trip plan written and sent</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"bs-tripplan:1\"><span>Return time and \"call 911 if not back by\" time set</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"bs-tripplan:2\"><span>Map of the area downloaded for offline use</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"bs-tripplan:3\"><span>Phone and satellite device charged</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Satellite messengers and iPhone","html":"<ul><li>Much of the backcountry near Sun Peaks has no cell signal (Assumption: check with your carrier map).</li><li>iPhone 14 or later can send Emergency SOS via satellite in Canada with iOS 16.1 or later <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Messages via satellite and Find My via satellite also work in Canada on iPhone 14 or later <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Apple says it is free for two years from activation of the phone <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. After that: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>It needs open sky. Trees, hills and canyons slow or block it <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>A dedicated satellite messenger (two way texting and SOS) works on any phone. Needs a paid plan.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>iPhone 14 or later in both pockets? That covers SOS. Only one does, or an older phone? Buy or rent a satellite messenger so each hunter can call for help alone.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Hypothermia","html":"<ul><li>Mild: shivering, cold, and the \"umbles\": fumbles, mumbles, stumbles, grumbles <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Moderate: violent shivering, confusion, poor judgment <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Severe: shivering <strong>stops</strong>, stiff muscles, glassy stare, slow breathing. This is an emergency <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>First aid: out of wind and wet, dry layers on, cover head and neck, rewarm gently <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Warm sweet drinks only if fully awake and able to swallow (Tip, common first aid advice).</li><li>October risk: wet from sweat or rain, then sitting still glassing as the sun drops.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Weather and wildfire","html":"<ul><li>Interior October can go from sun to sleet in an afternoon. Pack a dry layer and a toque every trip.</li><li>Daylight is short. Turn back time = 1 hour before sunset.</li><li>Kamloops Fire Centre campfire and open fire rules changed in late September 2026 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Today's status: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the BC Wildfire Service app before any fire.</li><li>Report a wildfire: 1 800 663 5555 toll free, or *5555 on a cell <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Never park a hot vehicle or <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"ATV\">ATV</button> on dry grass. The exhaust can start a fire (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Bears and cougars","html":"<ul><li>Black bears live around Kamloops and Sun Peaks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Grizzlies: the Thompson Nicola region holds about 317 grizzlies <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>. A Sun Peaks visitor site says only black bears at the resort <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>. My lean: carry spray as if both are there.</li><li>Bear spray: point it at the ground a few metres in front of the bear, then sweep up to its face <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Do not spray into the wind. It will blow back on you <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Range: about 3 m to 10 m depending on the can. Read your can <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Black bear attack: defensive (cubs or food) is the most common type. Spray. If it makes contact, play dead (75%, Parks Canada).</li><li>Predatory attack (stalks you, or at night): spray, stand big, fight back. Do not play dead (75%, Parks Canada).</li><li>Cougar: never run, look big, back away slowly, keep eye contact. If attacked, always fight back (75%, WildSafeBC).</li><li>A gut pile draws bears. Move the meat away from it fast and watch your back.</li><li>Report a dangerous bear or cougar: RAPP (Report All Poachers and Polluters) 1 877 952 7277 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"First aid kit with a tourniquet","html":"<ul><li>The big killer after a fall, knife slip or gunshot is bleeding from an arm or leg.</li><li>Red Cross guidance: firm direct pressure first. If bleeding from a limb is life threatening and pressure fails, use a tourniquet <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Place it about 5 to 10 cm (2 to 4 in) above the wound, between wound and heart. Tighten until bleeding stops. Write the time <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Buy a commercial windlass tourniquet, not a homemade one. The Red Cross training kit uses the SOFTT (Special Operations Forces Tactical Tourniquet) type <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Kit carried by each hunter on the body: tourniquet, gauze, pressure bandage, gloves, emergency blanket, lighter.</li><li>Take a first aid course. A kit you do not know how to use is weight.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): Tell someone where you went. Pack like you will sleep out. Leave the bush an hour before dark.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>No trip plan because \"we are only going up the road\".</li><li>Bear spray buried in the pack instead of on the hip belt or chest.</li><li>Sweating hard on the climb in, then sitting still in wet clothes to glass.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Before the first hunt: fill out the AdventureSmart trip plan, test SOS demo mode on your iPhone, and each practise drawing bear spray from the holster in under 2 seconds with the safety clip on.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.adventuresmart.ca/the-three-ts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AdventureSmart: The Three Ts</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://globalnews.ca/news/9437036/b-c-adventure-smart-safety-plan-stop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Global News: BC AdventureSmart safety plan, STOP</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://support.apple.com/en-ca/101573\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Apple Support: Use Emergency SOS via satellite on your iPhone (Canada)</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://support.apple.com/en-us/120930\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Apple Support: About Messages via satellite</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.redcross.org/take-a-class/resources/learn-first-aid/hypothermia\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Red Cross: hypothermia</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.redcross.ca/training-and-certification/first-aid-tips-and-resources/first-aid-tips/cold-related-emergencies-staying-warm-and-safe-in-canadian-winters\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canadian Red Cross: cold related emergencies</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.redcross.org/take-a-class/resources/articles/how-to-apply-a-tourniquet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Red Cross: how to apply a tourniquet</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/mtn/ours-bears/securite-safety/gaz-spray\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Parks Canada: bear spray, mountain national parks</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/nt/nahanni/securite-safety/ours_bears/security-safety\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Parks Canada: A guide to safety, Nahanni (attack types)</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bcforestsafe.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Safety-Alert-of-the-Month-March-2025-Final.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Forest Safety Council: bear spray safety alert</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://wildsafebc.com/species/cougar/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WildSafeBC: Cougar</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://tnccollaborative.org/in-our-backyard-grizzly-bear/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Thompson Nicola Conservation Collaborative: In Our Backyard, Grizzly Bear</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bestsunpeaks.com/bears-in-sun-peaks.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Best Sun Peaks: Bears in Sun Peaks</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, visitor site)</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/safety/wildfire-status/contact-channels/report-a-wildfire\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Report a wildfire</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://cfjctoday.com/2026/09/29/category-2-and-3-open-fires-permitted-within-the-cariboo-fire-centre/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kamloops Fire Centre open fire update, CFJC Today</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, news)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.mission.ca/community-environment/environment-sustainability/bears-wildlife\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">City of Mission: Bears and wildlife (RAPP number)</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, secondary)</li><li>Note: all pages read through search previews. Capped at 85%.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You realize you do not know where the truck is. First step?","options":["Walk fast downhill","STOP: Stop, Think, Observe, Plan","Fire three shots"],"answer":1,"why":"AdventureSmart teaches STOP. Pushing on is how most people get more lost."},{"q":"Which iPhones can use Emergency SOS via satellite in Canada?","options":["Any iPhone","iPhone 14 or later, with iOS 16.1 or later","Only Pro models"],"answer":1,"why":"Apple lists iPhone 14 or later, all models, with iOS 16.1 or later in Canada."},{"q":"Your brother stops shivering but is confused and stiff. What does that mean?","options":["He has warmed up","Severe hypothermia, an emergency","He is just tired"],"answer":1,"why":"Shivering that stops with confusion and stiffness is a sign of severe hypothermia. Call for help and rewarm gently."},{"q":"A black bear stalks you quietly at dusk and closes in. What do you do?","options":["Play dead","Spray, stand big, fight back if it attacks","Run to the truck"],"answer":1,"why":"Parks Canada: a predatory attack means spray and fight back. Playing dead is only for a defensive attack once contact is made."},{"q":"When do you use a tourniquet?","options":["Any cut","Life threatening bleeding from an arm or leg when direct pressure fails","Only on the head"],"answer":1,"why":"Direct pressure first. A tourniquet goes on a limb when bleeding is life threatening and pressure is not working."}],"checklists":["bs-tripplan"],"text":" !why most bad days in the bush start small: a wrong turn, wet socks, dark at 6 pm. a plan and a few tools turn them back into small problems. !warn study aid only. the official regulations are the law. stop when lost - adventuresmart teaches stop: stop, think, observe, plan (75%). - some adventuresmart material uses sit instead of stop. same idea (70%). - stop: do not keep walking. most people make it worse by pushing on. - think: where did you last know where you were? - observe: map app, compass, sun, creeks, the sound of a road. - plan: stay put, or move only if you are sure. shelter and fire before dark. - use it when lost, disoriented or hurt (75%). the trip plan - adventuresmart's 3 ts: trip planning, training, taking the essentials (75%). - always fill out a trip plan and leave it with someone at home (75%). - adventuresmart has a free trip plan app that sends your plan to a contact (70%). - write: who, vehicle and plate, where you park, route, return time, when to call for help. - say who to call if you are late: 911, and police will start a search (tip: confirm with your local police). - assumption: you hunt from the car, the atv and the boat. list all three in the plan. satellite messengers and iphone - much of the backcountry near sun peaks has no cell signal (assumption: check with your carrier map). - iphone 14 or later can send emergency sos via satellite in canada with ios 16.1 or later (85%). - messages via satellite and find my via satellite also work in canada on iphone 14 or later (80%). - apple says it is free for two years from activation of the phone (80%). after that: verify. - it needs open sky. trees, hills and canyons slow or block it (75%). - a dedicated satellite messenger (two way texting and sos) works on any phone. needs a paid plan. !lean iphone 14 or later in both pockets? that covers sos. only one does, or an older phone? buy or rent a satellite messenger so each hunter can call for help alone. hypothermia - mild: shivering, cold, and the \"umbles\": fumbles, mumbles, stumbles, grumbles (75%). - moderate: violent shivering, confusion, poor judgment (75%). - severe: shivering stops , stiff muscles, glassy stare, slow breathing. this is an emergency (75%). - first aid: out of wind and wet, dry layers on, cover head and neck, rewarm gently (75%). - warm sweet drinks only if fully awake and able to swallow (tip, common first aid advice). - october risk: wet from sweat or rain, then sitting still glassing as the sun drops. weather and wildfire - interior october can go from sun to sleet in an afternoon. pack a dry layer and a toque every trip. - daylight is short. turn back time = 1 hour before sunset. - kamloops fire centre campfire and open fire rules changed in late september 2026 (75%). today's status: verify on the bc wildfire service app before any fire. - report a wildfire: 1 800 663 5555 toll free, or 5555 on a cell (80%). - never park a hot vehicle or atv on dry grass. the exhaust can start a fire (tip). bears and cougars - black bears live around kamloops and sun peaks (75%). - grizzlies: the thompson nicola region holds about 317 grizzlies (65%). a sun peaks visitor site says only black bears at the resort (60%). my lean: carry spray as if both are there. - bear spray: point it at the ground a few metres in front of the bear, then sweep up to its face (70%). - do not spray into the wind. it will blow back on you (70%). - range: about 3 m to 10 m depending on the can. read your can (70%). - black bear attack: defensive (cubs or food) is the most common type. spray. if it makes contact, play dead (75%, parks canada). - predatory attack (stalks you, or at night): spray, stand big, fight back. do not play dead (75%, parks canada). - cougar: never run, look big, back away slowly, keep eye contact. if attacked, always fight back (75%, wildsafebc). - a gut pile draws bears. move the meat away from it fast and watch your back. - report a dangerous bear or cougar: rapp (report all poachers and polluters) 1 877 952 7277 (75%). first aid kit with a tourniquet - the big killer after a fall, knife slip or gunshot is bleeding from an arm or leg. - red cross guidance: firm direct pressure first. if bleeding from a limb is life threatening and pressure fails, use a tourniquet (70%). - place it about 5 to 10 cm (2 to 4 in) above the wound, between wound and heart. tighten until bleeding stops. write the time (70%). - buy a commercial windlass tourniquet, not a homemade one. the red cross training kit uses the softt (special operations forces tactical tourniquet) type (65%). - kit carried by each hunter on the body: tourniquet, gauze, pressure bandage, gloves, emergency blanket, lighter. - take a first aid course. a kit you do not know how to use is weight. !rule grandpa's rule (wisdom): tell someone where you went. pack like you will sleep out. leave the bush an hour before dark. !mistake - no trip plan because \"we are only going up the road\". - bear spray buried in the pack instead of on the hip belt or chest. - sweating hard on the climb in, then sitting still in wet clothes to glass. !field before the first hunt: fill out the adventuresmart trip plan, test sos demo mode on your iphone, and each practise drawing bear spray from the holster in under 2 seconds with the safety clip on. sources - adventuresmart: the three ts (https://www.adventuresmart.ca/the-three-ts/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - global news: bc adventuresmart safety plan, stop (https://globalnews.ca/news/9437036/b-c-adventure-smart-safety-plan-stop), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - apple support: use emergency sos via satellite on your iphone (canada) (https://support.apple.com/en-ca/101573), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - apple support: about messages via satellite (https://support.apple.com/en-us/120930), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - red cross: hypothermia (https://www.redcross.org/take-a-class/resources/learn-first-aid/hypothermia), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - canadian red cross: cold related emergencies (https://www.redcross.ca/training-and-certification/first-aid-tips-and-resources/first-aid-tips/cold-related-emergencies-staying-warm-and-safe-in-canadian-winters), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - red cross: how to apply a tourniquet (https://www.redcross.org/take-a-class/resources/articles/how-to-apply-a-tourniquet), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - parks canada: bear spray, mountain national parks (https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/mtn/ours-bears/securite-safety/gaz-spray), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - parks canada: a guide to safety, nahanni (attack types) (https://parks.canada.ca/pn-np/nt/nahanni/securite-safety/ours_bears/security-safety), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - bc forest safety council: bear spray safety alert (https://www.bcforestsafe.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/safety-alert-of-the-month-march-2025-final.pdf), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - wildsafebc: cougar (https://wildsafebc.com/species/cougar/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - thompson nicola conservation collaborative: in our backyard, grizzly bear (https://tnccollaborative.org/in-our-backyard-grizzly-bear/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - best sun peaks: bears in sun peaks (https://www.bestsunpeaks.com/bears-in-sun-peaks.html), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, visitor site) - bc report a wildfire (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/safety/wildfire-status/contact-channels/report-a-wildfire), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - kamloops fire centre open fire update, cfjc today (https://cfjctoday.com/2026/09/29/category-2-and-3-open-fires-permitted-within-the-cariboo-fire-centre/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, news) - city of mission: bears and wildlife (rapp number) (https://www.mission.ca/community-environment/environment-sustainability/bears-wildlife), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview, secondary) - note: all pages read through search previews. capped at 85%. "},{"id":"bc-rules","phase":2,"num":5,"title":"BC rules you must know","minutes":20,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"BC rules you must know","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Most fines are for simple things: a round in the chamber in the truck, a shot from the road, a missing tag.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><ul><li>Every rule below has a certainty %. Under 80% means: read the official text before you rely on it.</li><li>Most rules here were read in search previews of official pages. Capped at 85%.</li><li>Seasons, bag limits and fees live in the Rules tab, not here.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Licences: what to carry","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Hunting</th><th>Carry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deer</td><td>Photo ID, your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"FWID\">FWID</button> (Fish and Wildlife ID) number, every deer species licence for the year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ducks and geese</td><td>Photo ID, FWID number, federal <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MGBHP\">MGBHP</button> (Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit), paper or phone <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Quail and grouse</td><td>Photo ID, FWID number. No species licence for residents <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>The BC hunting licence is electronic only. Be able to give your FWID number <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Carry your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"PAL\">PAL</button> (Possession and Acquisition Licence) any time you have a firearm. Tip: habit, not a hunting rule.</li><li>Full detail: Phase 1 Session 2, Licences and tags.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Legal hunting hours","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Game</th><th>Earliest</th><th>Latest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deer, quail, grouse (all game except migratory birds)</td><td>1 hour before sunrise</td><td>1 hour after sunset</td></tr><tr><td>Ducks and geese</td><td>30 minutes before sunrise</td><td>30 minutes after sunset</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Big game and upland hours: 85%. Ducks and geese hours: 85%.</li><li>Ducks get <strong>less</strong> light than deer. Check both times every morning.</li><li>Sunrise and sunset change by town. Use a local table, not a city 200 km away. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"\"Loaded\" in BC","html":"<ul><li>BC meaning: live ammunition in the <strong>breech (chamber) or the magazine</strong>. A clip with live rounds attached to the firearm counts as the magazine <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>So a full magazine in the rifle with an empty chamber <strong>is loaded</strong> in BC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Federal \"unloaded\": no cartridge in the chamber and none in a magazine attached to or inserted in the firearm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Loose rounds in your pocket or a detached magazine: not loaded (80%, my reading of both definitions).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout law\"><div class=\"callout-t\">The rule</div><p>No loaded firearm in or on a motor vehicle, railway car, sleigh, aircraft, bicycle or other conveyance. No shooting from one either <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Truck, ATV and boat","html":"<ul><li><strong>Truck:</strong> magazine out, chamber empty before the firearm goes in the truck <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>ATV (all terrain vehicle):</strong> same rule. It is a motor vehicle or conveyance. Unload before you get on <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Engine off is not enough. Get off the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"ATV\">ATV</button>, step away, then load <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Federal rules also say load only where shooting is lawful (75%, secondary).</li><li><strong>No shooting wildlife from a motor vehicle or a boat propelled by a motor</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The 10 ft boat with motor: no deer shot from it, ever <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Kayak: no motor, so the BC motorboat rule does not apply. Ducks: see the duck screen. Deer from a kayak: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Tip: do not.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Roads","html":"<ul><li>No shooting <strong>across</strong> a road <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>No hunting or shooting on the road allowance of a public road <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Road allowance: 15 m (16 yd) each side of the middle of a road with under 3 lanes. Or 15 m from the pavement edge on 3 lanes or more. Or to private or cultivated land, if closer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Which roads count (for example a <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"FSR\">FSR</button>, Forest Service Road): <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the synopsis.</li><li>Safe habit: walk at least 15 m off the road, then load. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"The 100 m rule","html":"<aside class=\"callout law\"><div class=\"callout-t\">The rule</div><p>No hunting or shooting within 100 m (109 yd) of a church, school building, school yard, playground, regional district park, or a dwelling house, farm or ranch building occupied by people or domestic animals <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</p></aside><ul><li>\"Occupied\" includes animals in a barn <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>100 m is close. A rifle bullet travels kilometres. Use your rangefinder on buildings. Tip.</li><li>Owners can slaughter their own livestock near their buildings. That exemption is not for hunting <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Your brother's house on the 20 acre property counts.</strong> No shot within 100 m of it, even with his permission (80%, my reading: the only owner exemption is livestock slaughter).</li><li>Barns or sheds with animals inside count too <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Field job: rangefind the house from each likely stand. Mark a 100 m ring on your phone map. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"No Shooting Areas and closed areas","html":"<ul><li>A No Shooting Area: firing a firearm is banned, even outside hunting <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>They are on the regional maps in the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Synopsis\">Synopsis</button> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Region 3 synopsis has Kamloops area maps with No Shooting and No Hunting Areas <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Exact boundaries near Heffley Creek: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Some towns and regional districts add their own discharge bylaws. Sun Peaks and Heffley Creek: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Check the map <strong>before</strong> you go, not on the slope.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Parks and protected land","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Place</th><th>Hunting?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ecological reserve</td><td><strong>No.</strong> No hunting, no firing a firearm or bow <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>BC park</td><td>Only in parks listed for hunting, only in open season <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>BC park road</td><td>No shooting within 400 m of the centre line of a park road <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Wildlife Management Area</td><td>Rules differ by area. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> each one</td></tr><tr><td>First Nations reserve</td><td>Private land. Permission from the band office to hunt on <strong>or cross</strong> it <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Park closed to hunting: you may not even carry a firearm there, unless a park officer allows it <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Check each park page on bcparks.ca before you go <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Private land and the Trespass Act","html":"<ul><li>Hunting private land needs the owner's consent <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Get it <strong>in writing</strong>. Tip.</li><li>Under the Trespass Act, \"enclosed land\" includes land with a lawful fence, a natural boundary, or signs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Signs must be at each normal access point and readable in daylight <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>On enclosed land you are presumed to have <strong>no</strong> permission. You must prove you do <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Cultivated land (crops, hay, orchard): treat as no hunting without permission <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> wording.</li><li>A \"No Hunting\" sign may not be a trespass sign, but respect it anyway (65%, secondary).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Motor vehicle closures","html":"<ul><li>Motor Vehicle Closed Areas (old name: Access Management Areas) ban some or all vehicles, or only ATVs or snowmobiles <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Motor Vehicle Hunting Closed Areas ban using a vehicle to hunt, carry hunters, gear or game <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Region 3 maps: <a href=\"https://www.gov.bc.ca/thompsonaccess\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">gov.bc.ca/thompsonaccess</a> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Areas near Heffley Creek and Sun Peaks: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the map.</li><li>Fire season closures and gates also apply. Check BC Wildfire Service. Tip.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Banned methods","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rimfire on big game</td><td><strong>Not allowed</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>.22 rimfire on grouse or quail</td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in synopsis firearm table</td></tr><tr><td>Lights or spotlights</td><td><strong>Never</strong> hunt with a light <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Night hunting</td><td>Banned outside legal hours <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Drones</td><td>No drone on a hunting trip. No drone info from under 6 hours before <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Electronic calls</td><td>Banned, except wolf, coyote, cougar, lynx, bobcat and some listed birds <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Dogs for deer</td><td>Leash only <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Grouse and quail: unleashed dog OK <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Feeding or baiting deer</td><td>Banned within 200 m of a home, school yard or playground in Regions 3 and 8 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Bait for deer elsewhere: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td></tr><tr><td>Electronic firing mechanism</td><td>Not allowed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"title":"Ducks and geese (federal)","html":"<ul><li>Non toxic shot only for ducks, geese, coots and snipe. No lead <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Shotgun no larger than 10 gauge <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Holds <strong>3 shells max</strong>, chamber plus magazine. Plug must need the gun taken apart to remove <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>No hunting from a <strong>moving</strong> boat with a motor or sail <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>OK when the boat is beached, anchored or tied off <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>A motorboat may chase a wounded bird to retrieve it <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Possession limit: 3 times the daily limit, at all times, <strong>including at home</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Daily limits for 2026 to 2027: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the Rules tab.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Ducks: the bird in your cooler","html":"<ul><li>Unpreserved birds keep <strong>one fully feathered wing or the fully feathered head</strong> attached <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Store birds so each can be counted and identified. No frozen block of ducks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>\"Preserved\" means fully processed for storage, for example frozen and plucked. Exact definition: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Tip: leave a wing on until the duck is in your home freezer.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Deer: tag and evidence","html":"<ul><li>Notch the species licence right after the kill, before you move the animal <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. See Phase 1 Session 13.</li><li>Evidence of sex stays <strong>naturally attached</strong> until the meat is home, at a cutter, in cold storage, or inspected (75%, secondary).</li><li>Buck: the part of the head with antlers, <strong>or</strong> testicles or penis plus the tail or a patch of hide (75%, secondary).</li><li>Doe: the part of the head where antlers would be, <strong>or</strong> udder or teats plus the tail or a patch of hide (75%, secondary).</li><li>Antler restricted season (like 4 point): keep antlers attached to part of the skull <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Exact hide patch size: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Meat wastage","html":"<ul><li>You must remove the <strong>edible portions</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Deer edible portions: neck, ribs, all four quarters and the loins <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Game birds (ducks, quail, grouse): both breasts <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Leave guts and bones at the kill site, or take them to a landfill <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>You may give the animal to someone who then takes the edible portions out <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"CWD (Chronic Wasting Disease)","html":"<ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> is confirmed in the Kootenay Region <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>CWD Management Zone: <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MU\">MU</button> (Management Unit) 4-1 to 4-8 and 4-20 to 4-25 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>In that zone: mandatory CWD test for every deer, elk and moose, within one week <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>No brain or spinal column (tail OK) taken out of that zone <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Region 3 and Region 8 are <strong>not</strong> in the zone (85%, from the MU list).</li><li>Bringing cervid (deer family) parts in from outside BC is restricted (65%, secondary). <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> before any hunt outside BC.</li><li>Deer urine lures from outside BC: may be banned <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Tip: use none.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Transporting firearms (federal)","html":"<ul><li>Unloaded while in a vehicle <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Non restricted rifle or shotgun: no case or trigger lock needed while you travel <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Leaving it in the vehicle: lock it in the trunk or a locked compartment. No trunk: out of sight in the <strong>locked</strong> vehicle <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>No special permit to carry a non restricted firearm. Your PAL (Possession and Acquisition Licence) is enough <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Ammunition can travel with it, just not in it <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Giving away meat, and helpers","html":"<ul><li>Anyone carrying your game without you needs a <strong>Record of Receipt</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Record of Receipt shows: date and place, your name and address, who receives it, your FWID (Fish and Wildlife ID), the species licence number, species and sex <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Your brother hunts too. He needs his <strong>own</strong> hunting licence, species licences and federal bird permit <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Each hunter notches only his own licence, for the animal he killed. Shooting a deer for the other person's tag: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Treat as not allowed.</li><li>Ducks: each hunter has his own daily limit. Do not pool birds. Tip.</li><li>Carrying his deer while he is not with you: you need his Record of Receipt <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Friends you give meat to at home: write a short Record of Receipt anyway. Tip.</li><li>Selling game meat: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Treat as not allowed.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Wounded animal on closed or private land","html":"<ul><li>The Trespass Act has no exception for tracking wounded game (70%, secondary).</li><li>Posted or fenced land: ask the owner first. Written permission is best <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Owner says no, or can't be found: call a conservation officer. Tip.</li><li>Animal ran into a park or No Shooting Area: do not fire there. Call a conservation officer. Tip.</li><li>You must still make a real effort to recover wounded game <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> the exact rule.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Reporting poaching","html":"<ul><li>RAPP (Report All Poachers and Polluters): 1 877 952 7277 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Cell: #7277 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Open 24 hours, 7 days. You can stay anonymous <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Note: plate, vehicle, place, time, what you saw. Never confront. Tip.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>If you have to ask \"is this legal?\", the answer today is no. Find out at home, then come back.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>A full magazine in the rifle in the truck. That is loaded in BC.</li><li>A quick shot from the road or the ATV seat.</li><li>Freezing ducks with no wing or head attached.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Before each trip: open the synopsis map for your MU. Mark No Shooting Areas, closures and buildings on your phone map.</p></aside><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s5-rules\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s5-rules:0\"><span>Magazine out and chamber empty before the truck or ATV</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s5-rules:1\"><span>Walk 15 m off the road before loading</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s5-rules:2\"><span>Rangefinder check on any building inside 150 m</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s5-rules:3\"><span>100 m ring around my brother's house marked on my map</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s5-rules:4\"><span>No Shooting Areas checked on the synopsis map</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s5-rules:5\"><span>Motor vehicle closures checked for my MU</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s5-rules:6\"><span>Written permission for any private land</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s5-rules:7\"><span>RAPP number saved in my phone</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, full PDF</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: loaded, vehicles, roads, 100 m, hours, banned methods, parks, edible portions, Record of Receipt.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Region 3 Thompson synopsis</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: Kamloops maps, closures, baiting.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-8-okanagan.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Region 8 Okanagan synopsis</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: baiting.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting Regulation, B.C. Reg. 190/84</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hours.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/consol21/consol21/00_96462_01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Trespass Act</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/plants-animals-ecosystems/wildlife/wildlife-conservation/wildlife-health/chronic-wasting-disease/cwd-bc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC CWD page</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/natural-resource-stewardship/natural-resource-law-enforcement/conservation-officer-service/cos-rapp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC RAPP page</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/frequently-asked-questions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC hunting FAQ</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-2022-105/FullText.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Migratory Birds Regulations, 2022</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ECCC (Environment and Climate Change Canada) BC migratory bird summary</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://rcmp.ca/en/firearms/firearms-safety-training-transport-and-storage/storing-transporting-and-displaying-firearms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RCMP (Royal Canadian Mounted Police): storing, transporting and displaying firearms</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-98-209/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Storage, Display, Transportation and Handling of Firearms by Individuals Regulations, SOR/98-209</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li>Secondary, 60 to 79% max: <a href=\"https://bcfirearmsacademy.ca/possession-transportation-big-game/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BC Firearms Academy, evidence of sex</a>; <a href=\"https://www.huntingbc.ca/forum/archive/index.php/t-92535.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HuntingBC forum, wounded game on private land</a>; <a href=\"https://cwd-info.org/regulations/british-columbia-cwd-regulations/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cwd info.org BC page</a>; <a href=\"https://www.agcanada.com/daily/b-c-to-block-hunting-attractants-from-out-of-province\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgCanada, attractant ban</a>. Checked 2026-10-01.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Rifle in the truck: chamber empty, full magazine clipped in. Is it loaded in BC?","options":["No, the chamber is empty","Yes. Live rounds in an attached magazine count as loaded","Only if the safety is off"],"answer":1,"why":"BC defines loaded as live ammunition in the breech or the magazine. An attached clip counts as the magazine."},{"q":"A buck stands 60 m from a farmhouse with people home. Can you shoot?","options":["Yes, it is past 50 m","No. Inside 100 m of an occupied dwelling or farm building","Yes with a safe backstop"],"answer":1,"why":"No hunting or shooting within 100 m of an occupied dwelling, farm or ranch building."},{"q":"Ducks in your brother's boat. When can you shoot?","options":["Any time the motor is idling","Only when the boat is beached, anchored or tied off, not moving under motor","Never from any boat"],"answer":1,"why":"Federal rules ban hunting migratory birds from a moving boat with a motor or sail."},{"q":"What must stay on a duck until it is preserved at home?","options":["The feet","One fully feathered wing or the fully feathered head","Nothing"],"answer":1,"why":"Officers use the wing or head to identify the species and count your bag."},{"q":"Which deer parts must you take out as edible portions?","options":["Back straps only","Neck, ribs, all four quarters and the loins","Hind quarters only"],"answer":1,"why":"The synopsis defines edible portions for big game as neck, ribs, four quarters and loins."}],"checklists":["s5-rules"],"text":" !why most fines are for simple things: a round in the chamber in the truck, a shot from the road, a missing tag. !warn study aid only. the official regulations are the law. - every rule below has a certainty %. under 80% means: read the official text before you rely on it. - most rules here were read in search previews of official pages. capped at 85%. - seasons, bag limits and fees live in the rules tab, not here. licences: what to carry hunting carry --- --- deer photo id, your fwid (fish and wildlife id) number, every deer species licence for the year (85%) ducks and geese photo id, fwid number, federal mgbhp (migratory game bird hunting permit), paper or phone (85%) quail and grouse photo id, fwid number. no species licence for residents (80%) - the bc hunting licence is electronic only. be able to give your fwid number (85%). - carry your pal (possession and acquisition licence) any time you have a firearm. tip: habit, not a hunting rule. - full detail: phase 1 session 2, licences and tags. legal hunting hours game earliest latest --- --- --- deer, quail, grouse (all game except migratory birds) 1 hour before sunrise 1 hour after sunset ducks and geese 30 minutes before sunrise 30 minutes after sunset - big game and upland hours: 85%. ducks and geese hours: 85%. - ducks get less light than deer. check both times every morning. - sunrise and sunset change by town. use a local table, not a city 200 km away. tip. \"loaded\" in bc - bc meaning: live ammunition in the breech (chamber) or the magazine . a clip with live rounds attached to the firearm counts as the magazine (85%). - so a full magazine in the rifle with an empty chamber is loaded in bc (85%). - federal \"unloaded\": no cartridge in the chamber and none in a magazine attached to or inserted in the firearm (85%). - loose rounds in your pocket or a detached magazine: not loaded (80%, my reading of both definitions). !law no loaded firearm in or on a motor vehicle, railway car, sleigh, aircraft, bicycle or other conveyance. no shooting from one either (85%). truck, atv and boat - truck: magazine out, chamber empty before the firearm goes in the truck (85%). - atv (all terrain vehicle): same rule. it is a motor vehicle or conveyance. unload before you get on (80%). - engine off is not enough. get off the atv , step away, then load (80%). federal rules also say load only where shooting is lawful (75%, secondary). - no shooting wildlife from a motor vehicle or a boat propelled by a motor (85%). - the 10 ft boat with motor: no deer shot from it, ever (85%). - kayak: no motor, so the bc motorboat rule does not apply. ducks: see the duck screen. deer from a kayak: verify. tip: do not. roads - no shooting across a road (85%). - no hunting or shooting on the road allowance of a public road (80%). - road allowance: 15 m (16 yd) each side of the middle of a road with under 3 lanes. or 15 m from the pavement edge on 3 lanes or more. or to private or cultivated land, if closer (85%). - which roads count (for example a fsr , forest service road): verify in the synopsis. - safe habit: walk at least 15 m off the road, then load. tip. the 100 m rule !law no hunting or shooting within 100 m (109 yd) of a church, school building, school yard, playground, regional district park, or a dwelling house, farm or ranch building occupied by people or domestic animals (85%). - \"occupied\" includes animals in a barn (85%). - 100 m is close. a rifle bullet travels kilometres. use your rangefinder on buildings. tip. - owners can slaughter their own livestock near their buildings. that exemption is not for hunting (85%). - your brother's house on the 20 acre property counts. no shot within 100 m of it, even with his permission (80%, my reading: the only owner exemption is livestock slaughter). - barns or sheds with animals inside count too (85%). - field job: rangefind the house from each likely stand. mark a 100 m ring on your phone map. tip. no shooting areas and closed areas - a no shooting area: firing a firearm is banned, even outside hunting (85%). - they are on the regional maps in the synopsis (85%). - region 3 synopsis has kamloops area maps with no shooting and no hunting areas (80%). exact boundaries near heffley creek: verify. - some towns and regional districts add their own discharge bylaws. sun peaks and heffley creek: verify. - check the map before you go, not on the slope. parks and protected land place hunting? --- --- ecological reserve no. no hunting, no firing a firearm or bow (85%) bc park only in parks listed for hunting, only in open season (80%) bc park road no shooting within 400 m of the centre line of a park road (80%) wildlife management area rules differ by area. verify each one first nations reserve private land. permission from the band office to hunt on or cross it (85%) - park closed to hunting: you may not even carry a firearm there, unless a park officer allows it (80%). - check each park page on bcparks.ca before you go (80%). private land and the trespass act - hunting private land needs the owner's consent (85%). get it in writing . tip. - under the trespass act, \"enclosed land\" includes land with a lawful fence, a natural boundary, or signs (85%). - signs must be at each normal access point and readable in daylight (85%). - on enclosed land you are presumed to have no permission. you must prove you do (85%). - cultivated land (crops, hay, orchard): treat as no hunting without permission (70%). verify wording. - a \"no hunting\" sign may not be a trespass sign, but respect it anyway (65%, secondary). motor vehicle closures - motor vehicle closed areas (old name: access management areas) ban some or all vehicles, or only atvs or snowmobiles (85%). - motor vehicle hunting closed areas ban using a vehicle to hunt, carry hunters, gear or game (85%). - region 3 maps: gov.bc.ca/thompsonaccess (https://www.gov.bc.ca/thompsonaccess) (80%). - areas near heffley creek and sun peaks: verify on the map. - fire season closures and gates also apply. check bc wildfire service. tip. banned methods method rule --- --- rimfire on big game not allowed (80%) .22 rimfire on grouse or quail verify in synopsis firearm table lights or spotlights never hunt with a light (80%) night hunting banned outside legal hours (85%) drones no drone on a hunting trip. no drone info from under 6 hours before (85%) electronic calls banned, except wolf, coyote, cougar, lynx, bobcat and some listed birds (85%) dogs for deer leash only (80%). grouse and quail: unleashed dog ok (80%) feeding or baiting deer banned within 200 m of a home, school yard or playground in regions 3 and 8 (80%). bait for deer elsewhere: verify electronic firing mechanism not allowed (80%) ducks and geese (federal) - non toxic shot only for ducks, geese, coots and snipe. no lead (85%). - shotgun no larger than 10 gauge (85%). - holds 3 shells max , chamber plus magazine. plug must need the gun taken apart to remove (85%). - no hunting from a moving boat with a motor or sail (85%). - ok when the boat is beached, anchored or tied off (80%). - a motorboat may chase a wounded bird to retrieve it (85%). - possession limit: 3 times the daily limit, at all times, including at home (85%). - daily limits for 2026 to 2027: verify in the rules tab. ducks: the bird in your cooler - unpreserved birds keep one fully feathered wing or the fully feathered head attached (85%). - store birds so each can be counted and identified. no frozen block of ducks (80%). - \"preserved\" means fully processed for storage, for example frozen and plucked. exact definition: verify. - tip: leave a wing on until the duck is in your home freezer. deer: tag and evidence - notch the species licence right after the kill, before you move the animal (85%). see phase 1 session 13. - evidence of sex stays naturally attached until the meat is home, at a cutter, in cold storage, or inspected (75%, secondary). - buck: the part of the head with antlers, or testicles or penis plus the tail or a patch of hide (75%, secondary). - doe: the part of the head where antlers would be, or udder or teats plus the tail or a patch of hide (75%, secondary). - antler restricted season (like 4 point): keep antlers attached to part of the skull (75%). - exact hide patch size: verify. meat wastage - you must remove the edible portions (85%). - deer edible portions: neck, ribs, all four quarters and the loins (85%). - game birds (ducks, quail, grouse): both breasts (85%). - leave guts and bones at the kill site, or take them to a landfill (85%). - you may give the animal to someone who then takes the edible portions out (85%). cwd (chronic wasting disease) - cwd is confirmed in the kootenay region (85%). - cwd management zone: mu (management unit) 4-1 to 4-8 and 4-20 to 4-25 (85%). - in that zone: mandatory cwd test for every deer, elk and moose, within one week (85%). - no brain or spinal column (tail ok) taken out of that zone (85%). - region 3 and region 8 are not in the zone (85%, from the mu list). - bringing cervid (deer family) parts in from outside bc is restricted (65%, secondary). verify before any hunt outside bc. - deer urine lures from outside bc: may be banned (60%). verify. tip: use none. transporting firearms (federal) - unloaded while in a vehicle (85%). - non restricted rifle or shotgun: no case or trigger lock needed while you travel (85%). - leaving it in the vehicle: lock it in the trunk or a locked compartment. no trunk: out of sight in the locked vehicle (85%). - no special permit to carry a non restricted firearm. your pal (possession and acquisition licence) is enough (85%). - ammunition can travel with it, just not in it (80%). giving away meat, and helpers - anyone carrying your game without you needs a record of receipt (85%). - record of receipt shows: date and place, your name and address, who receives it, your fwid (fish and wildlife id), the species licence number, species and sex (85%). - your brother hunts too. he needs his own hunting licence, species licences and federal bird permit (85%). - each hunter notches only his own licence, for the animal he killed. shooting a deer for the other person's tag: verify. treat as not allowed. - ducks: each hunter has his own daily limit. do not pool birds. tip. - carrying his deer while he is not with you: you need his record of receipt (85%). - friends you give meat to at home: write a short record of receipt anyway. tip. - selling game meat: verify. treat as not allowed. wounded animal on closed or private land - the trespass act has no exception for tracking wounded game (70%, secondary). - posted or fenced land: ask the owner first. written permission is best (70%). - owner says no, or can't be found: call a conservation officer. tip. - animal ran into a park or no shooting area: do not fire there. call a conservation officer. tip. - you must still make a real effort to recover wounded game (70%). verify the exact rule. reporting poaching - rapp (report all poachers and polluters): 1 877 952 7277 (85%). - cell: 7277 (75%). - open 24 hours, 7 days. you can stay anonymous (85%). - note: plate, vehicle, place, time, what you saw. never confront. tip. !rule if you have to ask \"is this legal?\", the answer today is no. find out at home, then come back. !mistake - a full magazine in the rifle in the truck. that is loaded in bc. - a quick shot from the road or the atv seat. - freezing ducks with no wing or head attached. !field before each trip: open the synopsis map for your mu. mark no shooting areas, closures and buildings on your phone map. sources - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028, full pdf (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: loaded, vehicles, roads, 100 m, hours, banned methods, parks, edible portions, record of receipt. - region 3 thompson synopsis (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: kamloops maps, closures, baiting. - region 8 okanagan synopsis (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-8-okanagan.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: baiting. - hunting regulation, b.c. reg. 190/84 (https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hours. - trespass act (https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/consol21/consol21/00_96462_01) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - bc cwd page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/plants-animals-ecosystems/wildlife/wildlife-conservation/wildlife-health/chronic-wasting-disease/cwd-bc) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - bc rapp page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/natural-resource-stewardship/natural-resource-law-enforcement/conservation-officer-service/cos-rapp) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - bc hunting faq (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/frequently-asked-questions) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - migratory birds regulations, 2022 (https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/sor-2022-105/fulltext.html) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - eccc (environment and climate change canada) bc migratory bird summary (https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - rcmp (royal canadian mounted police): storing, transporting and displaying firearms (https://rcmp.ca/en/firearms/firearms-safety-training-transport-and-storage/storing-transporting-and-displaying-firearms) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - storage, display, transportation and handling of firearms by individuals regulations, sor/98-209 (https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/sor-98-209/) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - secondary, 60 to 79% max: bc firearms academy, evidence of sex (https://bcfirearmsacademy.ca/possession-transportation-big-game/); huntingbc forum, wounded game on private land (https://www.huntingbc.ca/forum/archive/index.php/t-92535.html); cwd info.org bc page (https://cwd-info.org/regulations/british-columbia-cwd-regulations/); agcanada, attractant ban (https://www.agcanada.com/daily/b-c-to-block-hunting-attractants-from-out-of-province). checked 2026-10-01. "},{"id":"wind-thermals","phase":2,"num":10,"title":"Wind and thermals","minutes":10,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Wind and thermals","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>A deer that smells you is gone before you see it. You cannot hide scent, but you can put it where deer are not.</p></aside>"},{"title":"How scent moves","html":"<ul><li>Your scent drifts with the air, like smoke from a campfire.</li><li>Deer rely heavily on smell to detect danger <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Deer often travel with the wind at their back or side so they can smell behind and look ahead (60%, hunter observation).</li><li>Steady wind is your friend. Shifting, light wind is the danger.</li><li>Rule of thumb: keep the deer <strong>upwind</strong> of you. The wind blows from the deer to you.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Thermals: morning up, evening down","html":"<ul><li>Thermals are air currents made by the ground warming and cooling <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Morning:</strong> the sun warms slopes and air rises <strong>uphill</strong> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Evening:</strong> slopes cool and air sinks <strong>downhill</strong>, into draws and valley floors <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Shaded north slopes and timber stay cool longer, so the switch comes later there (Tip).</li><li>On calm days, thermals beat the light breeze. In strong wind, the wind wins (Tip).</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 520\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"th-arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"520\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Morning: sun warms slope, air rises</text>\n  <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"62\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#e8590c\" fill-opacity=\"0.6\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M10,240 L120,200 L260,90 L300,80 L390,150 L390,240 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M90,190 L150,160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M160,148 L215,108\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M225,96 L262,68\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"275\" cy=\"76\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">You</text>\n  <ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"212\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"62\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deer</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Be ABOVE. Scent goes up, away.</text>\n  <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"262\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Evening: slope cools, air sinks</text>\n  <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"322\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M10,500 L120,460 L260,350 L300,340 L390,410 L390,500 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M245,352 L195,390\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M185,400 L135,437\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M120,450 L70,470\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <ellipse cx=\"270\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"320\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deer</text>\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"470\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"448\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">You</text>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"485\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Be BELOW. Scent sinks, away.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure>"},{"title":"Swirls and eddies","html":"<ul><li>Wind swirls in draws, saddles, valleys and thick timber, where air bounces off terrain <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Swirl also happens around the switch time, mid morning and near sunset, as thermals change direction (Tip).</li><li>Lakes and creeks: cold air often slides down creek bottoms in the evening (Tip).</li><li>In a swirl, no setup is safe. Back out and come back with steadier air.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to check the wind","html":"<ul><li><strong>Puffer bottle:</strong> a squeeze bottle of unscented powder. One puff shows drift and direction <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Milkweed fluff:</strong> floats far and shows thermals and swirls over terrain <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Lighter flame:</strong> quick check, shows only the air right at your hand (Tip). Mind fire risk in dry grass.</li><li><strong>Thread</strong> on your bow or rifle sling: always on, no hands (Tip).</li><li>Check every few minutes and at every new terrain feature, not just at the truck.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Milkweed is the best teacher. Watch one tuft for 30 seconds. You will see it climb, stall and curl back on itself.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Planning your approach","html":"<ol><li>Check the forecast wind direction and speed the night before.</li><li>On the map, mark likely bedding and feeding areas.</li><li>Mornings: come in from <strong>above</strong> or from the side so rising air carries scent up and away.</li><li>Evenings: sit <strong>below</strong> or to the side so sinking air carries scent down and away.</li><li>Plan an A and B spot for each wind. Wrong wind on the day? Go to B, do not force A.</li><li>Walk in with the wind in your face or across you, never at your back toward the deer.</li></ol><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): Hunt the wind you have, not the wind you wanted.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Checking wind once at the truck, then hunting a valley where it swirls.</li><li>Sitting on a morning ridge above deer that are bedded higher up, so rising air takes your scent right to them.</li><li>Ignoring the evening switch and staying at a midday spot as air begins to sink.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>On a scouting day, stand mid slope at 7 am, 12 noon and 5 pm. Puff the bottle or release milkweed each time. Note which way it goes in your journal.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.themeateater.com/wired-to-hunt/whitetail-hunting/understanding-thermals-in-whitetail-country\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MeatEater: Understanding thermals in whitetail country</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://realtree.com/articles/big-game-hunting/how-to-read-and-understand-mountain-wind\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Realtree: How to read and understand mountain wind</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://mossyoakgamekeeper.com/wildlife-management/whitetail-deer/deer-hunting-in-wind-and-thermals/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mossy Oak Gamekeeper: Deer hunting in wind and thermals</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"http://www.buckmasters.com/Hunting/Tips-and-Tactics/Articles/ID/543/Natural-Wind-Detector\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Buckmasters: Natural wind detector (milkweed)</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li>Hunt Mentor deer research notes, research/2026-10-01-deer.md, thermals line <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></li><li>Note: thermals are basic physics. Hunting use is hunter experience, labelled Tip.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which way do thermals usually move on a sunny morning?","options":["Downhill","Uphill","They do not move"],"answer":1,"why":"The sun warms the slope and the warm air rises uphill."},{"q":"Evening hunt on a calm day. Where do you set up?","options":["Above the deer","Below or beside the deer","Directly downhill in the deer's path, wind at your back"],"answer":1,"why":"Evening air sinks downhill. Sitting below or to the side means your scent sinks away from the deer."},{"q":"Your milkweed drifts left, then curls back to you. What is happening?","options":["Steady wind","A swirl. Your scent is going everywhere","Thermals stopped"],"answer":1,"why":"Curling and doubling back is a swirl. No setup is safe in a swirl."},{"q":"Wind on hunt day is wrong for your planned spot. Best move?","options":["Hunt it anyway","Go to your B spot that fits this wind","Add cover scent"],"answer":1,"why":"Plan an A and B spot for each wind. Forcing a bad wind educates deer."}],"checklists":[],"text":" !why a deer that smells you is gone before you see it. you cannot hide scent, but you can put it where deer are not. how scent moves - your scent drifts with the air, like smoke from a campfire. - deer rely heavily on smell to detect danger (70%). - deer often travel with the wind at their back or side so they can smell behind and look ahead (60%, hunter observation). - steady wind is your friend. shifting, light wind is the danger. - rule of thumb: keep the deer upwind of you. the wind blows from the deer to you. thermals: morning up, evening down - thermals are air currents made by the ground warming and cooling (75%). - morning: the sun warms slopes and air rises uphill (75%). - evening: slopes cool and air sinks downhill , into draws and valley floors (75%). - shaded north slopes and timber stay cool longer, so the switch comes later there (tip). - on calm days, thermals beat the light breeze. in strong wind, the wind wins (tip). swirls and eddies - wind swirls in draws, saddles, valleys and thick timber, where air bounces off terrain (70%). - swirl also happens around the switch time, mid morning and near sunset, as thermals change direction (tip). - lakes and creeks: cold air often slides down creek bottoms in the evening (tip). - in a swirl, no setup is safe. back out and come back with steadier air. how to check the wind - puffer bottle: a squeeze bottle of unscented powder. one puff shows drift and direction (70%). - milkweed fluff: floats far and shows thermals and swirls over terrain (70%). - lighter flame: quick check, shows only the air right at your hand (tip). mind fire risk in dry grass. - thread on your bow or rifle sling: always on, no hands (tip). - check every few minutes and at every new terrain feature, not just at the truck. !tip milkweed is the best teacher. watch one tuft for 30 seconds. you will see it climb, stall and curl back on itself. planning your approach 1. check the forecast wind direction and speed the night before. 2. on the map, mark likely bedding and feeding areas. 3. mornings: come in from above or from the side so rising air carries scent up and away. 4. evenings: sit below or to the side so sinking air carries scent down and away. 5. plan an a and b spot for each wind. wrong wind on the day? go to b, do not force a. 6. walk in with the wind in your face or across you, never at your back toward the deer. !rule grandpa's rule (wisdom): hunt the wind you have, not the wind you wanted. !mistake - checking wind once at the truck, then hunting a valley where it swirls. - sitting on a morning ridge above deer that are bedded higher up, so rising air takes your scent right to them. - ignoring the evening switch and staying at a midday spot as air begins to sink. !field on a scouting day, stand mid slope at 7 am, 12 noon and 5 pm. puff the bottle or release milkweed each time. note which way it goes in your journal. sources - meateater: understanding thermals in whitetail country (https://www.themeateater.com/wired-to-hunt/whitetail-hunting/understanding-thermals-in-whitetail-country), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - realtree: how to read and understand mountain wind (https://realtree.com/articles/big-game-hunting/how-to-read-and-understand-mountain-wind), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - mossy oak gamekeeper: deer hunting in wind and thermals (https://mossyoakgamekeeper.com/wildlife-management/whitetail-deer/deer-hunting-in-wind-and-thermals/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - buckmasters: natural wind detector (milkweed) (http://www.buckmasters.com/hunting/tips-and-tactics/articles/id/543/natural-wind-detector), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - hunt mentor deer research notes, research/2026-10-01-deer.md, thermals line (80%) - note: thermals are basic physics. hunting use is hunter experience, labelled tip. "},{"id":"glassing","phase":2,"num":11,"title":"Glassing","minutes":10,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Glassing","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>In open Interior country you find more deer with your eyes than with your boots. <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Glassing\">Glassing</button> lets you see them before they see you.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Binocular technique","html":"<ul><li>Set the eye cups and the right eye dial (diopter) once at home. Mark the setting.</li><li>Brace. Elbows on knees, back on a tree or pack. Shaking binoculars hide deer.</li><li>Hold still and let the view settle. Move the binoculars, not your eyes inside them (Tip).</li><li>Look through the binoculars for a long time, rest your eyes for a few seconds, then go back (Tip).</li><li>Use a hat brim or hand to cut glare when the sun is low in front of you.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Tripods","html":"<ul><li>A tripod steadies the binoculars and stops arm fatigue on long sessions <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Many western hunters rate a tripod as a bigger upgrade than better glass (60%, hunter opinion).</li><li>A light tripod and a binocular adapter is enough to start.</li><li>Steady glass shows small movement: a tail flick at 800 m (875 yd) you would miss handheld (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Start with the binoculars you have plus a cheap tripod adapter. Upgrade glass later, once you know what you use.</p></aside>"},{"title":"The grid method","html":"<ol><li>Pick a spot with a wide view and the wind right (see Wind and thermals).</li><li>Quick scan first: open slopes, cut blocks, edges where deer are easy to see <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Then split the view into a grid in your head <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Start at one corner. Glass one strip slowly, left to right <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Overlap each binocular view by about a third so nothing falls in a gap <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Drop down one strip and come back right to left <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Then glass it again. Deer stand up, move and turn as light changes.</li></ol>"},{"title":"What to look for","html":"<ul><li>Do not look for a whole deer. Look for <strong>parts</strong> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>A <strong>horizontal line</strong> in a world of vertical trees: a back or belly <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li><strong>Vertical legs</strong> under a shape that does not belong <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>An <strong>ear flick</strong> or tail twitch. Movement is the giveaway.</li><li>The <strong>white rump</strong> of a mule deer, or the flash of a white tail (Tip).</li><li>A shape that is the wrong colour: grey brown body against green or yellow.</li><li>Antler tips catching light, or a shiny black nose.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Best times and places","html":"<ul><li>Deer are most active at dawn and dusk <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Glass first light and the last hour before legal shooting light ends (Tip). Legal shooting hours: see the synopsis. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> times for each date.</li><li>Midday: glass shade, timber edges and benches where deer bed (Tip).</li><li>Glass from above, with the sun behind you, so deer are lit and you are not (Tip).</li><li>Near Sun Peaks: cut blocks, burns, open grass slopes and timber edges (Tip, check on your map).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): Sit longer than you think. When you want to move, glass it one more time.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Sweeping the binoculars fast across a hillside, like a camera pan.</li><li>Looking only for a whole standing deer in the open.</li><li>Glassing straight into a rising or setting sun.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Pick a hillside. Set a 20 minute timer. Glass it with the grid twice. Write down every thing you thought might be a deer, then check it.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.gohunt.com/browse/tips-and-tricks/glassing/the-three-pillars-of-glassing-and-how-they-will-help-you-find-more-big-game\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">goHUNT: The three pillars of glassing</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.themeateater.com/hunt/general/what-you-need-to-know-about-glassing-terrain\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MeatEater: What you need to know about glassing terrain</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.outdoorbenchmark.com/big-game-hunting/glassing-strategy-guide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Outdoor Benchmark: Glassing for big game</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.wideopenspaces.com/heres-glass-mule-deer-increase-odds-filling-tag/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wide Open Spaces: How to glass for mule deer</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.sitkagear.com/experience/what-colors-can-deer-see\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sitka: What colours can deer see (crepuscular activity)</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li>Hunt Mentor deer research notes, research/2026-10-01-deer.md (60%, hunter consensus)</li><li>Note: glassing is a skill, not a rule. Most points are hunter experience, labelled Tip.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What should you look for when glassing?","options":["A whole deer standing in the open","Parts: a horizontal back, legs, an ear flick, a white rump","Only antlers"],"answer":1,"why":"Deer are usually partly hidden. Look for pieces and movement that do not belong."},{"q":"Why use a tripod?","options":["It looks pro","Steady glass and less fatigue, so you see small movement","It is required"],"answer":1,"why":"A steady view shows a tail flick or ear twitch you miss with shaky hands."},{"q":"How do you glass a hillside with the grid method?","options":["Fast sweeps back and forth","Slow strips with overlap, corner to corner, then repeat","Only the middle"],"answer":1,"why":"Slow overlapping strips leave no gaps. Glass it again because deer move and light changes."},{"q":"When are deer most active?","options":["Midday","Dawn and dusk","Midnight only"],"answer":1,"why":"Deer are most active around dawn and dusk. Be glassing at first light."}],"checklists":[],"text":" !why in open interior country you find more deer with your eyes than with your boots. glassing lets you see them before they see you. binocular technique - set the eye cups and the right eye dial (diopter) once at home. mark the setting. - brace. elbows on knees, back on a tree or pack. shaking binoculars hide deer. - hold still and let the view settle. move the binoculars, not your eyes inside them (tip). - look through the binoculars for a long time, rest your eyes for a few seconds, then go back (tip). - use a hat brim or hand to cut glare when the sun is low in front of you. tripods - a tripod steadies the binoculars and stops arm fatigue on long sessions (65%). - many western hunters rate a tripod as a bigger upgrade than better glass (60%, hunter opinion). - a light tripod and a binocular adapter is enough to start. - steady glass shows small movement: a tail flick at 800 m (875 yd) you would miss handheld (tip). !lean start with the binoculars you have plus a cheap tripod adapter. upgrade glass later, once you know what you use. the grid method 1. pick a spot with a wide view and the wind right (see wind and thermals). 2. quick scan first: open slopes, cut blocks, edges where deer are easy to see (65%). 3. then split the view into a grid in your head (65%). 4. start at one corner. glass one strip slowly, left to right (65%). 5. overlap each binocular view by about a third so nothing falls in a gap (60%). 6. drop down one strip and come back right to left (65%). 7. then glass it again. deer stand up, move and turn as light changes. what to look for - do not look for a whole deer. look for parts (65%). - a horizontal line in a world of vertical trees: a back or belly (65%). - vertical legs under a shape that does not belong (65%). - an ear flick or tail twitch. movement is the giveaway. - the white rump of a mule deer, or the flash of a white tail (tip). - a shape that is the wrong colour: grey brown body against green or yellow. - antler tips catching light, or a shiny black nose. best times and places - deer are most active at dawn and dusk (70%). - glass first light and the last hour before legal shooting light ends (tip). legal shooting hours: see the synopsis. verify times for each date. - midday: glass shade, timber edges and benches where deer bed (tip). - glass from above, with the sun behind you, so deer are lit and you are not (tip). - near sun peaks: cut blocks, burns, open grass slopes and timber edges (tip, check on your map). !rule grandpa's rule (wisdom): sit longer than you think. when you want to move, glass it one more time. !mistake - sweeping the binoculars fast across a hillside, like a camera pan. - looking only for a whole standing deer in the open. - glassing straight into a rising or setting sun. !field pick a hillside. set a 20 minute timer. glass it with the grid twice. write down every thing you thought might be a deer, then check it. sources - gohunt: the three pillars of glassing (https://www.gohunt.com/browse/tips-and-tricks/glassing/the-three-pillars-of-glassing-and-how-they-will-help-you-find-more-big-game), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - meateater: what you need to know about glassing terrain (https://www.themeateater.com/hunt/general/what-you-need-to-know-about-glassing-terrain), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - outdoor benchmark: glassing for big game (https://www.outdoorbenchmark.com/big-game-hunting/glassing-strategy-guide), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - wide open spaces: how to glass for mule deer (https://www.wideopenspaces.com/heres-glass-mule-deer-increase-odds-filling-tag/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - sitka: what colours can deer see (crepuscular activity) (https://www.sitkagear.com/experience/what-colors-can-deer-see), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - hunt mentor deer research notes, research/2026-10-01-deer.md (60%, hunter consensus) - note: glassing is a skill, not a rule. most points are hunter experience, labelled tip. "},{"id":"still-hunting","phase":2,"num":12,"title":"Moving quietly","minutes":10,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Moving quietly","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Most beginners walk through the bush at a hiking pace. Deer hear and see them long before. Slow down and the woods change.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Still hunting vs stalking","html":"<ul><li><strong>Still hunting:</strong> walking very slowly through deer cover, hoping to see a deer before it sees you <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Stalking:</strong> you have already seen the deer. You close the distance for a shot <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Still hunting suits thick timber and wet, quiet days.</li><li>Stalking suits open country after <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Glassing\">Glassing</button>.</li><li>Both depend on wind. Read the wind session first.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Pace","html":"<aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Move 2 or 3 steps, then stand and look for 20 seconds or more. Some hunters stop for minutes (Tip, hunter method).</p></aside><ul><li>Look much more than you walk <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Expect to cover very little ground: a few hundred metres an hour is normal (Tip).</li><li>Look near as well as far. A bedded deer may be very close <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Before each step, pick where your foot goes next.</li><li>Heel down softly, roll to the ball, test before putting weight on it <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Using cover","html":"<ul><li>Move when the deer's head is down feeding or behind a tree (Tip).</li><li>Stay in shade. Avoid the skyline on ridges.</li><li>Keep a tree or bush between you and where deer may be, then look around it, not over it (Tip).</li><li>Move from one piece of cover to the next. Pause at each.</li><li>Freeze when a deer looks up. Wait until it relaxes (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Noise: what deer hear best","html":"<ul><li>White tailed deer hear about 0.25 to 30 kilohertz in one study, best at 4 to 8 kilohertz <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>People hear up to about 20 kilohertz. Deer hear higher pitch than we do <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>A second study found deer can hear even higher, past 50 kilohertz <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Sharp, high sounds are the giveaway: zippers, Velcro, nylon scrape, metal on metal (Tip).</li><li>A snapped stick can sound like a deer. A steady crunch, crunch rhythm sounds like a person (Tip).</li><li>Mule deer hearing studies: none found. Assumption: similar.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Clothing and kit noise","html":"<ul><li>Wool and brushed fleece are quieter in brush than hard nylon shells (Tip).</li><li>Tape or wrap metal parts that clink: sling swivels, buckles, knife sheath (Tip).</li><li>Swap Velcro pockets for buttons or magnets, or leave them open.</li><li>Before you leave the truck, jump up and down. Fix anything that rattles.</li><li>Keep the gun's action and safety quiet. Practise moving the safety slowly at home.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Grandpa's rule (wisdom): If you think you are going slow enough, halve it.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Walking at hiking pace and calling it still hunting.</li><li>Only looking far ahead and walking past a bedded deer at 20 m (22 yd).</li><li>A swishing nylon jacket and a Velcro pocket opened next to a feeding deer.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>On your scouting day, still hunt 200 m (219 yd) of timber and time it. Aim for 30 minutes or more. Your brother walks the same line behind you and tells you every noise he hears.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2193/2006-326\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">D'Angelo and others, 2007, Hearing range of white tailed deer by auditory brainstem response, Journal of Wildlife Management</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview of abstract)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/42438287_The_behavioral_audiogram_of_whitetail_deer_Odocoileus_virginianus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Heffner and Heffner, behavioral audiogram of whitetail deer (ResearchGate)</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://fieldandstream.com/stories/hunting/still-hunting\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Field and Stream: Mastering still hunting</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://nadeerhunter.com/7-steps-for-still-hunting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">North American Deer Hunter: 7 steps for still hunting</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li><a href=\"https://prohunt.com/articles/still-hunting-techniques-guide/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ProHunt: Still hunting techniques guide</a>, checked 2026-10-01 (search preview)</li><li>Note: pace and cover advice is hunter experience, labelled Tip. Hearing figures are from peer reviewed studies.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between still hunting and stalking?","options":["None","Still hunting is slow walking to find a deer. Stalking is closing in on a deer you have seen","Stalking is done from a blind"],"answer":1,"why":"Still hunting searches. Stalking closes the gap on a deer you already spotted."},{"q":"Which sound most likely spooks a deer?","options":["A low rumble far away","A zipper or Velcro rip close by","Wind in the trees"],"answer":1,"why":"Deer hear high pitch well, better than people. Zippers, Velcro and metal clinks are sharp high sounds."},{"q":"A deer lifts its head and stares your way. What do you do?","options":["Duck down fast","Freeze until it relaxes","Wave to make it move"],"answer":1,"why":"Deer pick up movement. Freezing gives it nothing to react to."},{"q":"A good still hunting pace looks like?","options":["Steady hiking walk","A couple of steps, then a long stop to look","Jogging between trees"],"answer":1,"why":"Look much more than you walk. Short moves, long looks."}],"checklists":[],"text":" !why most beginners walk through the bush at a hiking pace. deer hear and see them long before. slow down and the woods change. still hunting vs stalking - still hunting: walking very slowly through deer cover, hoping to see a deer before it sees you (70%). - stalking: you have already seen the deer. you close the distance for a shot (70%). - still hunting suits thick timber and wet, quiet days. - stalking suits open country after glassing . - both depend on wind. read the wind session first. pace !tip move 2 or 3 steps, then stand and look for 20 seconds or more. some hunters stop for minutes (tip, hunter method). - look much more than you walk (65%). - expect to cover very little ground: a few hundred metres an hour is normal (tip). - look near as well as far. a bedded deer may be very close (65%). - before each step, pick where your foot goes next. - heel down softly, roll to the ball, test before putting weight on it (65%). using cover - move when the deer's head is down feeding or behind a tree (tip). - stay in shade. avoid the skyline on ridges. - keep a tree or bush between you and where deer may be, then look around it, not over it (tip). - move from one piece of cover to the next. pause at each. - freeze when a deer looks up. wait until it relaxes (tip). noise: what deer hear best - white tailed deer hear about 0.25 to 30 kilohertz in one study, best at 4 to 8 kilohertz (75%). - people hear up to about 20 kilohertz. deer hear higher pitch than we do (75%). - a second study found deer can hear even higher, past 50 kilohertz (65%). - sharp, high sounds are the giveaway: zippers, velcro, nylon scrape, metal on metal (tip). - a snapped stick can sound like a deer. a steady crunch, crunch rhythm sounds like a person (tip). - mule deer hearing studies: none found. assumption: similar. clothing and kit noise - wool and brushed fleece are quieter in brush than hard nylon shells (tip). - tape or wrap metal parts that clink: sling swivels, buckles, knife sheath (tip). - swap velcro pockets for buttons or magnets, or leave them open. - before you leave the truck, jump up and down. fix anything that rattles. - keep the gun's action and safety quiet. practise moving the safety slowly at home. !rule grandpa's rule (wisdom): if you think you are going slow enough, halve it. !mistake - walking at hiking pace and calling it still hunting. - only looking far ahead and walking past a bedded deer at 20 m (22 yd). - a swishing nylon jacket and a velcro pocket opened next to a feeding deer. !field on your scouting day, still hunt 200 m (219 yd) of timber and time it. aim for 30 minutes or more. your brother walks the same line behind you and tells you every noise he hears. sources - d'angelo and others, 2007, hearing range of white tailed deer by auditory brainstem response, journal of wildlife management (https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2193/2006-326), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview of abstract) - heffner and heffner, behavioral audiogram of whitetail deer (researchgate) (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/42438287_the_behavioral_audiogram_of_whitetail_deer_odocoileus_virginianus), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - field and stream: mastering still hunting (https://fieldandstream.com/stories/hunting/still-hunting), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - north american deer hunter: 7 steps for still hunting (https://nadeerhunter.com/7-steps-for-still-hunting/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - prohunt: still hunting techniques guide (https://prohunt.com/articles/still-hunting-techniques-guide/), checked 2026-10-01 (search preview) - note: pace and cover advice is hunter experience, labelled tip. hearing figures are from peer reviewed studies. "},{"id":"mule-deer","phase":4,"num":1,"title":"Mule deer","minutes":20,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"Mule deer","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>The mule deer is the deer of the hills around Heffley Creek. Learn its day and you will know where to sit at first light.</p></aside><ul><li>Mule deer are the main deer of the southern BC Interior <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Your first hunt is mid to late October. That is the <strong>pre rut</strong>: bucks start to roam, the big breeding push is still weeks away <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>This session: how to tell one, where it is hour by hour, what it eats, how it hides, and how you hunt it.</li><li>The deep dive on senses and alarm is in <a href=\"#/s/deer-mind\">How a deer thinks</a>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Identify: buck, doe, fawn","html":"<ul><li><strong>Ears:</strong> very big, like a mule. That is the name <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Antlers (buck):</strong> forks that fork again, like a \"Y\" on a \"Y\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Tail:</strong> thin, white, rope like, with a <strong>black tip</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Rump:</strong> big white patch around the tail <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Doe:</strong> same body, no antlers, smaller neck <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Fawn:</strong> born in early summer; by October it has lost its spots and is about half the doe's size <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Metatarsal gland</strong> (scent patch on the outside of the hind leg): long, 10 to 15 cm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>A buck in October has a thick neck. A doe has a thin neck and a long, narrow face. If you can't see antlers clearly, you have not identified a buck.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Mule deer or white tail?","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 420\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"420\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"410\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 6\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mule deer</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">White tail</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Antlers (one side, seen from front)</text>\n  <!-- mule deer antler: forks that split again -->\n  <g stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M100 190 L100 160\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 160 L70 110\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 160 L130 110\"/>\n    <path d=\"M70 110 L55 72\"/>\n    <path d=\"M70 110 L85 75\"/>\n    <path d=\"M130 110 L118 74\"/>\n    <path d=\"M130 110 L148 76\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 178 L86 168\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Forks that fork again</text>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(\"Y\" on a \"Y\")</text>\n  <!-- whitetail antler: one main beam, tines up -->\n  <g stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M250 190 Q240 120 300 100 Q345 90 360 120\"/>\n    <path d=\"M262 140 L258 112\"/>\n    <path d=\"M285 108 L282 72\"/>\n    <path d=\"M315 98 L318 64\"/>\n    <path d=\"M343 102 L350 76\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">One main beam,</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">single tines up</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Tail and rump (seen from behind)</text>\n  <!-- mule deer rump -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"330\" rx=\"48\" ry=\"48\" fill=\"#f3ead8\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"94\" y=\"290\" width=\"12\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3ead8\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"94\" y=\"340\" width=\"12\" height=\"18\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#23261f\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"396\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Big white rump,</text>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"413\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">thin tail, black tip</text>\n  <!-- whitetail flag -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"300\" cy=\"345\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"34\" fill=\"#b08a5e\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M300 340 C270 320 268 285 285 275 L300 270 L315 275 C332 285 330 320 300 340 Z\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"300\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">flag up</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"396\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wide brown tail,</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"413\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">white below, waved up</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Mule deer</th><th>White tail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Antlers</td><td>Forks that fork again</td><td>One main beam, single tines up</td></tr><tr><td>Tail</td><td>Thin, white, black tip</td><td>Wide, brown on top, white under</td></tr><tr><td>Running away</td><td><strong>Stots</strong>: all 4 feet hit and push together, bouncing <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Gallops, tail up like a white <strong>flag</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ears</td><td>Very big</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Leg gland</td><td>Long, 10 to 15 cm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Small <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Both deer live near Heffley Creek. They are <strong>separate tags</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Hybrids exist but are uncommon <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. When in doubt, do not shoot (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"A day in the life: late October","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A deer day, late October</text>\n  <!-- night shading -->\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"50\" width=\"99\" height=\"160\" fill=\"#e6e1d3\"/>\n  <rect x=\"285\" y=\"50\" width=\"85\" height=\"160\" fill=\"#e6e1d3\"/>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">dark</text>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">dark</text>\n  <!-- activity curve -->\n  <path d=\"M30 165 C70 160 95 72 125 72 C150 72 160 178 200 180 C240 178 255 72 290 72 C320 72 335 160 370 165\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n  <line x1=\"30\" y1=\"210\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"210\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <!-- ticks: x = 30 + hour*14.17 -->\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <text x=\"30\" y=\"230\">0</text>\n    <text x=\"115\" y=\"230\">6</text>\n    <text x=\"200\" y=\"230\">12</text>\n    <text x=\"285\" y=\"230\">18</text>\n    <text x=\"370\" y=\"230\">24</text>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"248\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">hour of day (24 hour clock)</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">PEAK</text>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">PEAK</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"203\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">bedded, chewing cud</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"272\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Dawn: feed, then drift to beds</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Dusk: rise, move, feed into the night</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><strong>Night (about 21:00 to 04:00):</strong> feeds on and off in open grass and brush, rests between bouts <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Before first light (04:00 to 07:00):</strong> a strong activity peak. Feeding, moving <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Legal light starts:</strong> 1 hour before sunrise <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Mid to late October sunrise near Kamloops is about 07:30 to 07:50 (calculated) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Use the Legal light screen in Field Mode.</li><li><strong>Sunrise to about 09:00:</strong> last feeding, then drifts uphill toward cover <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Midday (10:00 to 15:00):</strong> bedded. Chews cud. Gets up briefly to stretch, nibble, and re bed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Late afternoon (16:00 to 18:00):</strong> rises, browses near the bed, then moves toward open feeding slopes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Sunset (about 17:40 to 18:00) to 21:00:</strong> second peak. Out in the open <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Peaks from a telemetry study: about 04:00 to 09:00 and 17:00 to 21:00 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Be on your glassing spot 45 minutes before legal light. The first and last hour are when you will see deer.</p></aside>"},{"title":"How it eats, poops and sleeps","html":"<ul><li><strong>Eats:</strong> a browser. Twigs, leaves and buds of shrubs, plus forbs (small leafy plants), with little grass in fall <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Chews cud:</strong> a ruminant. It fills up fast, then lies down to chew again <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Poops:</strong> piles of oval pellets, about 1 to 1.5 cm long. Firm and dry in fall and winter <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>How often:</strong> roughly 13 to 34 pellet groups a day <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Sleeps:</strong> in short naps, often with head up or curled, waking often <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Walks:</strong> slow, stopping to look and listen. Often walks the same trails each day <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Drinks:</strong> in dry fall weather, near water at dusk or dawn. With green feed and dew, it needs less (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Food by season near Kamloops","html":"<ul><li>Study of deer stomachs near Kamloops, September to April <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>:<ul><li>Grass was under 10% of the diet until spring.</li><li>More forbs in fall.</li><li>Shrubs important in early fall and late winter.</li><li>Douglas fir needles peak in December and January.</li></ul></li><li>Key browse: Douglas fir, saskatoon, Oregon grape, red osier dogwood, willow, rose, bitterbrush, rabbitbrush, sagebrush, snowbrush <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Late October menu:</strong> last green forbs, shrub leaves and twigs, fresh green grass shoots after fall rain <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Learn 3 plants this week: saskatoon, red osier dogwood (red stems) and Oregon grape (holly like leaves). Find them chewed and you are in a feeding area.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Where they are: BC and near Heffley Creek","html":"<ul><li>Mule deer like steeper, higher, open, broken ground. White tails like valley bottoms and farm fields <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Local reading <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>:<ul><li>Grassland and open Douglas fir benches above the valley.</li><li>Up toward Heffley Lake, Paul Lake and Sun Peaks in summer and early fall.</li><li>Lower, sunnier slopes as snow comes.</li></ul></li><li>Winter range: south and west facing slopes with old Douglas fir <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>A study of winter range north of Kamloops used slopes below about 760 m <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Rough elevations (Tip, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on a map): valley 370 to 450 m, Heffley Lake about 950 m, Sun Peaks village about 1,250 m.</li><li>Ungulate winter range maps: view the \"Ungulate Winter Range, Approved\" layer in iMapBC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Near Kamloops there may be no approved mule deer areas (<span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>).</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 340\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"340\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Where deer are on a slope</text>\n  <!-- ground profile -->\n  <path d=\"M0 300 L110 300 L180 230 L215 222 L250 175 L285 168 L340 80 L400 70 L400 340 L0 340 Z\" fill=\"#d9cfb3\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- trees on bench and upper slope -->\n  <g fill=\"#2f3a2b\">\n    <path d=\"M255 172 L262 145 L269 172 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M272 170 L279 140 L286 170 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M300 140 L307 112 L314 140 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M320 108 L327 80 L334 108 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M352 78 L359 52 L366 78 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <!-- field -->\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"292\" width=\"95\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <!-- deer dots -->\n  <circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"285\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"285\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"148\" cy=\"254\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"265\" cy=\"160\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"345\" cy=\"70\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <!-- labels -->\n  <text x=\"55\" y=\"322\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Valley fields:</text>\n  <text x=\"55\" y=\"338\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">white tails</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Open grass:</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"306\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">feeding</text>\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bench + timber:</text>\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">day beds</text>\n  <text x=\"345\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ridge</text>\n  <!-- thermals -->\n  <g stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\">\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <path d=\"M110 80 L98 82 L106 92 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\">Morning warms:</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">air flows UP</text>\n  <g stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">\n    <line x1=\"230\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"100\"/>\n    <path d=\"M180 100 L192 98 L184 88 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Evening cools:</text>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">air flows DOWN</text>\n</svg>\n</figure>"},{"title":"Migration: the big October question","html":"<ul><li>About 74% of tracked mule deer in a southern Interior study migrated <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Average trip about 47 to 55 km one way, up to about 100 km <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>They use the <strong>same route every year</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>What starts the move down: <strong>snow and cold</strong>, not the rut <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Timing: back on winter range October to November; in one year nearly all were back by mid October <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Another BC account: interior deer often stay high until December <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Deer avoid snow deeper than about 50 cm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Early snow up high in October? Hunt the mid slopes below the snow line. No snow yet? Glass the high benches and openings near Heffley Lake and Paul Lake first.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Senses: what scares and attracts them","html":"<ul><li><strong>Nose:</strong> strongest sense. Wind at your back toward the deer ends the hunt <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Ears:</strong> hear about 115 Hz (hertz) to 54 kHz (kilohertz), best at 4 to 8 kHz <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. That is the range of zippers, Velcro, nylon swish and metal clicks.</li><li><strong>Eyes:</strong> see blues well, no red cone, so blaze orange looks dull to them <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. They catch <strong>motion and outline</strong> fast <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Scares them:</strong> human scent, a skyline outline, sudden moves, metal clicks, voices, a slammed truck door (Tip).</li><li><strong>Calms them:</strong> slow natural walking rhythm, other relaxed deer, steady breeze (Tip).</li><li><strong>Attracts them:</strong> fresh green feed, does in heat (in the rut), other deer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Lights: a headlamp beam swinging across a slope will move deer. Use red light and keep it on the ground (Tip).</li><li>Full story: <a href=\"#/s/deer-mind\">How a deer thinks</a>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Social life and the rut","html":"<ul><li>Does live in family groups: mother, daughters and fawns <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Bucks spend summer in <strong>bachelor groups</strong> of 2 to 4. These break up in October <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Groups are mostly 5 or fewer. In winter 5 to 8, up to 15 to 30 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Deer return to the same home range year after year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Rut</strong> (breeding season) for mule deer: mid November to mid December, peak mid November <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Late October:</strong> bucks spar (push antlers, not real fights), rub small trees, and start checking doe groups <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>In the rut:</strong> a buck tends one doe at a time, follows her, and fights rivals <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Bucks get careless in daylight.</li><li>Antlers fall off in winter and regrow each year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Beds and bedding","html":"<ul><li>Day beds give <strong>hiding cover</strong> and <strong>shelter</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Warm days: north facing timber, benches, draws, shade <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Cold days: sunny south and west slopes, out of the wind <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Lore: bucks bed in the top third of a slope, looking down, with wind from behind (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>A bed is an oval of flattened grass or scraped earth, about 1 m long, often with pellets and hair nearby <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Deer bed where they can see danger below and smell danger above (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Tracks, scat and sign","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"320\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer track (front foot)</text>\n  <!-- two toes (halves of heart), point = toe tips at top -->\n  <path d=\"M195 70 C180 75 160 110 158 160 C156 200 168 225 185 228 C195 229 197 215 197 200 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M205 70 C220 75 240 110 242 160 C244 200 232 225 215 228 C205 229 203 215 203 200 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- dewclaws -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"170\" cy=\"262\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <ellipse cx=\"230\" cy=\"262\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- direction arrow -->\n  <g stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\">\n    <line x1=\"320\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"90\"/>\n    <path d=\"M320 70 L308 94 L332 94 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Travel</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">direction</text>\n  <!-- labels -->\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Pointed tips</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">= front</text>\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">2 toes</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">(split hoof)</text>\n  <line x1=\"185\" y1=\"228\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"215\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Round heel</text>\n  <line x1=\"162\" y1=\"262\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Dewclaws:</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">deep mud, snow,</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"300\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">or running</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">About 6 to 10 cm long</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Track: heart shape, 2 toes, pointed end shows direction of travel <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Mule deer front track: about 5.7 to 10 cm long <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>You can't tell mule deer from white tail by one track. Too much overlap <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Stotting leaves groups of 4 prints, 2.4 to 4.6 m apart <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Browse sign: deer <strong>tear</strong> twigs (no upper front teeth). Hares <strong>cut</strong> clean at 45 degrees <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Rubs: mule deer make fewer rubs than white tails, on small conifers and shrubs <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Fresh sign: shiny wet pellets, crisp track edges, green stem ends on browse (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Tactics: options","html":"<h3>Option A: spot and stalk</h3><ul><li>Glass from a high point at first light, find a buck, then sneak in with the wind in your face.</li><li>Pros: the classic mule deer method; you see lots of country (hunter consensus) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Cons: hard on a beginner; long stalks; easy to bump the deer.</li></ul><h3>Option B: sit and glass a feeding slope</h3><ul><li>Sit at the edge of a grassy slope or opening at dawn or dusk. Let them come.</li><li>Pros: quiet, safe, steady shot from a rest, great for learning.</li><li>Cons: needs the right spot and patience.</li></ul><h3>Option C: still hunting through timber</h3><ul><li>Walk slowly through bedding cover. 10 steps, stop 1 minute.</li><li>Pros: works midday when deer are bedded.</li><li>Cons: deer usually see you first; shots are quick and close.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Option B for the first trip. Dawn and dusk on a slope with a long view. Midday, scout with binoculars and learn the trails.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Rifle, load and range","html":"<ul><li>Your rifle: Tikka T3x Arctic in .308 Winchester. More than enough for deer (Tip).</li><li>Load: 150 to 165 grain hunting bullet built to expand, from a known brand. Zero with the same box you hunt with (Tip).</li><li>Mule deer do not need magnum power. They need one good hit (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Your maximum range is the distance where you can hit a 15 cm (6 in) plate 9 times out of 10 from a field rest. For most beginners: 200 m (219 yd). Past that, get closer.</p></aside><ul><li>Wind, angle and a racing heart make field shots much harder than the bench (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Shot placement (brief)","html":"<ul><li>Aim for the heart and lungs: just behind the front leg, one third up the body (standard hunter education advice) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Wait for the deer to stand <strong>broadside</strong> or slightly quartering away (Tip).</li><li>No shot at a running deer, a head, a neck, or a deer on the skyline (Tip).</li><li>Full lesson with diagram: the shot placement session (coming in a later phase).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Identify every animal fully before your finger touches the trigger. Know your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Backstop\">Backstop</button>.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Legal specifics","html":"<aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><ul><li><strong>Your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MU\">MU</button> (Management Unit) at Heffley Creek and Sun Peaks:</strong> <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the official map.</li><li><strong>4 point buck:</strong> a buck with at least 4 tines on one antler, <strong>not counting the brow tine</strong> (the short point near the base). Each tine at least 2.5 cm and longer than it is wide (search preview) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Region 3 mule deer seasons</strong> (search preview, many MUs, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> yours) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>:<ul><li>10 to 30 September: 4 point bucks or better.</li><li><strong>1 to 31 October: any buck.</strong></li><li>1 November to 10 December: 4 point bucks or better.</li></ul></li><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Antlerless\">Antlerless</button> mule deer: <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"LEH\">LEH</button> (Limited Entry Hunting) draw only <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Bag limit\">Bag limit</button>: search preview says 3 deer combined, at most 1 mule deer. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Hunting hours: 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Scents: BC bans deer, elk, moose and caribou parts or products from outside BC for hunting, because of <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Skip deer urine lures.</li></ul><div class=\"regs\" data-regs=\"deerR3\"></div><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Until you read your MU in the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Synopsis\">Synopsis</button>, count points on every buck as if the 4 point rule applies.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Meat care and yield","html":"<ul><li>A field dressed mule deer is about 70% of its live weight <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Boneless meat is about 48% of field dressed weight, or about 34% of live weight <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Average field dressed buck in a Wyoming study: about 52 kg (114 lb), giving about 25 kg (55 lb) of boneless meat <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Cool it fast. Open the body, prop it open, get it in shade (Tip).</li><li>Notch your species licence right away <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Exact tag and transport steps: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the synopsis.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Beginner mistakes","html":"<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Walking in at daylight. The deer were already up and leaving.</li><li>Hunting with the wind at your back, or ignoring the thermals.</li><li>Shooting at a buck you didn't count, in a 4 point season.</li></ul></aside><ul><li>Also: skylining yourself on a ridge, rushing a stalk, and taking a long shot you never practised.</li><li>Also: chasing a deer that stotted over the hill. Wait. Glass. It may stop and look back (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Grandpa's rules","html":"<aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Glass more, walk less. The deer you see from far away is the one you can sneak up on.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Wind first, then the deer. If the wind is wrong, go somewhere else.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>When a mule deer bounces off, sit still and count to 60. Mulies love one last look.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>This week: drive the Heffley Lake road at dusk with binoculars. Note every deer, the time, the slope and which way it faced. Write it in the Journal.</p></aside><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s4-mule-scout\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mule-scout:0\"><span>Find 2 glassing spots with a long view of open slope</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mule-scout:1\"><span>Learn saskatoon, red osier dogwood and Oregon grape by sight</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mule-scout:2\"><span>Find a deer trail and fresh pellets</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mule-scout:3\"><span>Check wind direction at dawn and dusk on one slope</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mule-scout:4\"><span>Read my MU page in the Region 3 synopsis</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li>Alberta Institute for Wildlife Conservation (AIWC), <a href=\"https://www.aiwc.ca/blog/spotting-the-difference-a-guide-to-albertas-mule-deer-and-whitetails/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mule deer vs white tail identification</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>University of Nebraska, <a href=\"https://digitalcommons.unl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1015&amp;context=nebgamepubs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer identification publication</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>BC Ministry of Environment, <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/muledeer.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mule deer in BC</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Staudenmaier and others 2021, <a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ecs2.3813\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mule deer and white tail habitat</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>BC Wildlife Federation, <a href=\"https://bcwf.bc.ca/initiatives/mule-deer-project/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Southern Interior Mule Deer Project</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Monteith and others 2011, <a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1890/ES10-00096.1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">what triggers migration</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Willms 1976, <a href=\"https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/pdf/10.4141/cjas76-065\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer diet near Kamloops</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Stewart and others 2002, <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/jmammal/article/84/3/1076/905056\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer activity timing</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>D'Angelo and others 2007, <a href=\"https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2193/2006-326\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer hearing</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>BC <a href=\"https://catalogue.data.gov.bc.ca/dataset/ungulate-winter-range-approved\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ungulate winter range dataset</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Region 3 synopsis 2026 to 2028</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (capped 85%)</li><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/plants-animals-ecosystems/wildlife/wildlife-conservation/wildlife-health/chronic-wasting-disease/cwd-bc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">chronic wasting disease page</a> and <a href=\"https://www.agcanada.com/daily/b-c-to-block-hunting-attractants-from-out-of-province\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgCanada news on attractant ban</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></li><li>New Mexico Wildlife, <a href=\"https://magazine.wildlife.state.nm.us/the-math-in-the-meat/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The math in the meat</a> and <a href=\"https://www.backcountrychronicles.com/mule-deer-pack-out-weight/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Backcountry Chronicles</a>, reporting University of Wyoming carcass studies, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></li><li>Region 3 <a href=\"http://lwdrodgun.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/Region-3-Mule-Deer-Hunter-Survey-Final-Report-December-2017.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mule deer hunter survey 2017</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></li><li>Research notes: <code>research/2026-10-01-deer.md</code>.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"A deer bounces away with all 4 feet hitting the ground together. What is it?","options":["White tail","Mule deer","Elk"],"answer":1,"why":"That bounce is called stotting. It is a mule deer trademark. White tails gallop with the tail up like a flag."},{"q":"When should you be on your glassing spot in late October?","options":["At 09:00 after the frost lifts","About 45 minutes before legal light","At noon when deer are bedded"],"answer":1,"why":"The morning peak is about 04:00 to 09:00. Arrive early, in the dark, and be still when light comes."},{"q":"In a 4 point season, which buck is legal?","options":["3 tines plus a brow tine on one side","4 tines on one antler, not counting the brow tine","Any buck with 4 points total on both antlers"],"answer":1,"why":"The brow tine does not count. You need 4 or more tines on one antler, each at least 2.5 cm. VERIFY your MU rules in the synopsis."},{"q":"What mostly triggers mule deer to move down to winter range?","options":["The rut","Snow and cold","Hunting pressure on opening day"],"answer":1,"why":"Studies show snow and cold drive the move. The rut comes later, mid November."},{"q":"What do mule deer near Kamloops mostly eat in fall?","options":["Mostly grass","Shrubs and forbs, little grass","Only Douglas fir"],"answer":1,"why":"A Kamloops stomach study found grass under 10% until spring. Fall diet is forbs and shrub browse."}],"checklists":["s4-mule-scout"],"text":" !why the mule deer is the deer of the hills around heffley creek. learn its day and you will know where to sit at first light. - mule deer are the main deer of the southern bc interior (85%). - your first hunt is mid to late october. that is the pre rut : bucks start to roam, the big breeding push is still weeks away (70%). - this session: how to tell one, where it is hour by hour, what it eats, how it hides, and how you hunt it. - the deep dive on senses and alarm is in how a deer thinks ( /s/deer-mind). identify: buck, doe, fawn - ears: very big, like a mule. that is the name (85%). - antlers (buck): forks that fork again, like a \"y\" on a \"y\" (85%). - tail: thin, white, rope like, with a black tip (85%). - rump: big white patch around the tail (85%). - doe: same body, no antlers, smaller neck (85%). - fawn: born in early summer; by october it has lost its spots and is about half the doe's size (75%). - metatarsal gland (scent patch on the outside of the hind leg): long, 10 to 15 cm (85%). !tip a buck in october has a thick neck. a doe has a thin neck and a long, narrow face. if you can't see antlers clearly, you have not identified a buck. mule deer or white tail? clue mule deer white tail --- --- --- antlers forks that fork again one main beam, single tines up tail thin, white, black tip wide, brown on top, white under running away stots : all 4 feet hit and push together, bouncing (85%) gallops, tail up like a white flag (85%) ears very big medium leg gland long, 10 to 15 cm (85%) small (85%) - both deer live near heffley creek. they are separate tags (80%). - hybrids exist but are uncommon (70%). when in doubt, do not shoot (tip). a day in the life: late october - night (about 21:00 to 04:00): feeds on and off in open grass and brush, rests between bouts (75%). - before first light (04:00 to 07:00): a strong activity peak. feeding, moving (85%). - legal light starts: 1 hour before sunrise (80%). mid to late october sunrise near kamloops is about 07:30 to 07:50 (calculated) (75%). use the legal light screen in field mode. - sunrise to about 09:00: last feeding, then drifts uphill toward cover (85%). - midday (10:00 to 15:00): bedded. chews cud. gets up briefly to stretch, nibble, and re bed (80%). - late afternoon (16:00 to 18:00): rises, browses near the bed, then moves toward open feeding slopes (80%). - sunset (about 17:40 to 18:00) to 21:00: second peak. out in the open (85%). - peaks from a telemetry study: about 04:00 to 09:00 and 17:00 to 21:00 (85%). !field be on your glassing spot 45 minutes before legal light. the first and last hour are when you will see deer. how it eats, poops and sleeps - eats: a browser. twigs, leaves and buds of shrubs, plus forbs (small leafy plants), with little grass in fall (85%). - chews cud: a ruminant. it fills up fast, then lies down to chew again (85%). - poops: piles of oval pellets, about 1 to 1.5 cm long. firm and dry in fall and winter (70%). - how often: roughly 13 to 34 pellet groups a day (70%). - sleeps: in short naps, often with head up or curled, waking often (70%). - walks: slow, stopping to look and listen. often walks the same trails each day (75%). - drinks: in dry fall weather, near water at dusk or dawn. with green feed and dew, it needs less (tip). food by season near kamloops - study of deer stomachs near kamloops, september to april (85%): - grass was under 10% of the diet until spring. - more forbs in fall. - shrubs important in early fall and late winter. - douglas fir needles peak in december and january. - key browse: douglas fir, saskatoon, oregon grape, red osier dogwood, willow, rose, bitterbrush, rabbitbrush, sagebrush, snowbrush (80%). - late october menu: last green forbs, shrub leaves and twigs, fresh green grass shoots after fall rain (75%). !tip learn 3 plants this week: saskatoon, red osier dogwood (red stems) and oregon grape (holly like leaves). find them chewed and you are in a feeding area. where they are: bc and near heffley creek - mule deer like steeper, higher, open, broken ground. white tails like valley bottoms and farm fields (85%). - local reading (75%): - grassland and open douglas fir benches above the valley. - up toward heffley lake, paul lake and sun peaks in summer and early fall. - lower, sunnier slopes as snow comes. - winter range: south and west facing slopes with old douglas fir (85%). - a study of winter range north of kamloops used slopes below about 760 m (80%). - rough elevations (tip, verify on a map): valley 370 to 450 m, heffley lake about 950 m, sun peaks village about 1,250 m. - ungulate winter range maps: view the \"ungulate winter range, approved\" layer in imapbc (85%). near kamloops there may be no approved mule deer areas (verify). migration: the big october question - about 74% of tracked mule deer in a southern interior study migrated (85%). - average trip about 47 to 55 km one way, up to about 100 km (85%). - they use the same route every year (85%). - what starts the move down: snow and cold , not the rut (85%). - timing: back on winter range october to november; in one year nearly all were back by mid october (85%). - another bc account: interior deer often stay high until december (80%). - deer avoid snow deeper than about 50 cm (80%). !lean early snow up high in october? hunt the mid slopes below the snow line. no snow yet? glass the high benches and openings near heffley lake and paul lake first. senses: what scares and attracts them - nose: strongest sense. wind at your back toward the deer ends the hunt (85%). - ears: hear about 115 hz (hertz) to 54 khz (kilohertz), best at 4 to 8 khz (85%). that is the range of zippers, velcro, nylon swish and metal clicks. - eyes: see blues well, no red cone, so blaze orange looks dull to them (75%). they catch motion and outline fast (85%). - scares them: human scent, a skyline outline, sudden moves, metal clicks, voices, a slammed truck door (tip). - calms them: slow natural walking rhythm, other relaxed deer, steady breeze (tip). - attracts them: fresh green feed, does in heat (in the rut), other deer (80%). - lights: a headlamp beam swinging across a slope will move deer. use red light and keep it on the ground (tip). - full story: how a deer thinks ( /s/deer-mind). social life and the rut - does live in family groups: mother, daughters and fawns (85%). - bucks spend summer in bachelor groups of 2 to 4. these break up in october (80%). - groups are mostly 5 or fewer. in winter 5 to 8, up to 15 to 30 (75%). - deer return to the same home range year after year (85%). - rut (breeding season) for mule deer: mid november to mid december, peak mid november (80%). - late october: bucks spar (push antlers, not real fights), rub small trees, and start checking doe groups (70%). - in the rut: a buck tends one doe at a time, follows her, and fights rivals (75%). bucks get careless in daylight. - antlers fall off in winter and regrow each year (85%). beds and bedding - day beds give hiding cover and shelter (80%). - warm days: north facing timber, benches, draws, shade (80%). - cold days: sunny south and west slopes, out of the wind (80%). - lore: bucks bed in the top third of a slope, looking down, with wind from behind (hunter lore) (60%). - a bed is an oval of flattened grass or scraped earth, about 1 m long, often with pellets and hair nearby (70%). - deer bed where they can see danger below and smell danger above (tip). tracks, scat and sign - track: heart shape, 2 toes, pointed end shows direction of travel (85%). - mule deer front track: about 5.7 to 10 cm long (60%). - you can't tell mule deer from white tail by one track. too much overlap (60%). - stotting leaves groups of 4 prints, 2.4 to 4.6 m apart (60%). - browse sign: deer tear twigs (no upper front teeth). hares cut clean at 45 degrees (75%). - rubs: mule deer make fewer rubs than white tails, on small conifers and shrubs (60%). - fresh sign: shiny wet pellets, crisp track edges, green stem ends on browse (tip). tactics: options option a: spot and stalk - glass from a high point at first light, find a buck, then sneak in with the wind in your face. - pros: the classic mule deer method; you see lots of country (hunter consensus) (60%). - cons: hard on a beginner; long stalks; easy to bump the deer. option b: sit and glass a feeding slope - sit at the edge of a grassy slope or opening at dawn or dusk. let them come. - pros: quiet, safe, steady shot from a rest, great for learning. - cons: needs the right spot and patience. option c: still hunting through timber - walk slowly through bedding cover. 10 steps, stop 1 minute. - pros: works midday when deer are bedded. - cons: deer usually see you first; shots are quick and close. !lean option b for the first trip. dawn and dusk on a slope with a long view. midday, scout with binoculars and learn the trails. rifle, load and range - your rifle: tikka t3x arctic in .308 winchester. more than enough for deer (tip). - load: 150 to 165 grain hunting bullet built to expand, from a known brand. zero with the same box you hunt with (tip). - mule deer do not need magnum power. they need one good hit (tip). !tip your maximum range is the distance where you can hit a 15 cm (6 in) plate 9 times out of 10 from a field rest. for most beginners: 200 m (219 yd). past that, get closer. - wind, angle and a racing heart make field shots much harder than the bench (tip). shot placement (brief) - aim for the heart and lungs: just behind the front leg, one third up the body (standard hunter education advice) (75%). - wait for the deer to stand broadside or slightly quartering away (tip). - no shot at a running deer, a head, a neck, or a deer on the skyline (tip). - full lesson with diagram: the shot placement session (coming in a later phase). !warn identify every animal fully before your finger touches the trigger. know your backstop . legal specifics !warn study aid only. the official regulations are the law. - your mu (management unit) at heffley creek and sun peaks: verify on the official map. - 4 point buck: a buck with at least 4 tines on one antler, not counting the brow tine (the short point near the base). each tine at least 2.5 cm and longer than it is wide (search preview) (80%). - region 3 mule deer seasons (search preview, many mus, verify yours) (65%): - 10 to 30 september: 4 point bucks or better. - 1 to 31 october: any buck. - 1 november to 10 december: 4 point bucks or better. - antlerless mule deer: leh (limited entry hunting) draw only (75%). - bag limit : search preview says 3 deer combined, at most 1 mule deer. verify (60%). - hunting hours: 1 hour before sunrise to 1 hour after sunset (80%). - scents: bc bans deer, elk, moose and caribou parts or products from outside bc for hunting, because of cwd (chronic wasting disease) (80%). skip deer urine lures. !lean until you read your mu in the synopsis , count points on every buck as if the 4 point rule applies. meat care and yield - a field dressed mule deer is about 70% of its live weight (70%). - boneless meat is about 48% of field dressed weight, or about 34% of live weight (70%). - average field dressed buck in a wyoming study: about 52 kg (114 lb), giving about 25 kg (55 lb) of boneless meat (70%). - cool it fast. open the body, prop it open, get it in shade (tip). - notch your species licence right away (75%). exact tag and transport steps: verify in the synopsis. beginner mistakes !mistake - walking in at daylight. the deer were already up and leaving. - hunting with the wind at your back, or ignoring the thermals. - shooting at a buck you didn't count, in a 4 point season. - also: skylining yourself on a ridge, rushing a stalk, and taking a long shot you never practised. - also: chasing a deer that stotted over the hill. wait. glass. it may stop and look back (hunter lore) (65%). grandpa's rules !rule glass more, walk less. the deer you see from far away is the one you can sneak up on. !rule wind first, then the deer. if the wind is wrong, go somewhere else. !rule when a mule deer bounces off, sit still and count to 60. mulies love one last look. !field this week: drive the heffley lake road at dusk with binoculars. note every deer, the time, the slope and which way it faced. write it in the journal. sources - alberta institute for wildlife conservation (aiwc), mule deer vs white tail identification (https://www.aiwc.ca/blog/spotting-the-difference-a-guide-to-albertas-mule-deer-and-whitetails/), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - university of nebraska, deer identification publication (https://digitalcommons.unl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1015&context=nebgamepubs), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - bc ministry of environment, mule deer in bc (https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/muledeer.pdf), search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - staudenmaier and others 2021, mule deer and white tail habitat (https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ecs2.3813), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - bc wildlife federation, southern interior mule deer project (https://bcwf.bc.ca/initiatives/mule-deer-project/), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - monteith and others 2011, what triggers migration (https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1890/es10-00096.1), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - willms 1976, deer diet near kamloops (https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/pdf/10.4141/cjas76-065), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - stewart and others 2002, deer activity timing (https://academic.oup.com/jmammal/article/84/3/1076/905056), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - d'angelo and others 2007, deer hearing (https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2193/2006-326), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - bc ungulate winter range dataset (https://catalogue.data.gov.bc.ca/dataset/ungulate-winter-range-approved), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - bc region 3 synopsis 2026 to 2028 (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf), search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (capped 85%) - bc chronic wasting disease page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/plants-animals-ecosystems/wildlife/wildlife-conservation/wildlife-health/chronic-wasting-disease/cwd-bc) and agcanada news on attractant ban (https://www.agcanada.com/daily/b-c-to-block-hunting-attractants-from-out-of-province), search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (80%) - new mexico wildlife, the math in the meat (https://magazine.wildlife.state.nm.us/the-math-in-the-meat/) and backcountry chronicles (https://www.backcountrychronicles.com/mule-deer-pack-out-weight/), reporting university of wyoming carcass studies, checked 2026-10-01 (70%) - region 3 mule deer hunter survey 2017 (http://lwdrodgun.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/region-3-mule-deer-hunter-survey-final-report-december-2017.pdf), checked 2026-10-01 (70%) - research notes: research/2026-10-01-deer.md . "},{"id":"white-tailed-deer","phase":4,"num":2,"title":"White tailed deer","minutes":15,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"White tailed deer","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>White tails live in the North Thompson valley bottoms, close to roads and farms. The antlerless season may be open during your October hunt.</p></aside><ul><li>White tails favour valley bottoms, river edges and farm fields <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Near Heffley Creek: North Thompson hayfields and cottonwood bottoms <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Late October is the start of the white tail rut build up: rubs and scrapes appear <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>They are a <strong>separate species licence</strong> from mule deer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Best methods for a beginner: sit on a stand, or still hunt slowly (hunter consensus) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Identify: buck, doe, fawn","html":"<ul><li><strong>Antlers (buck):</strong> one main beam curving forward, single tines pointing up <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Tail:</strong> wide, brown on top, bright white underneath <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Alarm:</strong> tail goes straight up and waves, the white <strong>flag</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Runs:</strong> a smooth gallop toward cover, not a bounce <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Ears:</strong> smaller than a mule deer's <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Leg gland</strong> (metatarsal, on the outside of the hind leg): small <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><strong>Doe and fawn:</strong> no antlers. By October fawns have lost their spots <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 420\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"420\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"410\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 6\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mule deer</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">White tail</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Antlers (one side, seen from front)</text>\n  <!-- mule deer antler: forks that split again -->\n  <g stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M100 190 L100 160\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 160 L70 110\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 160 L130 110\"/>\n    <path d=\"M70 110 L55 72\"/>\n    <path d=\"M70 110 L85 75\"/>\n    <path d=\"M130 110 L118 74\"/>\n    <path d=\"M130 110 L148 76\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 178 L86 168\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Forks that fork again</text>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(\"Y\" on a \"Y\")</text>\n  <!-- whitetail antler: one main beam, tines up -->\n  <g stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M250 190 Q240 120 300 100 Q345 90 360 120\"/>\n    <path d=\"M262 140 L258 112\"/>\n    <path d=\"M285 108 L282 72\"/>\n    <path d=\"M315 98 L318 64\"/>\n    <path d=\"M343 102 L350 76\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">One main beam,</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">single tines up</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Tail and rump (seen from behind)</text>\n  <!-- mule deer rump -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"330\" rx=\"48\" ry=\"48\" fill=\"#f3ead8\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"94\" y=\"290\" width=\"12\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3ead8\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"94\" y=\"340\" width=\"12\" height=\"18\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#23261f\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"396\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Big white rump,</text>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"413\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">thin tail, black tip</text>\n  <!-- whitetail flag -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"300\" cy=\"345\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"34\" fill=\"#b08a5e\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M300 340 C270 320 268 285 285 275 L300 270 L315 275 C332 285 330 320 300 340 Z\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"300\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">flag up</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"396\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wide brown tail,</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"413\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">white below, waved up</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>A doe and a \"button buck\" (male fawn with tiny bumps) look alike. Both count as antlerless. Know the rule before you shoot.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Where they live near Heffley Creek","html":"<ul><li>Valley bottoms, riparian strips (the green belt along rivers and creeks), farm fields <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Local reading: North Thompson hayfields and cottonwood bottoms north of Kamloops <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>They feed in fields at dusk and night, and bed in thick brush, willow and cottonwood close by <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>They do not migrate far like mule deer. Most stay in a small home range year round <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>They live close to houses and roads. Shooting distance rules matter a lot here.</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 340\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"340\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Where deer are on a slope</text>\n  <!-- ground profile -->\n  <path d=\"M0 300 L110 300 L180 230 L215 222 L250 175 L285 168 L340 80 L400 70 L400 340 L0 340 Z\" fill=\"#d9cfb3\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- trees on bench and upper slope -->\n  <g fill=\"#2f3a2b\">\n    <path d=\"M255 172 L262 145 L269 172 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M272 170 L279 140 L286 170 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M300 140 L307 112 L314 140 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M320 108 L327 80 L334 108 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M352 78 L359 52 L366 78 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <!-- field -->\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"292\" width=\"95\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <!-- deer dots -->\n  <circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"285\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"285\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"148\" cy=\"254\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"265\" cy=\"160\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"345\" cy=\"70\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <!-- labels -->\n  <text x=\"55\" y=\"322\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Valley fields:</text>\n  <text x=\"55\" y=\"338\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">white tails</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Open grass:</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"306\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">feeding</text>\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bench + timber:</text>\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">day beds</text>\n  <text x=\"345\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ridge</text>\n  <!-- thermals -->\n  <g stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\">\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <path d=\"M110 80 L98 82 L106 92 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\">Morning warms:</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">air flows UP</text>\n  <g stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">\n    <line x1=\"230\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"100\"/>\n    <path d=\"M180 100 L192 98 L184 88 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Evening cools:</text>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">air flows DOWN</text>\n</svg>\n</figure>"},{"title":"Daily pattern","html":"<ul><li>Same two peaks as mule deer: dawn and dusk <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Feeds in fields from late afternoon through the night <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Slips back into cover at or before first light <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Under hunting pressure, white tails move more at night <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Late October: bucks start moving in daylight as the rut nears <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Legal light starts 1 hour before sunrise and ends 1 hour after sunset <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Find the trail between the bedding brush and the field. Look for the most trampled path with the freshest tracks. Sit downwind of it.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Food, water and cover","html":"<ul><li>Hay and alfalfa regrowth in fall fields <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Browse: willow, red osier dogwood, rose, saskatoon along creeks <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Water is close in valley bottoms, so water is rarely the limit (Tip).</li><li>Cover: dense willow, cottonwood thickets, young conifers, brushy draws <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Rut sign: rubs and scrapes","html":"<ul><li><strong>Rut</strong> (breeding season): white tail peak is November. Most does conceive about 13 to 17 November <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><strong>Rubs:</strong> a buck scrapes bark off a sapling with his antlers. Bare, shredded wood at knee to waist height <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>White tails make many more rubs than mule deer <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li><strong>Scrape:</strong> a pawed bare patch of dirt, often under a low branch the buck chews and rubs his face on <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Scrapes show up late October to November <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>A <strong>rub line</strong> is several rubs in a row. It often marks a travel route (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Fresh rubs ooze sap and the wood is pale. Old rubs are grey. Hunt the fresh ones.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Tracks and sign","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"320\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer track (front foot)</text>\n  <!-- two toes (halves of heart), point = toe tips at top -->\n  <path d=\"M195 70 C180 75 160 110 158 160 C156 200 168 225 185 228 C195 229 197 215 197 200 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M205 70 C220 75 240 110 242 160 C244 200 232 225 215 228 C205 229 203 215 203 200 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- dewclaws -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"170\" cy=\"262\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <ellipse cx=\"230\" cy=\"262\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- direction arrow -->\n  <g stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\">\n    <line x1=\"320\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"90\"/>\n    <path d=\"M320 70 L308 94 L332 94 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Travel</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">direction</text>\n  <!-- labels -->\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Pointed tips</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">= front</text>\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">2 toes</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">(split hoof)</text>\n  <line x1=\"185\" y1=\"228\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"215\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Round heel</text>\n  <line x1=\"162\" y1=\"262\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Dewclaws:</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">deep mud, snow,</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"300\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">or running</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">About 6 to 10 cm long</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Heart shaped track, same as mule deer. You can't tell them apart by one track <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Pellets: oval, about 1 to 1.5 cm <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Trails: worn paths from bedding brush to field edges <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Beds: flattened ovals in tall grass or brush near the field edge <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Browse: torn, ragged twig ends <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Tactics: options","html":"<h3>Option A: stand or ground blind</h3><ul><li>Sit on the trail between bedding and food, downwind, before light or 2 hours before dark.</li><li>Pros: you are still, deer come to you, easy steady shot.</li><li>Cons: needs scouting and permission on farm land; cold; patience.</li></ul><h3>Option B: still hunting</h3><ul><li>Walk 10 steps, stop and look for a minute or more. Wind in your face.</li><li>Pros: covers ground; works in thick bottoms midday.</li><li>Cons: white tails are hard to sneak on in thick cover; quick close shots.</li></ul><h3>Option C: rattling and grunting</h3><ul><li>Clack antlers together and grunt to sound like bucks fighting.</li><li>Pros: can pull a buck in close.</li><li>Cons: works best in early November, not mid October. One Texas study saw about 65% of bucks respond, best on cool, calm, overcast mornings <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Option A. Get permission on one hayfield edge, find the main trail, and sit it at dusk.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Rifle, load and range","html":"<ul><li>Your .308 Winchester with a 150 to 165 grain bullet built to expand is plenty (Tip).</li><li>Shots in valley bottoms are often 50 to 150 m (55 to 164 yd) (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Same limit as mule deer: only shoot as far as you can hit a 15 cm (6 in) plate 9 times out of 10 from a field rest. For most beginners, 200 m (219 yd).</p></aside><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Flat fields, houses and roads. Before every shot, see a solid earth <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Backstop\">Backstop</button>. No shots toward buildings, livestock or roads.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Shot placement (brief)","html":"<ul><li>Heart and lungs: tight behind the front leg, one third up the body (standard hunter education advice) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Wait for broadside or slightly quartering away (Tip).</li><li>White tails often run 50 to 100 m after a good hit. Mark where it stood and where it entered cover (Tip).</li><li>Full lesson: the shot placement session (coming in a later phase).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Legal specifics","html":"<aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><ul><li><strong>Your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MU\">MU</button> (Management Unit):</strong> <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the official map.</li><li><strong><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Antlerless\">Antlerless</button> white tails:</strong> 10 to 31 October in MUs 3-12 to 3-20, 3-26 to 3-44 and 3-46 (search preview, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>White tailed buck season in your MU:</strong> <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li><strong><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Bag limit\">Bag limit</button>:</strong> search preview says 3 deer combined in Region 3, at most 2 white tails. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li><strong>Buildings:</strong> no hunting or shooting within 100 m of an occupied dwelling, farm or ranch building, church, school, playground or regional park <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Private land:</strong> get permission before you hunt a farm. Trespass rules: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the BC Trespass Act.</li><li><strong>Inside city or regional limits:</strong> some areas ban shooting. Local discharge bylaw: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Deer parts or scents from outside BC are banned for hunting, because of <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><div class=\"regs\" data-regs=\"deerR3,safety\"></div>"},{"title":"Meat care and yield","html":"<ul><li>Yield for white tails: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Expect a similar ratio to mule deer: about one third of live weight as boneless meat (Tip, assumption).</li><li>Cool the carcass fast. October days can be warm in the valley (Tip).</li><li>Notch your white tailed deer species licence right away <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Exact steps: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the synopsis.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Beginner mistakes","html":"<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Sitting right on the field edge in the open, where every deer sees you walk in.</li><li>Hunting a farm without written or clear permission.</li><li>Shooting at a flagging white tail running away.</li></ul></aside><ul><li>Also: walking through the bedding brush to get to your stand. Go around.</li><li>Also: noisy truck doors and voices near the field at dusk.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Grandpa's rules","html":"<aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Hunt the trail, not the field. Big bucks reach the field after dark.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Ask the farmer first, thank them after, and bring them a package of sausage.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>A flag in the air means \"goodbye\". Let it go. It will be back another evening.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>This week: knock on one farm door in the North Thompson valley. Ask to scout the field edges. Look for trails, rubs and a spot to sit downwind.</p></aside><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s4-wt-scout\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-wt-scout:0\"><span>Get permission on one farm or find legal Crown land in the valley</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-wt-scout:1\"><span>Find the main trail from cover to field</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-wt-scout:2\"><span>Find fresh rubs or scrapes</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-wt-scout:3\"><span>Pick a stand spot downwind of the trail with a safe backstop</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-wt-scout:4\"><span>Check distance to every building is over 100 m</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-wt-scout:5\"><span>Read antlerless dates for my MU in the synopsis</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li>Alberta Institute for Wildlife Conservation (AIWC), <a href=\"https://www.aiwc.ca/blog/spotting-the-difference-a-guide-to-albertas-mule-deer-and-whitetails/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mule deer vs white tail identification</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Staudenmaier and others 2021, <a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ecs2.3813\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mule deer and white tail habitat</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Stewart and others 2002, <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/jmammal/article/84/3/1076/905056\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer activity timing</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Penn State Extension, white tail breeding dates, via research notes, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></li><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Region 3 synopsis 2026 to 2028</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (capped 85%)</li><li>BC <a href=\"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting Regulation, B.C. Reg. 190/84</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></li><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/plants-animals-ecosystems/wildlife/wildlife-conservation/wildlife-health/chronic-wasting-disease/cwd-bc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">chronic wasting disease page</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></li><li>Research notes: <code>research/2026-10-01-deer.md</code>.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"A deer runs into the willows with a wide white tail held straight up. What is it?","options":["Mule deer","White tailed deer","Can't tell"],"answer":1,"why":"The raised white flag and smooth gallop are white tail signs. Mule deer have a thin black tipped tail and bounce."},{"q":"Where is the best place for your stand?","options":["In the middle of the hayfield","On the trail between bedding cover and the field, downwind","Inside the bedding brush"],"answer":1,"why":"Deer travel that trail at dusk and dawn. Downwind keeps your scent off them. Walking into bedding brush pushes them out."},{"q":"What is a scrape?","options":["Bark rubbed off a sapling","A pawed bare patch of dirt under a low branch","A deer bed"],"answer":1,"why":"Bucks paw the ground and work the branch above with their face and antlers. Bark stripping is a rub."},{"q":"How close to an occupied house or farm building may you shoot?","options":["Any distance with a backstop","Not within 100 m","Not within 50 m"],"answer":1,"why":"BC rules ban hunting or shooting within 100 m of occupied dwellings and farm buildings. VERIFY in the synopsis."},{"q":"When does rattling work best for white tails?","options":["Hot afternoons in September","Cool, calm mornings near the rut in early November","Any time of year"],"answer":1,"why":"Response was best on cool, calm, overcast mornings near the rut. Mid October is early for it."}],"checklists":["s4-wt-scout"],"text":" !why white tails live in the north thompson valley bottoms, close to roads and farms. the antlerless season may be open during your october hunt. - white tails favour valley bottoms, river edges and farm fields (85%). - near heffley creek: north thompson hayfields and cottonwood bottoms (75%). - late october is the start of the white tail rut build up: rubs and scrapes appear (60%). - they are a separate species licence from mule deer (80%). - best methods for a beginner: sit on a stand, or still hunt slowly (hunter consensus) (65%). identify: buck, doe, fawn - antlers (buck): one main beam curving forward, single tines pointing up (85%). - tail: wide, brown on top, bright white underneath (85%). - alarm: tail goes straight up and waves, the white flag (85%). - runs: a smooth gallop toward cover, not a bounce (85%). - ears: smaller than a mule deer's (85%). - leg gland (metatarsal, on the outside of the hind leg): small (85%). - doe and fawn: no antlers. by october fawns have lost their spots (75%). !warn a doe and a \"button buck\" (male fawn with tiny bumps) look alike. both count as antlerless. know the rule before you shoot. where they live near heffley creek - valley bottoms, riparian strips (the green belt along rivers and creeks), farm fields (85%). - local reading: north thompson hayfields and cottonwood bottoms north of kamloops (75%). - they feed in fields at dusk and night, and bed in thick brush, willow and cottonwood close by (75%). - they do not migrate far like mule deer. most stay in a small home range year round (70%). - they live close to houses and roads. shooting distance rules matter a lot here. daily pattern - same two peaks as mule deer: dawn and dusk (85%). - feeds in fields from late afternoon through the night (75%). - slips back into cover at or before first light (75%). - under hunting pressure, white tails move more at night (70%). - late october: bucks start moving in daylight as the rut nears (70%). - legal light starts 1 hour before sunrise and ends 1 hour after sunset (80%). !field find the trail between the bedding brush and the field. look for the most trampled path with the freshest tracks. sit downwind of it. food, water and cover - hay and alfalfa regrowth in fall fields (80%). - browse: willow, red osier dogwood, rose, saskatoon along creeks (75%). - water is close in valley bottoms, so water is rarely the limit (tip). - cover: dense willow, cottonwood thickets, young conifers, brushy draws (75%). rut sign: rubs and scrapes - rut (breeding season): white tail peak is november. most does conceive about 13 to 17 november (80%). - rubs: a buck scrapes bark off a sapling with his antlers. bare, shredded wood at knee to waist height (60%). - white tails make many more rubs than mule deer (60%). - scrape: a pawed bare patch of dirt, often under a low branch the buck chews and rubs his face on (60%). - scrapes show up late october to november (60%). - a rub line is several rubs in a row. it often marks a travel route (tip). !tip fresh rubs ooze sap and the wood is pale. old rubs are grey. hunt the fresh ones. tracks and sign - heart shaped track, same as mule deer. you can't tell them apart by one track (60%). - pellets: oval, about 1 to 1.5 cm (70%). - trails: worn paths from bedding brush to field edges (75%). - beds: flattened ovals in tall grass or brush near the field edge (70%). - browse: torn, ragged twig ends (75%). tactics: options option a: stand or ground blind - sit on the trail between bedding and food, downwind, before light or 2 hours before dark. - pros: you are still, deer come to you, easy steady shot. - cons: needs scouting and permission on farm land; cold; patience. option b: still hunting - walk 10 steps, stop and look for a minute or more. wind in your face. - pros: covers ground; works in thick bottoms midday. - cons: white tails are hard to sneak on in thick cover; quick close shots. option c: rattling and grunting - clack antlers together and grunt to sound like bucks fighting. - pros: can pull a buck in close. - cons: works best in early november, not mid october. one texas study saw about 65% of bucks respond, best on cool, calm, overcast mornings (70%). !lean option a. get permission on one hayfield edge, find the main trail, and sit it at dusk. rifle, load and range - your .308 winchester with a 150 to 165 grain bullet built to expand is plenty (tip). - shots in valley bottoms are often 50 to 150 m (55 to 164 yd) (tip). !tip same limit as mule deer: only shoot as far as you can hit a 15 cm (6 in) plate 9 times out of 10 from a field rest. for most beginners, 200 m (219 yd). !warn flat fields, houses and roads. before every shot, see a solid earth backstop . no shots toward buildings, livestock or roads. shot placement (brief) - heart and lungs: tight behind the front leg, one third up the body (standard hunter education advice) (75%). - wait for broadside or slightly quartering away (tip). - white tails often run 50 to 100 m after a good hit. mark where it stood and where it entered cover (tip). - full lesson: the shot placement session (coming in a later phase). legal specifics !warn study aid only. the official regulations are the law. - your mu (management unit): verify on the official map. - antlerless white tails: 10 to 31 october in mus 3-12 to 3-20, 3-26 to 3-44 and 3-46 (search preview, verify) (70%). - white tailed buck season in your mu: verify. - bag limit : search preview says 3 deer combined in region 3, at most 2 white tails. verify (60%). - buildings: no hunting or shooting within 100 m of an occupied dwelling, farm or ranch building, church, school, playground or regional park (75%). - private land: get permission before you hunt a farm. trespass rules: verify in the bc trespass act. - inside city or regional limits: some areas ban shooting. local discharge bylaw: verify. - deer parts or scents from outside bc are banned for hunting, because of cwd (chronic wasting disease) (80%). meat care and yield - yield for white tails: verify. expect a similar ratio to mule deer: about one third of live weight as boneless meat (tip, assumption). - cool the carcass fast. october days can be warm in the valley (tip). - notch your white tailed deer species licence right away (75%). exact steps: verify in the synopsis. beginner mistakes !mistake - sitting right on the field edge in the open, where every deer sees you walk in. - hunting a farm without written or clear permission. - shooting at a flagging white tail running away. - also: walking through the bedding brush to get to your stand. go around. - also: noisy truck doors and voices near the field at dusk. grandpa's rules !rule hunt the trail, not the field. big bucks reach the field after dark. !rule ask the farmer first, thank them after, and bring them a package of sausage. !rule a flag in the air means \"goodbye\". let it go. it will be back another evening. !field this week: knock on one farm door in the north thompson valley. ask to scout the field edges. look for trails, rubs and a spot to sit downwind. sources - alberta institute for wildlife conservation (aiwc), mule deer vs white tail identification (https://www.aiwc.ca/blog/spotting-the-difference-a-guide-to-albertas-mule-deer-and-whitetails/), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - staudenmaier and others 2021, mule deer and white tail habitat (https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ecs2.3813), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - stewart and others 2002, deer activity timing (https://academic.oup.com/jmammal/article/84/3/1076/905056), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - penn state extension, white tail breeding dates, via research notes, checked 2026-10-01 (80%) - bc region 3 synopsis 2026 to 2028 (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf), search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (capped 85%) - bc hunting regulation, b.c. reg. 190/84 (https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01), search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (75%) - bc chronic wasting disease page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/plants-animals-ecosystems/wildlife/wildlife-conservation/wildlife-health/chronic-wasting-disease/cwd-bc), search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (80%) - research notes: research/2026-10-01-deer.md . "},{"id":"deer-mind","phase":4,"num":3,"title":"How a deer thinks","minutes":12,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"How a deer thinks","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>A deer's whole life is staying alive. If you think like a scared prey animal, you will stop spooking them.</p></aside><ul><li>A deer trusts its <strong>nose</strong> first, then its <strong>ears</strong>, then its <strong>eyes</strong> (Tip, ranking based on the facts below).</li><li>Its nose almost never lies. Its eyes are great at motion, poor at detail <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Wind and <strong>thermals</strong> (air sliding up or down a slope) decide where your scent goes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Alarm has stages: alert, stamp, snort, run. You can sometimes stop it at stage 1 (Tip).</li><li>Cougars kill more southern Interior mule deer than any other cause. Deer are built around that fear <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Hunters change deer too: pressure pushes them into thicker cover and night movement <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Senses ranked","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rank</th><th>Sense</th><th>What it catches</th><th>Can you beat it?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Nose</td><td>Your scent from far downwind</td><td>Only with the wind <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Ears</td><td>High clicks, zips, nylon, voices</td><td>Yes, with quiet gear and slow feet <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Eyes</td><td>Motion and outline, not colour detail</td><td>Yes, with stillness and a background <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>You can fool the eyes. You can quiet your feet. You can never fool the nose. Play the wind.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Eyes","html":"<ul><li>Deer have 2 colour cones, peaking near 455 nm (blue) and 537 nm (green). No red cone <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>So blaze orange looks dull, close to grey or brown, to a deer <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Deer pick up more ultraviolet (UV) light than we do <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Some laundry detergents add UV brighteners that may glow to a deer (Tip) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>. Use unscented, no brightener soap.</li><li>Motion and outline give you away, not colour <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Lights: a moving headlamp or phone screen in the dark is pure motion. Red light, pointed at the ground (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Ears","html":"<ul><li>Deer hear about 115 Hz (hertz) to 54 kHz (kilohertz). Best around 4 to 8 kHz <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>That band is exactly zippers, Velcro, nylon swish, metal on metal, and snapping twigs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Each ear swivels on its own to point at a sound <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Natural sounds (a squirrel, a deer walking, wind) are filtered out. Odd rhythms and metal are not (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>Two legged walking is a steady \"crunch crunch crunch\". A deer walks, stops, walks. Copy that rhythm.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Nose","html":"<ul><li>Smell is a deer's strongest sense <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The often quoted \"297 million scent receptors\" figure is folklore, not a measured fact <span class=\"cert c-tip\" title=\"Certainty\">45%</span>.</li><li>Your scent comes from breath, skin, clothes, boots and your truck (Tip).</li><li>Cover scents and scent control help a little. The wind helps a lot (Tip).</li><li>Deer and elk urine lures from outside BC are banned for hunting because of <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Wind and thermals","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 340\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"340\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Where deer are on a slope</text>\n  <!-- ground profile -->\n  <path d=\"M0 300 L110 300 L180 230 L215 222 L250 175 L285 168 L340 80 L400 70 L400 340 L0 340 Z\" fill=\"#d9cfb3\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- trees on bench and upper slope -->\n  <g fill=\"#2f3a2b\">\n    <path d=\"M255 172 L262 145 L269 172 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M272 170 L279 140 L286 170 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M300 140 L307 112 L314 140 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M320 108 L327 80 L334 108 Z\"/>\n    <path d=\"M352 78 L359 52 L366 78 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <!-- field -->\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"292\" width=\"95\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <!-- deer dots -->\n  <circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"285\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"285\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"148\" cy=\"254\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"265\" cy=\"160\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"345\" cy=\"70\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <!-- labels -->\n  <text x=\"55\" y=\"322\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Valley fields:</text>\n  <text x=\"55\" y=\"338\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">white tails</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Open grass:</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"306\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">feeding</text>\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"228\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bench + timber:</text>\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"244\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">day beds</text>\n  <text x=\"345\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ridge</text>\n  <!-- thermals -->\n  <g stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#e8590c\">\n    <line x1=\"60\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"80\"/>\n    <path d=\"M110 80 L98 82 L106 92 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\">Morning warms:</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">air flows UP</text>\n  <g stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">\n    <line x1=\"230\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"100\"/>\n    <path d=\"M180 100 L192 98 L184 88 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">Evening cools:</text>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">air flows DOWN</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Sun warms the slope in the morning. Warm air rises, so <strong>scent drifts uphill</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Slopes cool in the evening. Cool air sinks, so <strong>scent drifts downhill</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>At dawn before the sun hits, air often still sinks. It flips after sun warms the slope <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Draws and saddles swirl the air. Your scent can go everywhere (Tip).</li><li>Main wind usually beats thermals when it blows hard (Tip).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Morning: approach from above or from the side. Evening: hunt from below or the side, and leave before the cold air drains your scent down to the bedded deer.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Carry a puffer bottle of unscented powder or a thread on your rifle. Check it every 10 minutes.</p></aside>"},{"title":"When a deer is alarmed","html":"<ol><li><strong>Alert:</strong> head up, ears forward, stare. Freeze until it looks away (Tip).</li><li><strong>Head bob:</strong> drops the head as if to feed, then snaps it up to catch you moving (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Foot stamp:</strong> stamps a front hoof. Warns other deer and tries to make you move <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Snort or blow:</strong> a loud whoosh of air. All deer nearby are now on alert <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Escape:</strong><ul><li>Mule deer <strong>stot</strong>: bounces on all 4 feet, good for steep broken ground <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>White tail <strong>flags</strong> and gallops to cover <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></li></ol><ul><li>Mule deer often stop and look back from about 150 to 300 m (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Tip (opinion)</div><p>If a deer stares at you, do not move a finger. Count to 100. Most deer go back to feeding if nothing else happens.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Predator pressure","html":"<ul><li>In the southern Interior mule deer project, cougars were the top cause of death, coyotes second <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Wolves have come back to the Thompson area <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Against cougars (ambush hunters), deer pick spots with long sightlines <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Against wolves and dogs (chase hunters), deer use rugged steep slopes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Does live in family groups: more eyes and ears <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>What this means for you: deer bed where they can <strong>see below and smell above</strong> (Tip).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Human pressure","html":"<ul><li>Pressured deer move more at night and use thicker cover <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Opening days and weekends push deer away from roads (Tip).</li><li>Deer learn sounds: a truck door slam at a pullout can clear a slope (Tip).</li><li>Deer return to the same home range year after year, so spooking them is not forever <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Walk 500 m or more past the road and the pullouts. Park quietly. Close the door with your hip.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Folklore vs science","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Folklore</th><th>What we know</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deer can't see blaze orange</td><td>Blaze orange looks dull to deer. Motion still gives you away <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Deer have 297 million scent receptors</td><td>Unmeasured folklore. Nose is still their best sense <span class=\"cert c-tip\" title=\"Certainty\">45%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Bucks always bed on the top third of a slope</td><td>Often true, not a rule (lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span></td></tr><tr><td>The rut makes mule deer migrate</td><td>Snow and cold trigger migration <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Deer urine lures are a must</td><td>Out of province deer products are banned in BC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Wind beats lures (Tip)</td></tr><tr><td>Grunt calls work all season</td><td>Rattling and calling work best near the rut, early November <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"title":"Mistakes and rules","html":"<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Checking the wind once at the truck, then never again.</li><li>Moving when a deer is staring at you.</li><li>Wearing loud nylon and a jingling pack.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>When a deer stamps, you've already lost the eyes. Don't lose the ears too. Freeze.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>The deer never makes a mistake with its nose. Make yours with the wind in your favour.</p></aside><div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"s4-mind-kit\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mind-kit:0\"><span>Wind checker (powder puffer or thread)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mind-kit:1\"><span>Unscented soap, no UV brightener</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mind-kit:2\"><span>Quiet outer layer (wool or fleece, no nylon swish)</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mind-kit:3\"><span>Tape over any clinking metal on pack and sling</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"s4-mind-kit:4\"><span>Red light headlamp</span></label></div>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li>Jacobs and others 1994, deer colour vision (cone peaks), via research notes, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>D'Angelo and others 2007, <a href=\"https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2193/2006-326\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer hearing</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Stewart and others 2002, <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/jmammal/article/84/3/1076/905056\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">deer activity timing</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Monteith and others 2011, <a href=\"https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1890/ES10-00096.1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">what triggers migration</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>BC Wildlife Federation, <a href=\"https://bcwf.bc.ca/initiatives/mule-deer-project/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Southern Interior Mule Deer Project</a>, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Lingle 2003, deer group behaviour and predators, via research notes, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/plants-animals-ecosystems/wildlife/wildlife-conservation/wildlife-health/chronic-wasting-disease/cwd-bc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">chronic wasting disease page</a>, search preview, checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></li><li>Research notes: <code>research/2026-10-01-deer.md</code>.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which sense can you never fully beat?","options":["Eyes","Ears","Nose"],"answer":2,"why":"Smell is a deer's strongest sense. Only the wind protects you."},{"q":"It is 08:30, sun on the slope. Which way does your scent drift?","options":["Uphill","Downhill","Nowhere"],"answer":0,"why":"Sun warms the slope and warm air rises, so scent drifts uphill. Approach from above or the side."},{"q":"A deer stares at you and stamps a hoof. What do you do?","options":["Raise the rifle fast","Freeze completely","Step behind a tree"],"answer":1,"why":"The stamp is a test to make you move. Freeze until it looks away or relaxes."},{"q":"What gives a hunter in blaze orange away?","options":["The bright colour","Motion and outline","Nothing"],"answer":1,"why":"Deer have no red cone, so orange looks dull. They catch motion and shape very fast."},{"q":"What is the top cause of death for southern Interior mule deer?","options":["Hunters","Cougars","Winter starvation"],"answer":1,"why":"In the southern Interior mule deer project, cougars were first and coyotes second."}],"checklists":["s4-mind-kit"],"text":" !why a deer's whole life is staying alive. if you think like a scared prey animal, you will stop spooking them. - a deer trusts its nose first, then its ears , then its eyes (tip, ranking based on the facts below). - its nose almost never lies. its eyes are great at motion, poor at detail (85%). - wind and thermals (air sliding up or down a slope) decide where your scent goes (80%). - alarm has stages: alert, stamp, snort, run. you can sometimes stop it at stage 1 (tip). - cougars kill more southern interior mule deer than any other cause. deer are built around that fear (85%). - hunters change deer too: pressure pushes them into thicker cover and night movement (70%). senses ranked rank sense what it catches can you beat it? --- --- --- --- 1 nose your scent from far downwind only with the wind (85%) 2 ears high clicks, zips, nylon, voices yes, with quiet gear and slow feet (85%) 3 eyes motion and outline, not colour detail yes, with stillness and a background (85%) !rule you can fool the eyes. you can quiet your feet. you can never fool the nose. play the wind. eyes - deer have 2 colour cones, peaking near 455 nm (blue) and 537 nm (green). no red cone (85%). - so blaze orange looks dull, close to grey or brown, to a deer (75%). - deer pick up more ultraviolet (uv) light than we do (75%). - some laundry detergents add uv brighteners that may glow to a deer (tip) (65%). use unscented, no brightener soap. - motion and outline give you away, not colour (85%). - lights: a moving headlamp or phone screen in the dark is pure motion. red light, pointed at the ground (tip). ears - deer hear about 115 hz (hertz) to 54 khz (kilohertz). best around 4 to 8 khz (85%). - that band is exactly zippers, velcro, nylon swish, metal on metal, and snapping twigs (85%). - each ear swivels on its own to point at a sound (75%). - natural sounds (a squirrel, a deer walking, wind) are filtered out. odd rhythms and metal are not (tip). !tip two legged walking is a steady \"crunch crunch crunch\". a deer walks, stops, walks. copy that rhythm. nose - smell is a deer's strongest sense (85%). - the often quoted \"297 million scent receptors\" figure is folklore, not a measured fact (45%). - your scent comes from breath, skin, clothes, boots and your truck (tip). - cover scents and scent control help a little. the wind helps a lot (tip). - deer and elk urine lures from outside bc are banned for hunting because of cwd (chronic wasting disease) (80%). wind and thermals - sun warms the slope in the morning. warm air rises, so scent drifts uphill (80%). - slopes cool in the evening. cool air sinks, so scent drifts downhill (80%). - at dawn before the sun hits, air often still sinks. it flips after sun warms the slope (75%). - draws and saddles swirl the air. your scent can go everywhere (tip). - main wind usually beats thermals when it blows hard (tip). !lean morning: approach from above or from the side. evening: hunt from below or the side, and leave before the cold air drains your scent down to the bedded deer. !field carry a puffer bottle of unscented powder or a thread on your rifle. check it every 10 minutes. when a deer is alarmed 1. alert: head up, ears forward, stare. freeze until it looks away (tip). 2. head bob: drops the head as if to feed, then snaps it up to catch you moving (hunter lore) (70%). 3. foot stamp: stamps a front hoof. warns other deer and tries to make you move (70%). 4. snort or blow: a loud whoosh of air. all deer nearby are now on alert (70%). 5. escape: - mule deer stot : bounces on all 4 feet, good for steep broken ground (85%). - white tail flags and gallops to cover (85%). - mule deer often stop and look back from about 150 to 300 m (hunter lore) (65%). !tip if a deer stares at you, do not move a finger. count to 100. most deer go back to feeding if nothing else happens. predator pressure - in the southern interior mule deer project, cougars were the top cause of death, coyotes second (85%). - wolves have come back to the thompson area (85%). - against cougars (ambush hunters), deer pick spots with long sightlines (80%). - against wolves and dogs (chase hunters), deer use rugged steep slopes (80%). - does live in family groups: more eyes and ears (85%). - what this means for you: deer bed where they can see below and smell above (tip). human pressure - pressured deer move more at night and use thicker cover (70%). - opening days and weekends push deer away from roads (tip). - deer learn sounds: a truck door slam at a pullout can clear a slope (tip). - deer return to the same home range year after year, so spooking them is not forever (85%). !lean walk 500 m or more past the road and the pullouts. park quietly. close the door with your hip. folklore vs science folklore what we know --- --- deer can't see blaze orange blaze orange looks dull to deer. motion still gives you away (75%) deer have 297 million scent receptors unmeasured folklore. nose is still their best sense (45%) bucks always bed on the top third of a slope often true, not a rule (lore) (60%) the rut makes mule deer migrate snow and cold trigger migration (85%) deer urine lures are a must out of province deer products are banned in bc (80%). wind beats lures (tip) grunt calls work all season rattling and calling work best near the rut, early november (70%) mistakes and rules !mistake - checking the wind once at the truck, then never again. - moving when a deer is staring at you. - wearing loud nylon and a jingling pack. !rule when a deer stamps, you've already lost the eyes. don't lose the ears too. freeze. !rule the deer never makes a mistake with its nose. make yours with the wind in your favour. sources - jacobs and others 1994, deer colour vision (cone peaks), via research notes, checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - d'angelo and others 2007, deer hearing (https://wildlife.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2193/2006-326), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - stewart and others 2002, deer activity timing (https://academic.oup.com/jmammal/article/84/3/1076/905056), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - monteith and others 2011, what triggers migration (https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1890/es10-00096.1), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - bc wildlife federation, southern interior mule deer project (https://bcwf.bc.ca/initiatives/mule-deer-project/), checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - lingle 2003, deer group behaviour and predators, via research notes, checked 2026-10-01 (85%) - bc chronic wasting disease page (https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/plants-animals-ecosystems/wildlife/wildlife-conservation/wildlife-health/chronic-wasting-disease/cwd-bc), search preview, checked 2026-10-01 (80%) - research notes: research/2026-10-01-deer.md . "},{"id":"trip-yukon","phase":6,"num":14,"title":"First trip: Yukon","minutes":13,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"First trip: Yukon","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Yukon is the closest big wild country, but a BC resident cannot hunt its big game alone. Know that before you book fuel.</p></aside><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p>"},{"title":"Can a BC resident hunt here","html":"<ul><li>Yes. In Yukon you are a <strong>non resident Canadian</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Small game and game birds: you may hunt <strong>without a guide</strong> on a non resident small game licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Big game: you must be guided by a registered Yukon outfitter, or by a Yukon resident who holds a <strong>special guide licence</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Deer and elk: <strong>Yukon residents only</strong>. No non resident may hunt them, even with a guide <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Your brother is in the same boat. Same licences, same guide rules.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>A friend in Whitehorse cannot just \"take you moose hunting\". They need a special guide licence won in a lottery, and they may not be paid <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Without a guide, or with one","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>BC resident may hunt</th><th>What it takes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Grouse, ptarmigan</td><td>Yes, no guide</td><td>Small game licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ducks and geese</td><td>Yes, no guide</td><td>Small game licence plus the federal <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MGBHP\">MGBHP</button> (Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Snowshoe hare, squirrel</td><td>Yes, no guide</td><td>Small game licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Moose, caribou, wood bison</td><td>Outfitter, or a Yukon resident with a special guide licence</td><td>Big game licence plus seal <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Black bear, wolf</td><td>Outfitter: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> whether a special guide may cover them</td><td>Big game licence plus seal <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Sheep, goat, grizzly, wolverine, elk</td><td>Registered outfitter only. Not allowed under a special guide licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Big game licence plus seal</td></tr><tr><td>Deer, elk</td><td>No. Residents only <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Not available</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Coyote for non residents: a $50 harvest fee is listed, guide status <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Licences, draws and fees","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Fee (CAD)</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non resident Canadian big game licence</td><td>$75 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Non resident small game licence (Canadian or alien)</td><td>$20 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Seals: moose $10, caribou $10, bison $10, black bear $5, grizzly $25</td><td>Listed on yukon.ca. May be resident pricing: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> for non residents <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Non resident harvest fees: black bear $75, wolf $75, wolverine $75, coyote $50</td><td><span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Wildlife export permit</td><td>Free at an Environment office <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Special guide licence lottery: a <strong>Yukon resident</strong> applies, not you <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Choices: moose or caribou (100 licences) or wood bison (25 licences) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Window: spring, around the first half of April in recent years. Check the page each year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Permit Hunt Authorizations (Yukon's draw hunts): resident process. Your eligibility: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to apply","html":"<ol><li>Small game licence: buy online on yukon.ca, or at an Environment office or vendor <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Bring photo ID. Yukon asks for identity documents to issue a licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Ducks and geese: your federal <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MGBHP\">MGBHP</button> from BC is valid across Canada <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Big game: book a registered outfitter, or have a Yukon resident friend enter the special guide lottery in spring <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Read the current Yukon hunting regulations summary before you go. Seasons and subzones change <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Best first trip for you","html":"<aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Drive the Alaska Highway in September with your brother and the 12 gauge. Grouse and ptarmigan on the small game licence, ducks on the federal permit. No guide, no draw, almost no fees. Moose waits until a Yukon friend wins a special guide licence, or you save for an outfitter.</p></aside><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Estimate (CAD)</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Small game licence, each</td><td>$20 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Official fee</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel, Heffley Creek to Whitehorse and back</td><td>$900 to $1,100</td><td>Estimate: about 2,300 km each way, Assumption: $0.22 per km</td></tr><tr><td>Camping and food, 7 days, two people</td><td>$500 to $800</td><td>Estimate</td></tr><tr><td>Total, split two ways</td><td>$750 to $1,000 each</td><td>Estimate, opinion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>An outfitted moose hunt is a different budget. Ask three outfitters for quotes. Not estimated here.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Taking firearms there","html":"<ul><li>No border. Yukon is Canada. Your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"PAL\">PAL</button> (Possession and Acquisition Licence) covers you <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Transport rules are federal and the same as at home: unloaded, and treated as the RCMP (Royal Canadian Mounted Police) transport rules require <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Yukon is bear country. A loaded firearm in camp is still a storage question. Follow the RCMP storage rules <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Bringing meat, hides and antlers home","html":"<h3>Yukon's export rule</h3><ul><li>You need a <strong>wildlife export permit</strong> before you remove or ship wildlife parts from Yukon, including antlers or horns you find <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Exception: up to 20 kg (44 lb) of processed big game meat needs no permit <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Apply in person at a Department of Environment office. Free <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Birds and small game: the permit page talks about wildlife parts generally. Ask at the office: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li></ul><h3>BC's import rule (CWD)</h3><ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) drives BC's rule. From the 2026 to 2028 Synopsis, page 12, It's Unlawful item 50 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>:</li><li>\"To possess any part of a Cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of British Columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into British Columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></li><li>Item 51 bans using any out of province deer, elk, moose or caribou part or derivative, such as scents, for hunting in BC <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer, elk, moose or caribou from outside BC</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">BC synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, item 50</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May enter BC</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Edible meat of:</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">four quarters</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">loins</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">neck and ribs</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide, antlers, skull plate</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">only if cleaned of</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">ALL tissue before</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">crossing into BC</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"210\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6ddd0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"298\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stays behind</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Head and brain</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Spinal column</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Eyes, lymph nodes</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Guts and organs</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Bone in neck or back</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Uncleaned skull cap</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide with flesh on it</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"282\" width=\"372\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bone out the meat and clean the skull plate before you leave the kill area</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><h3>Yukon's own import rule</h3><ul><li>Yukon bans bringing in cervid carcasses or parts except a cleaned skull cap with antlers, cleaned teeth, meat detached from head and backbone, finished mounts and tanned hides <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>So do not carry BC deer bones north with you either.</li></ul><ul><li>CFIA (Canadian Food Inspection Agency): no national border is crossed, so no CFIA step. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> if you ever ship meat commercially.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Blaze orange rules","html":"<ul><li>Yukon's checklists list hunter orange as recommended gear, and its safety guide says bright orange helps other hunters see you <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>A legal requirement to wear it was not found in the search previews: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the current regulations summary.</li><li>My lean: wear an orange hat and vest anyway. Costs nothing and the rule in BC may change too.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Before you go","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"trip-yukon-prep\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-yukon-prep:0\"><span>Read the current Yukon hunting regulations summary</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-yukon-prep:1\"><span>Buy non resident small game licence online</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-yukon-prep:2\"><span>Federal bird permit saved on phone</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-yukon-prep:3\"><span>PAL card and photo ID in wallet</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-yukon-prep:4\"><span>Non toxic shot for ducks, lead is fine for grouse</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-yukon-prep:5\"><span>Plan the export permit stop at an Environment office</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-yukon-prep:6\"><span>Bone out and clean any meat before crossing back into BC</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>The further you drive, the simpler the hunt should be. Birds first, moose later.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Driving 2,300 km expecting to hunt moose with a Whitehorse friend who has no special guide licence.</li><li>Buying a deer tag plan. Deer are residents only in Yukon.</li><li>Leaving the export permit for the morning you drive home. Offices keep office hours.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Grouse along the Alaska Highway gravel in the first hour of light. Walk the edges, 12 gauge, improved cylinder, 7.5 shot. Watch for traffic and the road allowance rules: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> Yukon's road rules.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://yukon.ca/en/outdoor-recreation-and-wildlife/hunting-and-trapping/get-yukon-hunting-licence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Get a Yukon hunting licence</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: licence fees, who needs a guide, deer residents only.</li><li><a href=\"https://yukon.ca/en/special-guide-licence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Special guide licence</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: lottery, species, no payment.</li><li><a href=\"https://yukon.ca/en/outdoor-recreation-and-wildlife/hunting-and-trapping/hunting-deer-yukon\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting deer in the Yukon</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://yukon.ca/en/wildlife-parts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">How to buy, sell or export wildlife parts</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: export permit, 20 kg exception.</li><li><a href=\"https://yukon.ca/en/yukon-hunting-regulations-summary\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yukon hunting regulations summary</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://yukon.ca/en/wildlife-health\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yukon wildlife health and CWD</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: import rule.</li><li><a href=\"https://yukon.ca/sites/default/files/env/env-basic-hunting-checklist.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yukon basic hunting checklist</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hunter orange listed as gear.</li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://rcmp.ca/en/firearms/firearms-safety-training-transport-and-storage/storing-transporting-and-displaying-firearms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RCMP storing, transporting and displaying firearms</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li>Note: yukon.ca pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. Quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You and your brother want a Yukon moose. What is the cheapest legal route?","options":["Buy big game licences and go","A Yukon resident friend wins a special guide licence and guides you for free","Hire a BC guide to come along"],"answer":1,"why":"Non resident Canadians must be guided by a registered Yukon outfitter or a Yukon resident holding a special guide licence. The special guide may not be paid."},{"q":"Can you hunt mule deer in Yukon with an outfitter?","options":["Yes with a $50 seal","No, deer and elk are for Yukon residents only","Only in October"],"answer":1,"why":"Yukon limits deer and elk hunting to Yukon residents."},{"q":"You shot 30 kg of grouse and ptarmigan meat. Any paperwork to leave Yukon?","options":["None, it is food","Ask for a wildlife export permit at an Environment office","A CFIA certificate"],"answer":1,"why":"Yukon requires a wildlife export permit before wildlife parts leave the territory. The permit is free. The 20 kg exception is written for processed big game meat, so ask."}],"checklists":["trip-yukon-prep"],"text":" !why yukon is the closest big wild country, but a bc resident cannot hunt its big game alone. know that before you book fuel. study aid only. the official regulations are the law. can a bc resident hunt here - yes. in yukon you are a non resident canadian (85%). - small game and game birds: you may hunt without a guide on a non resident small game licence (85%). - big game: you must be guided by a registered yukon outfitter, or by a yukon resident who holds a special guide licence (85%). - deer and elk: yukon residents only . no non resident may hunt them, even with a guide (85%). - your brother is in the same boat. same licences, same guide rules. !warn a friend in whitehorse cannot just \"take you moose hunting\". they need a special guide licence won in a lottery, and they may not be paid (85%). without a guide, or with one animal bc resident may hunt what it takes --- --- --- grouse, ptarmigan yes, no guide small game licence (85%) ducks and geese yes, no guide small game licence plus the federal mgbhp (migratory game bird hunting permit) (80%) snowshoe hare, squirrel yes, no guide small game licence (80%) moose, caribou, wood bison outfitter, or a yukon resident with a special guide licence big game licence plus seal (85%) black bear, wolf outfitter: verify whether a special guide may cover them big game licence plus seal (80%) sheep, goat, grizzly, wolverine, elk registered outfitter only. not allowed under a special guide licence (85%) big game licence plus seal deer, elk no. residents only (85%) not available - coyote for non residents: a $50 harvest fee is listed, guide status verify (80%). licences, draws and fees item fee (cad) checked --- --- --- non resident canadian big game licence $75 (85%) 2026-10-01 non resident small game licence (canadian or alien) $20 (85%) 2026-10-01 seals: moose $10, caribou $10, bison $10, black bear $5, grizzly $25 listed on yukon.ca. may be resident pricing: verify for non residents (80%) 2026-10-01 non resident harvest fees: black bear $75, wolf $75, wolverine $75, coyote $50 (85%) 2026-10-01 wildlife export permit free at an environment office (85%) 2026-10-01 - special guide licence lottery: a yukon resident applies, not you (85%). - choices: moose or caribou (100 licences) or wood bison (25 licences) (85%). - window: spring, around the first half of april in recent years. check the page each year (80%). - permit hunt authorizations (yukon's draw hunts): resident process. your eligibility: verify. how to apply 1. small game licence: buy online on yukon.ca, or at an environment office or vendor (85%). 2. bring photo id. yukon asks for identity documents to issue a licence (85%). 3. ducks and geese: your federal mgbhp from bc is valid across canada (80%). 4. big game: book a registered outfitter, or have a yukon resident friend enter the special guide lottery in spring (85%). 5. read the current yukon hunting regulations summary before you go. seasons and subzones change (85%). best first trip for you !lean drive the alaska highway in september with your brother and the 12 gauge. grouse and ptarmigan on the small game licence, ducks on the federal permit. no guide, no draw, almost no fees. moose waits until a yukon friend wins a special guide licence, or you save for an outfitter. cost line estimate (cad) note --- --- --- small game licence, each $20 (85%) official fee fuel, heffley creek to whitehorse and back $900 to $1,100 estimate: about 2,300 km each way, assumption: $0.22 per km camping and food, 7 days, two people $500 to $800 estimate total, split two ways $750 to $1,000 each estimate, opinion - an outfitted moose hunt is a different budget. ask three outfitters for quotes. not estimated here. taking firearms there - no border. yukon is canada. your pal (possession and acquisition licence) covers you (85%). - transport rules are federal and the same as at home: unloaded, and treated as the rcmp (royal canadian mounted police) transport rules require (85%). - yukon is bear country. a loaded firearm in camp is still a storage question. follow the rcmp storage rules (80%). bringing meat, hides and antlers home yukon's export rule - you need a wildlife export permit before you remove or ship wildlife parts from yukon, including antlers or horns you find (85%). - exception: up to 20 kg (44 lb) of processed big game meat needs no permit (85%). - apply in person at a department of environment office. free (85%). - birds and small game: the permit page talks about wildlife parts generally. ask at the office: verify. bc's import rule (cwd) - cwd (chronic wasting disease) drives bc's rule. from the 2026 to 2028 synopsis, page 12, it's unlawful item 50 (99%): - \"to possess any part of a cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of british columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into british columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" (99%) - item 51 bans using any out of province deer, elk, moose or caribou part or derivative, such as scents, for hunting in bc (99%). yukon's own import rule - yukon bans bringing in cervid carcasses or parts except a cleaned skull cap with antlers, cleaned teeth, meat detached from head and backbone, finished mounts and tanned hides (85%). - so do not carry bc deer bones north with you either. - cfia (canadian food inspection agency): no national border is crossed, so no cfia step. verify if you ever ship meat commercially. blaze orange rules - yukon's checklists list hunter orange as recommended gear, and its safety guide says bright orange helps other hunters see you (80%). - a legal requirement to wear it was not found in the search previews: verify in the current regulations summary. - my lean: wear an orange hat and vest anyway. costs nothing and the rule in bc may change too. before you go !rule the further you drive, the simpler the hunt should be. birds first, moose later. !mistake - driving 2,300 km expecting to hunt moose with a whitehorse friend who has no special guide licence. - buying a deer tag plan. deer are residents only in yukon. - leaving the export permit for the morning you drive home. offices keep office hours. !field grouse along the alaska highway gravel in the first hour of light. walk the edges, 12 gauge, improved cylinder, 7.5 shot. watch for traffic and the road allowance rules: verify yukon's road rules. sources - get a yukon hunting licence (https://yukon.ca/en/outdoor-recreation-and-wildlife/hunting-and-trapping/get-yukon-hunting-licence) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: licence fees, who needs a guide, deer residents only. - special guide licence (https://yukon.ca/en/special-guide-licence) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: lottery, species, no payment. - hunting deer in the yukon (https://yukon.ca/en/outdoor-recreation-and-wildlife/hunting-and-trapping/hunting-deer-yukon) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - how to buy, sell or export wildlife parts (https://yukon.ca/en/wildlife-parts) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: export permit, 20 kg exception. - yukon hunting regulations summary (https://yukon.ca/en/yukon-hunting-regulations-summary) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - yukon wildlife health and cwd (https://yukon.ca/en/wildlife-health) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: import rule. - yukon basic hunting checklist (https://yukon.ca/sites/default/files/env/env-basic-hunting-checklist.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hunter orange listed as gear. - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01. - rcmp storing, transporting and displaying firearms (https://rcmp.ca/en/firearms/firearms-safety-training-transport-and-storage/storing-transporting-and-displaying-firearms) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - note: yukon.ca pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style. "},{"id":"trip-alberta","phase":6,"num":15,"title":"First trip: Alberta","minutes":14,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"First trip: Alberta","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Alberta is one long day's drive. Birds need no host. Deer need an Alberta friend with a hunter host licence. Plan around that one fact.</p></aside><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p>"},{"title":"Can a BC resident hunt here","html":"<ul><li>Yes. Alberta calls you a <strong>non resident (Canadian)</strong>: a Canadian citizen who lived in Canada the 12 months before the hunt <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Official wording: \"Anyone who is a non resident or a non resident alien hunter must be accompanied by either a hunter host or an outfitter guide if hunting big game, wolf, or coyote.\" (85%, quote shown without hyphens)</li><li>Game birds are not on that list. Upland birds and waterfowl: no host, no outfitter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Your brother is also a non resident. One hunter host may host at most 2 hunters a year, so one Alberta friend can cover you both <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Without a guide, or with one","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>BC resident may hunt</th><th>What it takes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ducks, geese, grouse, partridge, pheasant</td><td>Yes, no host</td><td>WIN card, Wildlife Certificate, Game Bird Licence, pheasant licence if pheasant, federal <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MGBHP\">MGBHP</button> (Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit) for ducks and geese <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>White tailed deer, mule deer (antlered), black bear</td><td>With a hunter host or outfitter</td><td>Licence from a private issuer, hunter host must hold a Hunter Host Licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Antlered elk, antlered moose, cougar</td><td>With a hunter host or outfitter</td><td>Same <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Wolf, coyote</td><td>With a hunter host or outfitter</td><td>Wolf and coyote licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Fee: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td></tr><tr><td>Draw only species (antlerless, sheep, pronghorn)</td><td>Draw with a resident partner, then hunt with a host</td><td>See draws below <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>A hunter host is an Alberta resident, usually a friend or relative, who is <strong>not paid</strong> to come along <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The host needs a WIN (Wildlife Identification Number) card and a Hunter Host Licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Licences, draws and fees","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Fee (CAD, before 5% GST)</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WIN card activation, one time</td><td>$10 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Wildlife Certificate, non resident Canadian</td><td>$35 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Game Bird Licence, non resident Canadian</td><td>$77 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>White tailed deer licence, non resident Canadian</td><td>$193 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Mule deer licence, non resident Canadian</td><td>$193 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Black bear licence, non resident Canadian</td><td>$110 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Hunter Host Licence (your Alberta friend buys it)</td><td>$19 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Pheasant licence, non resident</td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>GST (Goods and Services Tax) of 5% is extra. Outfitters may add service fees <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Draws: \"Only Residents and, to a lesser extent, Non residents (Canadians) are eligible\" (85%, quote shown without hyphens).</li><li>A non resident Canadian must apply <strong>with at least one Alberta resident</strong> in the draw, except the non resident trophy sheep draw <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>If drawn, you still hunt with a hunter host <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to apply","html":"<ol><li>Get a WIN card online at mywildalberta.ca or from a licence issuer. One time fee, never expires <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Buy the Wildlife Certificate, then the Game Bird Licence, online or from a private licence issuer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Deer: your Alberta friend buys the Hunter Host Licence first. Then you buy the deer licence from a private issuer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Draws open in late spring. Check the draw booklet for the exact window each year: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> dates.</li><li>Read the Alberta Guide to Hunting Regulations. It is updated every July <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Best first trip for you","html":"<aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>October, four days, southern or central Alberta grain country with your brother and the 12 gauge. Ducks and geese in the morning, sharp tailed grouse and Hungarian partridge after lunch. No host needed. If an Alberta friend buys a $19 host licence, add a white tailed deer licence and make it a November trip instead.</p></aside><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Estimate (CAD)</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WIN, Wildlife Certificate, Game Bird Licence, each</td><td>$122 plus GST <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Official fees</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel, Heffley Creek to Lethbridge area and back</td><td>$350 to $400</td><td>Estimate: about 800 km each way, Assumption: $0.22 per km</td></tr><tr><td>Motel or camping and food, 4 days, two people</td><td>$500 to $900</td><td>Estimate</td></tr><tr><td>Total, split two ways</td><td>$550 to $800 each</td><td>Estimate, opinion</td></tr><tr><td>Add a deer: white tailed deer licence</td><td>$193 plus GST <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Only with a hunter host</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Access: most of that country is private farmland. Ask permission in person, weeks ahead (opinion).</li></ul>"},{"title":"Taking firearms there","html":"<ul><li>No border. Your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"PAL\">PAL</button> (Possession and Acquisition Licence) is federal and covers Alberta <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Transport rules are the same federal RCMP (Royal Canadian Mounted Police) rules as at home <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Alberta road and vehicle hunting rules differ from BC. Read the general regulations section before you load a shell near a road: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Bringing meat, hides and antlers home","html":"<h3>Alberta's export rule</h3><ul><li>No export permit is needed for a legally taken game bird, coyote, white tailed deer, mule deer, moose, elk, black bear or pronghorn if you export it within 30 days of the kill or 5 days of season close, whichever is first, you travel with it, and you carry the licence it was taken under <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Wolf is not in that list. Ask Fish and Wildlife about an export permit: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li></ul><h3>Alberta's CWD rules</h3><ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) is established in Alberta. Head submission is mandatory for deer in designated WMUs (Wildlife Management Units) in eastern Alberta <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Alberta's advice: bone out the meat, keep evidence of sex and species, and leave the rest at the kill site or in a landfill <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><h3>BC's import rule (CWD)</h3><ul><li>From the BC Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful item 50 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>:</li><li>\"To possess any part of a Cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of British Columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into British Columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></li><li>Item 51: no out of province deer, elk, moose or caribou part or derivative, such as urine scents, may be used for hunting in BC <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer, elk, moose or caribou from outside BC</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">BC synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, item 50</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May enter BC</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Edible meat of:</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">four quarters</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">loins</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">neck and ribs</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide, antlers, skull plate</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">only if cleaned of</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">ALL tissue before</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">crossing into BC</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"210\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6ddd0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"298\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stays behind</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Head and brain</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Spinal column</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Eyes, lymph nodes</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Guts and organs</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Bone in neck or back</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Uncleaned skull cap</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide with flesh on it</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"282\" width=\"372\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bone out the meat and clean the skull plate before you leave the kill area</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Evidence of sex for Alberta must stay attached until you are home. Boning out while keeping that evidence takes care: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> Alberta's exact evidence rule.</li><li>CFIA (Canadian Food Inspection Agency): no national border, no CFIA step. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> only if shipping commercially.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Blaze orange rules","html":"<ul><li>Alberta: \"High visibility clothing has not been required in practice for many years in Alberta, and this provision has now been formally repealed.\" (80%, search preview of Alberta regulations page)</li><li>Bright colours are recommended, not required <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Grain field waterfowl hunting is done in camouflage. Deer hunts: wear orange anyway. Opinion.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Before you go","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"trip-alberta-prep\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alberta-prep:0\"><span>Get a WIN card online</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alberta-prep:1\"><span>Buy Wildlife Certificate and Game Bird Licence</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alberta-prep:2\"><span>Federal bird permit saved on phone</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alberta-prep:3\"><span>Ask an Alberta friend about a Hunter Host Licence if deer is the plan</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alberta-prep:4\"><span>Read the Alberta Guide to Hunting Regulations, July edition</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alberta-prep:5\"><span>Landowner permission arranged</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alberta-prep:6\"><span>Bone out and clean any deer parts before the BC line</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Birds pay for the scouting. Hunt birds this year, learn the country, and the deer hunt next year is already half planned.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Assuming a BC hunter can hunt Alberta deer alone. Big game, wolf and coyote all need a host or outfitter.</li><li>Forgetting the host must buy a Hunter Host Licence before the hunt, not after.</li><li>Driving home with a whole deer head on the tailgate. BC bans it.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Glass the stubble at last light to find where geese feed. Ask the farmer that evening. Set up before first light.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://mywildalberta.ca/hunting/non-resident-hunters/default.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Non resident hunters, mywildalberta.ca</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: host or outfitter rule, species list.</li><li><a href=\"https://mywildalberta.ca/hunting/non-resident-hunters/hunter-hosts.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunter hosts</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: 2 hunters per host, no fee.</li><li><a href=\"https://mywildalberta.ca/buy-licences/hunting-licences-fees/default.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting licences and fees</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: fees.</li><li><a href=\"https://mywildalberta.ca/buy-licences/hunting-licences-fees/game-bird-hunting-licences-permits/non-resident-canadians-and-aliens.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Game bird licences, non residents</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://mywildalberta.ca/buy-licences/win.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WIN card</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://mywildalberta.ca/hunting/hunting-draws/default.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting draws</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: eligibility.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.alberta.ca/exporting-wildlife-from-alberta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exporting wildlife from Alberta</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.alberta.ca/chronic-wasting-disease-information-for-hunters\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CWD information for hunters, alberta.ca</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://mywildalberta.ca/hunting/regulations/default.aspx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alberta regulations page</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: high visibility clothing repealed.</li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01.</li><li>Note: Alberta pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. Quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You and your brother want to shoot ducks near Brooks. Who must come along?","options":["A hunter host","An outfitter","Nobody. Game birds need no host or outfitter"],"answer":2,"why":"Alberta's host or outfitter rule applies to big game, wolf and coyote. Game birds are not on the list."},{"q":"What does your Alberta friend need before hosting you on a deer hunt?","options":["Nothing, he is a resident","A WIN card and a Hunter Host Licence","An outfitter permit"],"answer":1,"why":"A hunter host must be an adult Alberta resident with a WIN card and a Hunter Host Licence, and may host at most 2 hunters a year."},{"q":"Which deer parts may cross into BC from Alberta?","options":["Whole carcass if under 24 hours old","Meat of the four quarters, loins, neck and ribs, plus hide, antlers and skull plate cleaned of all tissue","Anything if the head was tested"],"answer":1,"why":"BC Synopsis page 12, item 50. The brain and spine stay in Alberta."}],"checklists":["trip-alberta-prep"],"text":" !why alberta is one long day's drive. birds need no host. deer need an alberta friend with a hunter host licence. plan around that one fact. study aid only. the official regulations are the law. can a bc resident hunt here - yes. alberta calls you a non resident (canadian) : a canadian citizen who lived in canada the 12 months before the hunt (85%). - official wording: \"anyone who is a non resident or a non resident alien hunter must be accompanied by either a hunter host or an outfitter guide if hunting big game, wolf, or coyote.\" (85%, quote shown without hyphens) - game birds are not on that list. upland birds and waterfowl: no host, no outfitter (85%). - your brother is also a non resident. one hunter host may host at most 2 hunters a year, so one alberta friend can cover you both (85%). without a guide, or with one animal bc resident may hunt what it takes --- --- --- ducks, geese, grouse, partridge, pheasant yes, no host win card, wildlife certificate, game bird licence, pheasant licence if pheasant, federal mgbhp (migratory game bird hunting permit) for ducks and geese (85%) white tailed deer, mule deer (antlered), black bear with a hunter host or outfitter licence from a private issuer, hunter host must hold a hunter host licence (85%) antlered elk, antlered moose, cougar with a hunter host or outfitter same (85%) wolf, coyote with a hunter host or outfitter wolf and coyote licence (85%). fee: verify draw only species (antlerless, sheep, pronghorn) draw with a resident partner, then hunt with a host see draws below (85%) - a hunter host is an alberta resident, usually a friend or relative, who is not paid to come along (85%). - the host needs a win (wildlife identification number) card and a hunter host licence (85%). licences, draws and fees item fee (cad, before 5% gst) checked --- --- --- win card activation, one time $10 (85%) 2026-10-01 wildlife certificate, non resident canadian $35 (85%) 2026-10-01 game bird licence, non resident canadian $77 (85%) 2026-10-01 white tailed deer licence, non resident canadian $193 (85%) 2026-10-01 mule deer licence, non resident canadian $193 (85%) 2026-10-01 black bear licence, non resident canadian $110 (85%) 2026-10-01 hunter host licence (your alberta friend buys it) $19 (85%) 2026-10-01 pheasant licence, non resident verify 2026-10-01 - gst (goods and services tax) of 5% is extra. outfitters may add service fees (85%). - draws: \"only residents and, to a lesser extent, non residents (canadians) are eligible\" (85%, quote shown without hyphens). - a non resident canadian must apply with at least one alberta resident in the draw, except the non resident trophy sheep draw (85%). - if drawn, you still hunt with a hunter host (85%). how to apply 1. get a win card online at mywildalberta.ca or from a licence issuer. one time fee, never expires (85%). 2. buy the wildlife certificate, then the game bird licence, online or from a private licence issuer (85%). 3. deer: your alberta friend buys the hunter host licence first. then you buy the deer licence from a private issuer (85%). 4. draws open in late spring. check the draw booklet for the exact window each year: verify dates. 5. read the alberta guide to hunting regulations. it is updated every july (85%). best first trip for you !lean october, four days, southern or central alberta grain country with your brother and the 12 gauge. ducks and geese in the morning, sharp tailed grouse and hungarian partridge after lunch. no host needed. if an alberta friend buys a $19 host licence, add a white tailed deer licence and make it a november trip instead. cost line estimate (cad) note --- --- --- win, wildlife certificate, game bird licence, each $122 plus gst (85%) official fees fuel, heffley creek to lethbridge area and back $350 to $400 estimate: about 800 km each way, assumption: $0.22 per km motel or camping and food, 4 days, two people $500 to $900 estimate total, split two ways $550 to $800 each estimate, opinion add a deer: white tailed deer licence $193 plus gst (85%) only with a hunter host - access: most of that country is private farmland. ask permission in person, weeks ahead (opinion). taking firearms there - no border. your pal (possession and acquisition licence) is federal and covers alberta (85%). - transport rules are the same federal rcmp (royal canadian mounted police) rules as at home (85%). - alberta road and vehicle hunting rules differ from bc. read the general regulations section before you load a shell near a road: verify. bringing meat, hides and antlers home alberta's export rule - no export permit is needed for a legally taken game bird, coyote, white tailed deer, mule deer, moose, elk, black bear or pronghorn if you export it within 30 days of the kill or 5 days of season close, whichever is first, you travel with it, and you carry the licence it was taken under (85%). - wolf is not in that list. ask fish and wildlife about an export permit: verify. alberta's cwd rules - cwd (chronic wasting disease) is established in alberta. head submission is mandatory for deer in designated wmus (wildlife management units) in eastern alberta (85%). - alberta's advice: bone out the meat, keep evidence of sex and species, and leave the rest at the kill site or in a landfill (85%). bc's import rule (cwd) - from the bc synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful item 50 (99%): - \"to possess any part of a cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of british columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into british columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" (99%) - item 51: no out of province deer, elk, moose or caribou part or derivative, such as urine scents, may be used for hunting in bc (99%). - evidence of sex for alberta must stay attached until you are home. boning out while keeping that evidence takes care: verify alberta's exact evidence rule. - cfia (canadian food inspection agency): no national border, no cfia step. verify only if shipping commercially. blaze orange rules - alberta: \"high visibility clothing has not been required in practice for many years in alberta, and this provision has now been formally repealed.\" (80%, search preview of alberta regulations page) - bright colours are recommended, not required (80%). - grain field waterfowl hunting is done in camouflage. deer hunts: wear orange anyway. opinion. before you go !rule birds pay for the scouting. hunt birds this year, learn the country, and the deer hunt next year is already half planned. !mistake - assuming a bc hunter can hunt alberta deer alone. big game, wolf and coyote all need a host or outfitter. - forgetting the host must buy a hunter host licence before the hunt, not after. - driving home with a whole deer head on the tailgate. bc bans it. !field glass the stubble at last light to find where geese feed. ask the farmer that evening. set up before first light. sources - non resident hunters, mywildalberta.ca (https://mywildalberta.ca/hunting/non-resident-hunters/default.aspx) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: host or outfitter rule, species list. - hunter hosts (https://mywildalberta.ca/hunting/non-resident-hunters/hunter-hosts.aspx) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: 2 hunters per host, no fee. - hunting licences and fees (https://mywildalberta.ca/buy-licences/hunting-licences-fees/default.aspx) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: fees. - game bird licences, non residents (https://mywildalberta.ca/buy-licences/hunting-licences-fees/game-bird-hunting-licences-permits/non-resident-canadians-and-aliens.aspx) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - win card (https://mywildalberta.ca/buy-licences/win.aspx) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - hunting draws (https://mywildalberta.ca/hunting/hunting-draws/default.aspx) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: eligibility. - exporting wildlife from alberta (https://www.alberta.ca/exporting-wildlife-from-alberta) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - cwd information for hunters, alberta.ca (https://www.alberta.ca/chronic-wasting-disease-information-for-hunters) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - alberta regulations page (https://mywildalberta.ca/hunting/regulations/default.aspx) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: high visibility clothing repealed. - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01. - note: alberta pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style. "},{"id":"trip-saskatchewan","phase":6,"num":16,"title":"First trip: Saskatchewan","minutes":14,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"First trip: Saskatchewan","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Saskatchewan lets a Canadian resident hunt white tailed deer and black bear with no guide. The deer licence is a draw, and the draw closes in May. Miss May, miss the year.</p></aside><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p>"},{"title":"Can a BC resident hunt here","html":"<ul><li>Yes. You are a <strong>Canadian resident</strong>: principal residence in Canada, citizen or 12 months resident <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>No guide or outfitter is needed for white tailed deer, black bear or game birds on Canadian resident licences <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Big game licences come in \"non guided\" and \"guided\" versions. Guided costs more and comes through an outfitter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Mule deer, elk, moose and pronghorn: the Big Game Draw is for Saskatchewan residents. Your eligibility for those: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>, assume no.</li><li>Your brother applies on his own HAL (Hunting, Angling and Trapping Licence) account. One account each.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Without a guide, or with one","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>BC resident may hunt</th><th>What it takes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>White tailed deer</td><td>Yes, no guide</td><td>Canadian Resident White tailed Deer Draw, then a $180 licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Black bear</td><td>Yes, no guide</td><td>Canadian resident black bear licence, non guided $120 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ducks, geese, grouse, partridge</td><td>Yes, no guide</td><td>Canadian resident game bird licence $100, federal <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MGBHP\">MGBHP</button> (Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit) for ducks and geese <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Coyote</td><td>Likely yes</td><td>Licence or exemption for Canadian residents: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td></tr><tr><td>Mule deer, moose, elk, pronghorn</td><td>Saskatchewan resident draw</td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>, likely not open to you</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"title":"Draw or over the counter","html":"<ul><li>Official wording: \"Canadian residents can enter the draw for a licence to hunt white tailed deer.\" (85%, quote shown without hyphens)</li><li>\"Saskatchewan residents do not need to apply in the white tailed deer draw. They may purchase a regular white tailed deer licence beginning August 1.\" (85%, quote shown without hyphens)</li><li>So for you the deer licence is <strong>draw</strong>. For a Saskatchewan resident it is over the counter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Leftover Canadian resident deer licences after the draw: not found in the official previews. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the draw page in June.</li><li>Black bear and game bird licences for Canadian residents: buy them in your HAL account, no draw <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Licences, draws and fees","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Fee (CAD)</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wildlife Habitat Certificate, needed first</td><td>$20 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Canadian Resident Draw white tailed deer licence</td><td>$180 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Canadian resident black bear, non guided</td><td>$120 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Canadian resident game bird licence</td><td>$100 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Guided big game licence (through an outfitter)</td><td>$240 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Draw application fee</td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Draw window: May 1 to 4 pm on the last Thursday in May. No late or changed applications <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Results in your HAL account in June. Successful hunters buy the licence from August 1 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The Canadian Resident White tailed Deer Draw Supplement lists zones and quotas each year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to apply","html":"<ol><li>Open a HAL account on saskatchewan.ca. One per hunter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Buy the Wildlife Habitat Certificate <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>In May, enter the Canadian Resident White tailed Deer Draw and pick zones from the supplement <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>In June, check results. From August 1, buy the deer licence if drawn <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Any time in season: buy the game bird licence, and the black bear licence for a spring or fall bear hunt <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Read the current Summary of Hunting Regulations for zone dates and the blaze orange rule <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Best first trip for you","html":"<aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Enter the deer draw next May with your brother. If drawn, hunt white tailed deer in November with the Tikka .308, no guide, in a zone the supplement shows has quota. This year, go in October with the 12 gauge for ducks, geese and sharp tailed grouse on the $100 game bird licence to learn the ground.</p></aside><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Estimate (CAD)</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Habitat Certificate and game bird licence, each</td><td>$120 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Official fees</td></tr><tr><td>Deer year: Habitat Certificate and deer licence, each</td><td>$200 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Official fees, if drawn</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel, Heffley Creek to Saskatoon area and back</td><td>$600 to $700</td><td>Estimate: about 1,400 km each way, Assumption: $0.22 per km</td></tr><tr><td>Motel and food, 6 days, two people</td><td>$900 to $1,400</td><td>Estimate</td></tr><tr><td>Total bird trip, split two ways</td><td>$900 to $1,200 each</td><td>Estimate, opinion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Many Saskatchewan deer hunters hunt private land with permission or Wildlife Habitat Protection lands. Ask the local office which is which: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Taking firearms there","html":"<ul><li>No border. Your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"PAL\">PAL</button> (Possession and Acquisition Licence) covers you <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Federal RCMP (Royal Canadian Mounted Police) transport rules apply as at home <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Saskatchewan has its own rules on loaded firearms in vehicles and on road allowances. Read the summary before the trip: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> specifics.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Bringing meat, hides and antlers home","html":"<h3>Saskatchewan's export rule</h3><ul><li>No general hunter export permit was found on saskatchewan.ca. Ask the Inquiry Centre, 1 800 567 4224 (85% that the number is official). Export permit need: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Saskatchewan's own advice to its hunters: bring back only low risk parts, \"deboned meat, antlers, clean skull plate, hide, finished taxidermy products\" with no spine or head attached <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><h3>Saskatchewan's CWD rules</h3><ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) is widespread in Saskatchewan. Mandatory testing applies in select zones from the 2025 season <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Field dress and bone out near the kill, or move it no more than 80 km before you do <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Carcass disposal bins are free for hunters <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><h3>BC's import rule (CWD)</h3><ul><li>From the BC Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful item 50 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>:</li><li>\"To possess any part of a Cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of British Columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into British Columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></li><li>Item 51: no out of province cervid part or derivative, such as scents, may be used to hunt in BC <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer, elk, moose or caribou from outside BC</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">BC synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, item 50</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May enter BC</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Edible meat of:</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">four quarters</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">loins</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">neck and ribs</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide, antlers, skull plate</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">only if cleaned of</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">ALL tissue before</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">crossing into BC</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"210\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6ddd0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"298\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stays behind</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Head and brain</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Spinal column</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Eyes, lymph nodes</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Guts and organs</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Bone in neck or back</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Uncleaned skull cap</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide with flesh on it</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"282\" width=\"372\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bone out the meat and clean the skull plate before you leave the kill area</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>You drive home through Alberta. Alberta bans nothing you are carrying if the meat is boned out, but check its transport rules: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>CFIA (Canadian Food Inspection Agency): no national border, no CFIA step. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> only if shipping commercially.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Blaze orange rules","html":"<ul><li>Official wording for big game with a rifle: hunters \"must wear a vest (an outer garment that covers the torso) of scarlet, bright yellow, blaze orange or white or any combination of these colours.\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>A CSA (Canadian Standards Association) labelled class 2 vest or class 3 coveralls also count <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Head covering: older rules required one. Current wording: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the summary.</li><li>My lean: orange vest and orange toque for deer. Camouflage is fine for birds.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Before you go","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"trip-sask-prep\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-sask-prep:0\"><span>Open a HAL account, one each</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-sask-prep:1\"><span>Buy the Wildlife Habitat Certificate</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-sask-prep:2\"><span>Enter the deer draw in May, pick zones from the supplement</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-sask-prep:3\"><span>Check draw results in June, buy licence from August 1</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-sask-prep:4\"><span>Buy the game bird licence for the October trip</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-sask-prep:5\"><span>Orange vest packed</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-sask-prep:6\"><span>Bone out meat and clean the skull plate before leaving the zone</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Set a phone alarm for May 1. The deer draw is the one date that decides the whole year.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Waiting for August to think about deer. The Canadian resident draw closed in May.</li><li>Assuming you can buy the deer licence over the counter like a Saskatchewan resident.</li><li>Dragging a whole deer across two provinces. Bone it out in the zone.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>White tails on the prairie bed in sloughs and bluffs. Still hunt the downwind edge at first light, glass each bluff before you step into it.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/parks-culture-heritage-and-sport/hunting-trapping-and-angling/hunting/apply-for-canadian-residents-white-tailed-deer-draw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Enter the Canadian Resident White tailed Deer Draw</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: draw, dates, $180, August 1.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/parks-culture-heritage-and-sport/hunting-trapping-and-angling/hunting/buy-a-hunting-licence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting licences, saskatchewan.ca</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: fees, residency.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/parks-culture-heritage-and-sport/hunting-trapping-and-angling/hunting/apply-for-the-big-game-draw\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Big Game Draw</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://publications.saskatchewan.ca/api/v1/products/126433/formats/148294/download\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2025 to 2026 Summary of Hunting Regulations</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: blaze orange wording.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/environment-public-health-and-safety/wildlife-issues/fish-and-wildlife-diseases/chronic-wasting-disease/what-hunters-can-do\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CWD hunting best practices</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/environment-public-health-and-safety/wildlife-issues/fish-and-wildlife-diseases/chronic-wasting-disease/carcass-disposal-options\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CWD carcass disposal options</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2025/august/18/province-initiates-mandatory-testing-for-cwd-and-bovine-tuberculosis-in-select-zones\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mandatory CWD testing news release, August 2025</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01.</li><li>Note: Saskatchewan pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. Quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"A BC resident wants a Saskatchewan white tailed deer licence. How?","options":["Buy it over the counter from August 1","Enter the Canadian Resident draw by the last Thursday in May, buy the licence if drawn","Book an outfitter, it is the only way"],"answer":1,"why":"Saskatchewan residents buy over the counter. Canadian residents enter the draw in May and buy from August 1 if successful."},{"q":"What must you wear to hunt deer with a rifle in Saskatchewan?","options":["Nothing special","A vest of scarlet, bright yellow, blaze orange or white, or a CSA class 2 or 3 garment","Full camouflage"],"answer":1,"why":"The summary requires a high visibility vest in those colours when hunting big game with a rifle."},{"q":"Which of these is legal to carry into BC from your Saskatchewan buck?","options":["The whole head for a mount","Boned out meat and a skull plate cleaned of all tissue","The spine for soup"],"answer":1,"why":"BC Synopsis page 12, item 50 allows only the edible meat of four quarters, loins, neck and ribs, plus hide, antlers and skull plate cleaned of all tissue."}],"checklists":["trip-sask-prep"],"text":" !why saskatchewan lets a canadian resident hunt white tailed deer and black bear with no guide. the deer licence is a draw, and the draw closes in may. miss may, miss the year. study aid only. the official regulations are the law. can a bc resident hunt here - yes. you are a canadian resident : principal residence in canada, citizen or 12 months resident (85%). - no guide or outfitter is needed for white tailed deer, black bear or game birds on canadian resident licences (85%). - big game licences come in \"non guided\" and \"guided\" versions. guided costs more and comes through an outfitter (85%). - mule deer, elk, moose and pronghorn: the big game draw is for saskatchewan residents. your eligibility for those: verify, assume no. - your brother applies on his own hal (hunting, angling and trapping licence) account. one account each. without a guide, or with one animal bc resident may hunt what it takes --- --- --- white tailed deer yes, no guide canadian resident white tailed deer draw, then a $180 licence (85%) black bear yes, no guide canadian resident black bear licence, non guided $120 (85%) ducks, geese, grouse, partridge yes, no guide canadian resident game bird licence $100, federal mgbhp (migratory game bird hunting permit) for ducks and geese (85%) coyote likely yes licence or exemption for canadian residents: verify mule deer, moose, elk, pronghorn saskatchewan resident draw verify, likely not open to you draw or over the counter - official wording: \"canadian residents can enter the draw for a licence to hunt white tailed deer.\" (85%, quote shown without hyphens) - \"saskatchewan residents do not need to apply in the white tailed deer draw. they may purchase a regular white tailed deer licence beginning august 1.\" (85%, quote shown without hyphens) - so for you the deer licence is draw . for a saskatchewan resident it is over the counter (85%). - leftover canadian resident deer licences after the draw: not found in the official previews. verify on the draw page in june. - black bear and game bird licences for canadian residents: buy them in your hal account, no draw (85%). licences, draws and fees item fee (cad) checked --- --- --- wildlife habitat certificate, needed first $20 (85%) 2026-10-01 canadian resident draw white tailed deer licence $180 (85%) 2026-10-01 canadian resident black bear, non guided $120 (85%) 2026-10-01 canadian resident game bird licence $100 (85%) 2026-10-01 guided big game licence (through an outfitter) $240 (85%) 2026-10-01 draw application fee verify 2026-10-01 - draw window: may 1 to 4 pm on the last thursday in may. no late or changed applications (85%). - results in your hal account in june. successful hunters buy the licence from august 1 (85%). - the canadian resident white tailed deer draw supplement lists zones and quotas each year (85%). how to apply 1. open a hal account on saskatchewan.ca. one per hunter (85%). 2. buy the wildlife habitat certificate (85%). 3. in may, enter the canadian resident white tailed deer draw and pick zones from the supplement (85%). 4. in june, check results. from august 1, buy the deer licence if drawn (85%). 5. any time in season: buy the game bird licence, and the black bear licence for a spring or fall bear hunt (85%). 6. read the current summary of hunting regulations for zone dates and the blaze orange rule (85%). best first trip for you !lean enter the deer draw next may with your brother. if drawn, hunt white tailed deer in november with the tikka .308, no guide, in a zone the supplement shows has quota. this year, go in october with the 12 gauge for ducks, geese and sharp tailed grouse on the $100 game bird licence to learn the ground. cost line estimate (cad) note --- --- --- habitat certificate and game bird licence, each $120 (85%) official fees deer year: habitat certificate and deer licence, each $200 (85%) official fees, if drawn fuel, heffley creek to saskatoon area and back $600 to $700 estimate: about 1,400 km each way, assumption: $0.22 per km motel and food, 6 days, two people $900 to $1,400 estimate total bird trip, split two ways $900 to $1,200 each estimate, opinion - many saskatchewan deer hunters hunt private land with permission or wildlife habitat protection lands. ask the local office which is which: verify. taking firearms there - no border. your pal (possession and acquisition licence) covers you (85%). - federal rcmp (royal canadian mounted police) transport rules apply as at home (85%). - saskatchewan has its own rules on loaded firearms in vehicles and on road allowances. read the summary before the trip: verify specifics. bringing meat, hides and antlers home saskatchewan's export rule - no general hunter export permit was found on saskatchewan.ca. ask the inquiry centre, 1 800 567 4224 (85% that the number is official). export permit need: verify. - saskatchewan's own advice to its hunters: bring back only low risk parts, \"deboned meat, antlers, clean skull plate, hide, finished taxidermy products\" with no spine or head attached (85%). saskatchewan's cwd rules - cwd (chronic wasting disease) is widespread in saskatchewan. mandatory testing applies in select zones from the 2025 season (85%). - field dress and bone out near the kill, or move it no more than 80 km before you do (85%). - carcass disposal bins are free for hunters (85%). bc's import rule (cwd) - from the bc synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful item 50 (99%): - \"to possess any part of a cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of british columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into british columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" (99%) - item 51: no out of province cervid part or derivative, such as scents, may be used to hunt in bc (99%). - you drive home through alberta. alberta bans nothing you are carrying if the meat is boned out, but check its transport rules: verify. - cfia (canadian food inspection agency): no national border, no cfia step. verify only if shipping commercially. blaze orange rules - official wording for big game with a rifle: hunters \"must wear a vest (an outer garment that covers the torso) of scarlet, bright yellow, blaze orange or white or any combination of these colours.\" (85%) - a csa (canadian standards association) labelled class 2 vest or class 3 coveralls also count (85%). - head covering: older rules required one. current wording: verify in the summary. - my lean: orange vest and orange toque for deer. camouflage is fine for birds. before you go !rule set a phone alarm for may 1. the deer draw is the one date that decides the whole year. !mistake - waiting for august to think about deer. the canadian resident draw closed in may. - assuming you can buy the deer licence over the counter like a saskatchewan resident. - dragging a whole deer across two provinces. bone it out in the zone. !field white tails on the prairie bed in sloughs and bluffs. still hunt the downwind edge at first light, glass each bluff before you step into it. sources - enter the canadian resident white tailed deer draw (https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/parks-culture-heritage-and-sport/hunting-trapping-and-angling/hunting/apply-for-canadian-residents-white-tailed-deer-draw) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: draw, dates, $180, august 1. - hunting licences, saskatchewan.ca (https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/parks-culture-heritage-and-sport/hunting-trapping-and-angling/hunting/buy-a-hunting-licence) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: fees, residency. - big game draw (https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/parks-culture-heritage-and-sport/hunting-trapping-and-angling/hunting/apply-for-the-big-game-draw) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - 2025 to 2026 summary of hunting regulations (https://publications.saskatchewan.ca/api/v1/products/126433/formats/148294/download) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: blaze orange wording. - cwd hunting best practices (https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/environment-public-health-and-safety/wildlife-issues/fish-and-wildlife-diseases/chronic-wasting-disease/what-hunters-can-do) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - cwd carcass disposal options (https://www.saskatchewan.ca/residents/environment-public-health-and-safety/wildlife-issues/fish-and-wildlife-diseases/chronic-wasting-disease/carcass-disposal-options) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - mandatory cwd testing news release, august 2025 (https://www.saskatchewan.ca/government/news-and-media/2025/august/18/province-initiates-mandatory-testing-for-cwd-and-bovine-tuberculosis-in-select-zones) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01. - note: saskatchewan pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style. "},{"id":"trip-manitoba","phase":6,"num":17,"title":"First trip: Manitoba","minutes":13,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"First trip: Manitoba","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Manitoba sells a Canadian resident deer licence with no draw and no guide, but only in listed areas, and it has the strictest orange rule of the five trips.</p></aside><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p>"},{"title":"Can a BC resident hunt here","html":"<ul><li>Yes. Manitoba calls you a <strong>Canadian resident</strong>: not a Manitoba resident, but a Canadian citizen or permanent resident, or someone who lived in Canada 6 consecutive months of the last 12 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The guide rule in Manitoba is written for <strong>non Canadian residents</strong>: they must hunt big game with a guide employed by the outfitter that issued the licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>As a Canadian resident you may hold Canadian resident licences for white tailed deer, general moose, conservation moose, black bear, wild turkey and game birds <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Whether every one of those is sold without an outfitter: deer and game bird yes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>, bear and moose <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Your brother: same status, his own licences.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Without a guide, or with one","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>BC resident may hunt</th><th>What it takes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>White tailed deer</td><td>Yes, no guide, in listed GHAs (Game Hunting Areas)</td><td>Canadian resident white tailed deer licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ducks, geese, grouse, partridge</td><td>Yes, no guide</td><td>Canadian resident game bird licence, federal <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MGBHP\">MGBHP</button> (Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit) for ducks and geese <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Black bear</td><td>Canadian resident general black bear licence exists <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Outfitter need and areas: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td></tr><tr><td>Moose</td><td>Canadian resident general and conservation moose licences exist <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Areas and any draw: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td></tr><tr><td>Elk</td><td>Resident draw</td><td>Assume no</td></tr><tr><td>Coyote, wolf</td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Canadian resident deer, archery: GHAs 5 to 7, 8, 10, 11, 15, 15A, 17 and 17B, August 26 to September 15 and October 14 to November 10 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Canadian resident deer, general (rifle) season dates in those GHAs: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in Schedule B of the Hunting Seasons and Bag Limits Regulation.</li><li>\"General\" licence means rifle, shotgun, muzzleloader, crossbow or bow may be used <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Licences, draws and fees","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Fee (CAD)</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Canadian resident white tailed deer licence</td><td>$184.25, fees effective April 1, 2026 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Canadian resident game bird licence</td><td>$108.00 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Wild animal export permit</td><td>$20 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Whether a hunter needs one for their own deer: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Canadian resident black bear licence</td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Canadian resident moose licences</td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>No draw for the Canadian resident deer or game bird licence was found <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Elk and moose draws are for Manitoba residents <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Manitoba sells licences through its online licensing system, elicensing <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to apply","html":"<ol><li>Create an account on Manitoba's elicensing site <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Buy the Canadian resident white tailed deer licence, or the game bird licence, online <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Read the current Manitoba Hunting Guide for GHA boundaries, dates and tags <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Check the CWD (Chronic Wasting Disease) surveillance zones and any mandatory testing before you pick a GHA: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> zones.</li><li>Plan the export step before you leave: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> if the $20 permit applies to you.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Best first trip for you","html":"<aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Manitoba is a 2,100 km drive. Make it count: November, white tailed deer with the Tikka .308 in one of the listed GHAs, you and your brother on Canadian resident licences, no guide. Scout the GHA on the map first and phone the regional office about access and CWD zones. If the rifle dates do not line up, hunt ducks and geese in October instead.</p></aside><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Estimate (CAD)</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deer licence, each</td><td>$184.25 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Official fee</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel, Heffley Creek to Winnipeg area and back</td><td>$900 to $1,000</td><td>Estimate: about 2,100 km each way, Assumption: $0.22 per km</td></tr><tr><td>Motel and food, 7 days, two people</td><td>$1,100 to $1,600</td><td>Estimate</td></tr><tr><td>Total, split two ways</td><td>$1,200 to $1,500 each</td><td>Estimate, opinion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Honest view: Saskatchewan or Alberta give a similar hunt for less driving. Manitoba makes sense if you have a friend or family there. Opinion.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Taking firearms there","html":"<ul><li>No border. Your <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"PAL\">PAL</button> (Possession and Acquisition Licence) covers Manitoba <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Federal RCMP (Royal Canadian Mounted Police) transport rules apply <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>You cross Alberta and Saskatchewan on the way. Provincial vehicle rules differ, so keep the rifle unloaded and cased the whole drive. Opinion.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Bringing meat, hides and antlers home","html":"<h3>Manitoba's export and CWD rules</h3><ul><li>Manitoba lists a $20 wild animal export permit <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Whether it applies to your own harvest: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> with the regional office.</li><li>Manitoba's CWD advice: \"Do not transport high risk parts (such as the brain, eyes and spine) outside the area where the animal was harvested.\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>Manitoba's own import ban: no deer, elk, moose or caribou from another province or state may enter Manitoba unless the head, hide, hooves, mammary glands, entrails, internal organs and spinal column are removed and left behind. Deboned or processed meat is allowed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>That ban cuts both ways on a road trip. Do not carry BC deer bones east either.</li></ul><h3>BC's import rule (CWD)</h3><ul><li>From the BC Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful item 50 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>:</li><li>\"To possess any part of a Cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of British Columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into British Columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></li><li>Item 51: no out of province cervid part or derivative may be used for hunting in BC <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer, elk, moose or caribou from outside BC</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">BC synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, item 50</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May enter BC</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Edible meat of:</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">four quarters</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">loins</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">neck and ribs</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide, antlers, skull plate</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">only if cleaned of</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">ALL tissue before</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">crossing into BC</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"210\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6ddd0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"298\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stays behind</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Head and brain</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Spinal column</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Eyes, lymph nodes</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Guts and organs</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Bone in neck or back</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Uncleaned skull cap</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide with flesh on it</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"282\" width=\"372\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bone out the meat and clean the skull plate before you leave the kill area</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>You will cross Saskatchewan and Alberta with that meat. Boned out meat and a cleaned skull plate satisfy all three provinces <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>CFIA (Canadian Food Inspection Agency): no national border, no CFIA step. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> only if shipping commercially.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Blaze orange rules","html":"<ul><li>Manitoba's General Hunting Regulation, M.R. 351/87, for anyone hunting, dressing or retrieving a big game animal or coyote, or guiding or accompanying them <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>:</li><li>A head covering \"completely hunter orange in colour (other than on the brim of a hat)\", a small crest or logo allowed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Plus \"2,580 cm² (400 in²) of material hunter orange in colour, affixed above the waist and visible from all sides\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>\"Hunter orange\" is defined by wavelength, 595 to 605 nanometres, and brightness <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Archery only exceptions: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Birds: no orange rule found, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Your brother walking with you, no rifle, still needs the orange. The rule covers anyone accompanying <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Before you go","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"trip-manitoba-prep\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-manitoba-prep:0\"><span>Create a Manitoba elicensing account, one each</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-manitoba-prep:1\"><span>Buy Canadian resident deer or game bird licence</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-manitoba-prep:2\"><span>Confirm rifle season dates for your GHA in the Hunting Guide</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-manitoba-prep:3\"><span>Orange hat plus 2,580 square cm of orange above the waist, each person</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-manitoba-prep:4\"><span>Ask the regional office about the export permit and CWD zones</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-manitoba-prep:5\"><span>Bone out meat and clean the skull plate before the Saskatchewan line</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-manitoba-prep:6\"><span>Federal bird permit saved on phone</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>When a province writes its orange rule in square centimetres, it means it. Buy the vest before the trip, not at the gas station.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Hunting deer in a GHA that is not on the Canadian resident list.</li><li>Orange vest but a camouflage hat. The hat must be completely orange.</li><li>Hauling a head home for a mount. Three provinces say no to the brain and spine.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Aspen parkland deer: still hunt the bush edges along cut grain at first light, orange on, rifle unloaded until you are off the road allowance.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.gov.mb.ca/elicensing/fees.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Manitoba licence and permit fees effective April 1, 2026</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: deer, game bird, export permit fees.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.gov.mb.ca/nrnd/fish-wildlife/wildlife/hunting-guide-licence/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Manitoba hunting guide and licence page</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: Canadian resident definition, outfitter rule for non Canadian residents.</li><li><a href=\"https://web2.gov.mb.ca/laws/regs/current/165-91.php?lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting Seasons and Bag Limits Regulation, M.R. 165/91</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: Canadian resident deer areas and archery dates, general licence meaning.</li><li><a href=\"https://web2.gov.mb.ca/laws/regs/current/351-87.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">General Hunting Regulation, M.R. 351/87</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hunter orange wording.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/pubs/fish_wildlife/huntingguide.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Manitoba Hunting Guide 2025 PDF</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://gov.mb.ca/fish-wildlife/wildlife/cwd.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CWD in Manitoba</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: high risk parts, import ban.</li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01.</li><li>Note: Manitoba pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. Quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Does a BC resident need an outfitter to hunt white tailed deer in Manitoba?","options":["Yes, all non residents do","No. The guide rule is for non Canadian residents. Canadian residents buy a deer licence for listed GHAs","Only in archery season"],"answer":1,"why":"Manitoba's outfitter and guide requirement applies to non Canadian residents. Canadian residents may buy a Canadian resident deer licence valid in listed Game Hunting Areas."},{"q":"What orange does Manitoba require for a deer hunter?","options":["Any orange hat","A completely orange head covering plus 2,580 square cm of hunter orange above the waist, visible from all sides","An orange vest only"],"answer":1,"why":"General Hunting Regulation M.R. 351/87 requires both the orange head covering and 2,580 square cm of hunter orange above the waist."},{"q":"Your brother carries no rifle and just helps drag. Does he need orange?","options":["No, he is not hunting","Yes. The rule covers anyone accompanying a big game hunter or retrieving game","Only in November"],"answer":1,"why":"The regulation names anyone who hunts, dresses or retrieves big game, or guides or otherwise accompanies such a person."}],"checklists":["trip-manitoba-prep"],"text":" !why manitoba sells a canadian resident deer licence with no draw and no guide, but only in listed areas, and it has the strictest orange rule of the five trips. study aid only. the official regulations are the law. can a bc resident hunt here - yes. manitoba calls you a canadian resident : not a manitoba resident, but a canadian citizen or permanent resident, or someone who lived in canada 6 consecutive months of the last 12 (85%). - the guide rule in manitoba is written for non canadian residents : they must hunt big game with a guide employed by the outfitter that issued the licence (85%). - as a canadian resident you may hold canadian resident licences for white tailed deer, general moose, conservation moose, black bear, wild turkey and game birds (85%). - whether every one of those is sold without an outfitter: deer and game bird yes (85%), bear and moose verify. - your brother: same status, his own licences. without a guide, or with one animal bc resident may hunt what it takes --- --- --- white tailed deer yes, no guide, in listed ghas (game hunting areas) canadian resident white tailed deer licence (85%) ducks, geese, grouse, partridge yes, no guide canadian resident game bird licence, federal mgbhp (migratory game bird hunting permit) for ducks and geese (85%) black bear canadian resident general black bear licence exists (85%) outfitter need and areas: verify moose canadian resident general and conservation moose licences exist (85%) areas and any draw: verify elk resident draw assume no coyote, wolf verify verify - canadian resident deer, archery: ghas 5 to 7, 8, 10, 11, 15, 15a, 17 and 17b, august 26 to september 15 and october 14 to november 10 (85%). - canadian resident deer, general (rifle) season dates in those ghas: verify in schedule b of the hunting seasons and bag limits regulation. - \"general\" licence means rifle, shotgun, muzzleloader, crossbow or bow may be used (85%). licences, draws and fees item fee (cad) checked --- --- --- canadian resident white tailed deer licence $184.25, fees effective april 1, 2026 (85%) 2026-10-01 canadian resident game bird licence $108.00 (85%) 2026-10-01 wild animal export permit $20 (85%). whether a hunter needs one for their own deer: verify 2026-10-01 canadian resident black bear licence verify 2026-10-01 canadian resident moose licences verify 2026-10-01 - no draw for the canadian resident deer or game bird licence was found (85%). - elk and moose draws are for manitoba residents (85%). - manitoba sells licences through its online licensing system, elicensing (85%). how to apply 1. create an account on manitoba's elicensing site (85%). 2. buy the canadian resident white tailed deer licence, or the game bird licence, online (85%). 3. read the current manitoba hunting guide for gha boundaries, dates and tags (85%). 4. check the cwd (chronic wasting disease) surveillance zones and any mandatory testing before you pick a gha: verify zones. 5. plan the export step before you leave: verify if the $20 permit applies to you. best first trip for you !lean manitoba is a 2,100 km drive. make it count: november, white tailed deer with the tikka .308 in one of the listed ghas, you and your brother on canadian resident licences, no guide. scout the gha on the map first and phone the regional office about access and cwd zones. if the rifle dates do not line up, hunt ducks and geese in october instead. cost line estimate (cad) note --- --- --- deer licence, each $184.25 (85%) official fee fuel, heffley creek to winnipeg area and back $900 to $1,000 estimate: about 2,100 km each way, assumption: $0.22 per km motel and food, 7 days, two people $1,100 to $1,600 estimate total, split two ways $1,200 to $1,500 each estimate, opinion - honest view: saskatchewan or alberta give a similar hunt for less driving. manitoba makes sense if you have a friend or family there. opinion. taking firearms there - no border. your pal (possession and acquisition licence) covers manitoba (85%). - federal rcmp (royal canadian mounted police) transport rules apply (85%). - you cross alberta and saskatchewan on the way. provincial vehicle rules differ, so keep the rifle unloaded and cased the whole drive. opinion. bringing meat, hides and antlers home manitoba's export and cwd rules - manitoba lists a $20 wild animal export permit (85%). whether it applies to your own harvest: verify with the regional office. - manitoba's cwd advice: \"do not transport high risk parts (such as the brain, eyes and spine) outside the area where the animal was harvested.\" (85%) - manitoba's own import ban: no deer, elk, moose or caribou from another province or state may enter manitoba unless the head, hide, hooves, mammary glands, entrails, internal organs and spinal column are removed and left behind. deboned or processed meat is allowed (85%). - that ban cuts both ways on a road trip. do not carry bc deer bones east either. bc's import rule (cwd) - from the bc synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful item 50 (99%): - \"to possess any part of a cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of british columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into british columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" (99%) - item 51: no out of province cervid part or derivative may be used for hunting in bc (99%). - you will cross saskatchewan and alberta with that meat. boned out meat and a cleaned skull plate satisfy all three provinces (80%). - cfia (canadian food inspection agency): no national border, no cfia step. verify only if shipping commercially. blaze orange rules - manitoba's general hunting regulation, m.r. 351/87, for anyone hunting, dressing or retrieving a big game animal or coyote, or guiding or accompanying them (85%): - a head covering \"completely hunter orange in colour (other than on the brim of a hat)\", a small crest or logo allowed (85%). - plus \"2,580 cm² (400 in²) of material hunter orange in colour, affixed above the waist and visible from all sides\" (85%). - \"hunter orange\" is defined by wavelength, 595 to 605 nanometres, and brightness (85%). - archery only exceptions: verify. birds: no orange rule found, verify. - your brother walking with you, no rifle, still needs the orange. the rule covers anyone accompanying (85%). before you go !rule when a province writes its orange rule in square centimetres, it means it. buy the vest before the trip, not at the gas station. !mistake - hunting deer in a gha that is not on the canadian resident list. - orange vest but a camouflage hat. the hat must be completely orange. - hauling a head home for a mount. three provinces say no to the brain and spine. !field aspen parkland deer: still hunt the bush edges along cut grain at first light, orange on, rifle unloaded until you are off the road allowance. sources - manitoba licence and permit fees effective april 1, 2026 (https://www.gov.mb.ca/elicensing/fees.html) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: deer, game bird, export permit fees. - manitoba hunting guide and licence page (https://www.gov.mb.ca/nrnd/fish-wildlife/wildlife/hunting-guide-licence/index.html) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: canadian resident definition, outfitter rule for non canadian residents. - hunting seasons and bag limits regulation, m.r. 165/91 (https://web2.gov.mb.ca/laws/regs/current/165-91.php?lang=en) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: canadian resident deer areas and archery dates, general licence meaning. - general hunting regulation, m.r. 351/87 (https://web2.gov.mb.ca/laws/regs/current/351-87.php) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hunter orange wording. - manitoba hunting guide 2025 pdf (https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/pubs/fish_wildlife/huntingguide.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - cwd in manitoba (https://gov.mb.ca/fish-wildlife/wildlife/cwd.html) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: high risk parts, import ban. - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01. - note: manitoba pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style. "},{"id":"trip-alaska","phase":6,"num":18,"title":"First trip: Alaska","minutes":15,"checked":"2026-10-01","steps":[{"title":"First trip: Alaska","html":"<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Alaska is the dream and the paperwork. As a Canadian you are a \"nonresident alien\" there: a guide for every big game animal, a $630 licence, and a US firearm permit before you leave home.</p></aside><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p>"},{"title":"Can a BC resident hunt here","html":"<ul><li>Yes, as a <strong>nonresident alien</strong>: not a US citizen and not living in the US <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Big game: \"A nonresident alien must be personally accompanied by an Alaska licensed guide to hunt ANY big game animal\" (85%, quote shown without hyphen).</li><li>That list: black bear, brown or grizzly bear, bison, caribou, Dall sheep, deer, elk, moose, mountain goat, muskox, wolf and wolverine <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The guide must be within 100 yards (91 m) of you when you attempt to take game <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Small game and waterfowl: no guide needed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>The \"second degree of kindred\" exemption, where a US nonresident hunts sheep, goat or brown bear with a close Alaska resident relative, does <strong>not</strong> apply to nonresident aliens <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Your brother is a nonresident alien too. Two guides, or one guide booked for two hunters: ask the outfitter.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Without a guide, or with one","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Animal</th><th>BC resident may hunt</th><th>What it takes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Grouse, ptarmigan, hares</td><td>Yes, no guide <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td><td>Nonresident alien hunting licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Ducks and geese</td><td>Yes, no guide <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td><td>Nonresident alien licence, Alaska waterfowl stamp, Federal Duck Stamp <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Black bear, deer, caribou, moose, wolf</td><td>Guide required <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Licence, locking tag, harvest ticket or permit <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Brown or grizzly bear, Dall sheep, mountain goat</td><td>Guide required <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Licence, locking tag, often a draw permit <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Bison, muskox, elk</td><td>Guide required <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Draw or registration permits <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Harvest tickets and locking tags must be in hand before the hunt. The locking tag goes on the animal <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Licences, draws and fees","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Fee (USD)</th><th>Checked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nonresident alien annual hunting licence</td><td>$630 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: brown or grizzly bear</td><td>$1,300 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: Dall sheep</td><td>$1,100 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: moose</td><td>$1,000 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: caribou</td><td>$850 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: mountain goat</td><td>$800 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: black bear</td><td>$600 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: wolverine</td><td>$500 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: deer</td><td>$400 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Nonresident alien locking tag: wolf</td><td>$100 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Alaska waterfowl stamp, nonresident alien</td><td>$10 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr><tr><td>Federal Duck Stamp (US Fish and Wildlife Service)</td><td><span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td><td>2026-10-01</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>There is no cheaper small game licence for nonresident aliens. The $60 small game licence is for US nonresidents only <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Draw permits: applications run in winter for the following season. Dates: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the Drawing Permit Hunt Supplement.</li><li>Guide fees are private and large. Ask for written quotes. Not estimated here.</li></ul>"},{"title":"How to apply","html":"<ol><li>Buy the nonresident alien hunting licence online at the Alaska Department of Fish and Game store. Do this first: ATF needs a copy <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>File ATF Form 6NIA (see firearms screen). Allow 4 to 6 weeks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Book a registered Alaska guide for any big game. The guide usually handles tags and permits with you <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Buy locking tags before the hunt. Carry harvest tickets <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Read the current Alaska Hunting Regulations for your GMU (Game Management Unit) <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ol>"},{"title":"Best first trip for you","html":"<aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Not big game, not yet. The honest first Alaska trip for two Canadians is a September road trip through Yukon to the Tok and Anchorage country for ptarmigan, grouse and ducks on the nonresident alien licence, with the paperwork done right. It teaches you the border, the licence system and the country. Save for a guided black bear or caribou after that.</p></aside><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Estimate (USD unless noted)</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nonresident alien licence, each</td><td>$630 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td><td>Official fee</td></tr><tr><td>Waterfowl stamp plus Federal Duck Stamp</td><td>$10 plus <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></td><td>Official fees</td></tr><tr><td>ATF Form 6NIA</td><td>Fee: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>, believed none</td><td>Each hunter</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel, Heffley Creek to Tok and back</td><td>CAD $1,400 to $1,600</td><td>Estimate: about 3,300 km each way, Assumption: CAD $0.22 per km</td></tr><tr><td>Camping, food and one motel night each way, 12 days, two people</td><td>CAD $1,200 to $1,800</td><td>Estimate</td></tr><tr><td>Total, split two ways</td><td>about CAD $2,300 to $2,900 each</td><td>Estimate, opinion, at roughly 1.35 CAD per USD, Assumption</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>A guided black bear trip, with flights, is a different budget again. Three quotes before you decide. Opinion.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Taking firearms there","html":"<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 560\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"560\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"ak\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#23261f\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Your rifle to Alaska and back</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"48\" width=\"320\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1. Buy your Alaska hunting licence online</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"94\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">ATF needs a copy of it with your application</text>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"110\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ak)\"/>\n\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"132\" width=\"320\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"156\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2. File ATF Form 6NIA, one per hunter</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">About 4 to 6 weeks. Approval valid 1 year</text>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"194\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"214\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ak)\"/>\n\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"216\" width=\"320\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"240\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3. Leave Canada</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Optional: CBSA card BSF407 listing serial numbers</text>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"278\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"298\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ak)\"/>\n\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"300\" width=\"320\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6ddd0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"324\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4. US border (CBP): declare the firearm</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"346\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Show approved 6NIA plus the Alaska licence</text>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"362\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"382\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ak)\"/>\n\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"384\" width=\"320\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"408\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5. Hunt. Keep 6NIA and licence on you</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"430\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Unloaded and cased in the truck is the safe habit</text>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"446\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"466\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ak)\"/>\n\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"468\" width=\"320\" height=\"62\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6ddd0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"492\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6. Back into Canada (CBSA): declare it</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"514\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Show your PAL. Non restricted re entry only</text>\n\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"550\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ammunition rides on the same 6NIA. Keep every paper in one folder.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><h3>Leaving Canada</h3><ul><li>CBSA (Canada Border Services Agency): you do not have to stop at a CBSA office when leaving with a non restricted firearm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Recommended: CBSA Form BSF407, Identification of Articles for Temporary Exportation, listing the serial number, so you can show the rifle was not bought abroad <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Formerly Form Y38 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>RCMP (Royal Canadian Mounted Police): an export permit from Global Affairs Canada \"may be required\" for firearms, parts or ammunition taken out temporarily for hunting <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Call Global Affairs, 343 203 4331, and ask: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> whether it applies to a hunter's own rifle.</li></ul><h3>Entering the US</h3><ul><li>ATF (Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives) Form 6NIA: \"any nonimmigrant alien temporarily bringing firearm(s) and/or ammunition into the United States for legitimate hunting or lawful sporting purposes must first obtain an approved ATF Form 6NIA\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The application needs a copy of a valid US state hunting licence. Your Alaska licence satisfies this <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Processing about 4 to 6 weeks. Approval valid one year <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>At the border, CBP (Customs and Border Protection): present the approved 6NIA plus the hunting licence <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The form covers the Tikka .308, the 12 gauge and the ammunition you list. One form per hunter: your brother files his own <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Form fee: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>, believed none.</li></ul><h3>Coming home</h3><ul><li>CBSA: residents may re import non restricted firearms by presenting a valid <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"PAL\">PAL</button> (Possession and Acquisition Licence) for that class <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Declare the firearms. Have the BSF407 card ready <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Leftover US ammunition coming back: declare it. Limits: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the CBSA page.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>This is the complicated part. Start the 6NIA in June for a September trip. No approved form, no rifle across the line. The guide can rent you one, but ptarmigan with a borrowed gun is not the trip you planned.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Bringing meat, hides and antlers home","html":"<h3>Alaska's export rules</h3><ul><li>All brown or grizzly and black bears must be <strong>sealed</strong> by Fish and Game before they leave Alaska <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Sheep rams from listed units must be sealed, and horns may not leave the state before sealing <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Sealing is done within 30 days of the kill, or sooner if the permit says so <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>A CITES (Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species) permit is required to ship hides, skulls, meat or other parts of bears, wolves or lynx out of the US. Issued by USFWS (US Fish and Wildlife Service) offices in Anchorage and Fairbanks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>USFWS Form 3-177, Declaration of Importation or Exportation of Fish and Wildlife, may be required for meat, hides, skulls or trophies leaving the US <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><h3>Canada's import rules</h3><ul><li>CFIA (Canadian Food Inspection Agency): a hunter harvested game carcass from the US may enter if the hunter's licence accompanies it and you bring it yourself, not by courier. No CFIA quantity limit (80%, search preview). canada.ca was not reachable: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> on the CFIA page linked below.</li><li>Wild game birds you shot in the US are permitted with the hunting permit <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Bird flu rules change fast: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> before the trip.</li><li>CBSA declares everything. Keep the Alaska licence and tags with the meat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><h3>BC's import rule (CWD)</h3><ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) has not been found in Alaska, but BC's rule does not care where the animal came from <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>From the BC Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful item 50 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>:</li><li>\"To possess any part of a Cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of British Columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into British Columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></li><li>So an Alaska caribou or Sitka deer comes home boned out, antlers on a skull plate scraped clean. Item 51 also bans using any out of province cervid part for hunting in BC <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>You also drive through Yukon, which bans cervid carcasses and parts except cleaned skull caps with antlers, cleaned teeth, meat detached from head and backbone, finished mounts and tanned hides <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer, elk, moose or caribou from outside BC</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">BC synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, item 50</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9efe0\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">May enter BC</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Edible meat of:</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">four quarters</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">loins</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">neck and ribs</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide, antlers, skull plate</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">only if cleaned of</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">ALL tissue before</text>\n  <text x=\"26\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">crossing into BC</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"210\" y=\"62\" width=\"176\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6ddd0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"298\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stays behind</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Head and brain</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Spinal column</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Eyes, lymph nodes</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Guts and organs</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"192\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Bone in neck or back</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Uncleaned skull cap</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"242\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Hide with flesh on it</text>\n\n  <rect x=\"14\" y=\"282\" width=\"372\" height=\"2\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bone out the meat and clean the skull plate before you leave the kill area</text>\n</svg>\n</figure>"},{"title":"Blaze orange rules","html":"<ul><li>\"Alaska does not require wearing hunter orange clothing, but investigators consistently have found that it reduces hunting accidents.\" (85%, Alaska hunter education page)</li><li>Some youth hunts require blaze orange vests or jackets <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>My lean: orange hat at minimum. You will be hunting the same ptarmigan slopes as locals in September.</li></ul>"},{"title":"Before you go","html":"<div class=\"checklist\" data-list=\"trip-alaska-prep\"><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:0\"><span>Buy nonresident alien hunting licence online, print it</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:1\"><span>File ATF Form 6NIA for each hunter, 4 to 6 weeks ahead</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:2\"><span>Ask Global Affairs Canada whether an export permit applies</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:3\"><span>CBSA Form BSF407 card for each firearm</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:4\"><span>Alaska waterfowl stamp and Federal Duck Stamp if hunting ducks</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:5\"><span>Guide booked in writing for any big game</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:6\"><span>Current Alaska Hunting Regulations for your GMU downloaded</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:7\"><span>Passports, PAL cards, licences and 6NIA in one folder</span></label><label class=\"check\"><input type=\"checkbox\" data-key=\"trip-alaska-prep:8\"><span>Plan the meat: bone out, clean skull plate, keep tags with the meat</span></label></div><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>Paperwork is part of the hunt. A trip that starts with a refused rifle at the border ends there.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Thinking \"nonresident\" rules apply to you. Canadians are nonresident aliens and need a guide for all big game.</li><li>Applying for the 6NIA before buying the Alaska licence. ATF wants the licence copy with the form.</li><li>Packing a caribou head for the taxidermist. Yukon and BC both say no.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Ptarmigan hold in willow above treeline in September. Walk the brush with the 12 gauge, modified choke, 6 shot, and watch for bears on every berry slope.</p></aside>"},{"title":"Sources","html":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=hunting.guidereqs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alaska big game guide requirements</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: nonresident alien rule, 100 yards, kindred.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=huntlicense.prices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alaska licence, stamp and tag prices</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: $630, tag fees, waterfowl stamp.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=license.pricinglist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alaska licence pricing list</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=waterfowlhunting.requirements\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alaska waterfowl hunting requirements</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=hunting.shipping\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shipping or transporting game from Alaska</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: sealing, CITES, Form 3-177.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=hunting.huntered\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alaska hunter education page</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hunter orange.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/static/regulations/wildliferegulations/pdfs/regulations_complete.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2026 to 2027 Alaska Hunting Regulations PDF</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.atf.gov/firearms/docs/form/form-6nia-applicationpermit-temporary-importation-firearms-and-ammunition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ATF Form 6NIA page</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.atf.gov/rules-and-regulations/form-and-information-collection-notices/application/permit-temporary-importation-firearms-and-ammunition-nonimmigrant-aliens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ATF 6NIA information collection notice</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: 4 to 6 weeks, one year, licence copy.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.help.cbp.gov/s/article/Article-1204?language=en_US\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CBP: bringing a firearm into the US for hunting</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.cbsa-asfc.gc.ca/import/iefw-iefa-eng.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CBSA: firearms and weapons border requirements</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: PAL on re entry, BSF407, no stop on exit. canada.ca blocked: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.cbsa-asfc.gc.ca/publications/dm-md/d19/d19-13-2-eng.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CBSA Memorandum D19-13-2</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01.</li><li><a href=\"https://rcmp.ca/en/firearms/importing-and-exporting/exporting\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RCMP: exporting firearms</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: export permit may be required.</li><li><a href=\"https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-safety-consumers/bring-food-personal-use\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CFIA: bringing food into Canada for personal use</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. Site blocked from this environment: <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://yukon.ca/en/wildlife-health\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yukon wildlife health and CWD</a> (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: Yukon import rule.</li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, It's Unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01.</li><li>Note: US and federal pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. Quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style. Fees in US dollars.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"You are a Canadian citizen living in BC. Can you hunt Alaska black bear with just your brother?","options":["Yes, black bear needs no guide","No. Nonresident aliens need an Alaska licensed guide for every big game animal","Yes if you have a locking tag"],"answer":1,"why":"The guide rule for US nonresidents covers only brown bear, sheep and goat. For nonresident aliens it covers all big game, black bear included."},{"q":"What is the first step to get your rifle into Alaska?","options":["Show your PAL at the US border","Buy the Alaska hunting licence, then file ATF Form 6NIA weeks ahead","Mail the rifle to the guide"],"answer":1,"why":"ATF needs a copy of a valid US state hunting licence with the 6NIA application, and processing takes about 4 to 6 weeks."},{"q":"Which Alaska caribou parts can you drive home to Heffley Creek?","options":["Whole head and cape for the taxidermist","Boned out meat and a skull plate with antlers cleaned of all tissue","Anything, CWD is not in Alaska"],"answer":1,"why":"BC Synopsis page 12 item 50 applies to any cervid killed outside BC. Yukon has a matching import ban on the drive through."}],"checklists":["trip-alaska-prep"],"text":" !why alaska is the dream and the paperwork. as a canadian you are a \"nonresident alien\" there: a guide for every big game animal, a $630 licence, and a us firearm permit before you leave home. study aid only. the official regulations are the law. can a bc resident hunt here - yes, as a nonresident alien : not a us citizen and not living in the us (85%). - big game: \"a nonresident alien must be personally accompanied by an alaska licensed guide to hunt any big game animal\" (85%, quote shown without hyphen). - that list: black bear, brown or grizzly bear, bison, caribou, dall sheep, deer, elk, moose, mountain goat, muskox, wolf and wolverine (85%). - the guide must be within 100 yards (91 m) of you when you attempt to take game (85%). - small game and waterfowl: no guide needed (80%). - the \"second degree of kindred\" exemption, where a us nonresident hunts sheep, goat or brown bear with a close alaska resident relative, does not apply to nonresident aliens (85%). - your brother is a nonresident alien too. two guides, or one guide booked for two hunters: ask the outfitter. without a guide, or with one animal bc resident may hunt what it takes --- --- --- grouse, ptarmigan, hares yes, no guide (80%) nonresident alien hunting licence (85%) ducks and geese yes, no guide (80%) nonresident alien licence, alaska waterfowl stamp, federal duck stamp (85%) black bear, deer, caribou, moose, wolf guide required (85%) licence, locking tag, harvest ticket or permit (85%) brown or grizzly bear, dall sheep, mountain goat guide required (85%) licence, locking tag, often a draw permit (85%) bison, muskox, elk guide required (85%) draw or registration permits (80%) - harvest tickets and locking tags must be in hand before the hunt. the locking tag goes on the animal (80%). licences, draws and fees item fee (usd) checked --- --- --- nonresident alien annual hunting licence $630 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: brown or grizzly bear $1,300 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: dall sheep $1,100 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: moose $1,000 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: caribou $850 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: mountain goat $800 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: black bear $600 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: wolverine $500 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: deer $400 (85%) 2026-10-01 nonresident alien locking tag: wolf $100 (85%) 2026-10-01 alaska waterfowl stamp, nonresident alien $10 (85%) 2026-10-01 federal duck stamp (us fish and wildlife service) verify 2026-10-01 - there is no cheaper small game licence for nonresident aliens. the $60 small game licence is for us nonresidents only (80%). - draw permits: applications run in winter for the following season. dates: verify in the drawing permit hunt supplement. - guide fees are private and large. ask for written quotes. not estimated here. how to apply 1. buy the nonresident alien hunting licence online at the alaska department of fish and game store. do this first: atf needs a copy (85%). 2. file atf form 6nia (see firearms screen). allow 4 to 6 weeks (85%). 3. book a registered alaska guide for any big game. the guide usually handles tags and permits with you (80%). 4. buy locking tags before the hunt. carry harvest tickets (80%). 5. read the current alaska hunting regulations for your gmu (game management unit) (85%). best first trip for you !lean not big game, not yet. the honest first alaska trip for two canadians is a september road trip through yukon to the tok and anchorage country for ptarmigan, grouse and ducks on the nonresident alien licence, with the paperwork done right. it teaches you the border, the licence system and the country. save for a guided black bear or caribou after that. cost line estimate (usd unless noted) note --- --- --- nonresident alien licence, each $630 (85%) official fee waterfowl stamp plus federal duck stamp $10 plus verify official fees atf form 6nia fee: verify, believed none each hunter fuel, heffley creek to tok and back cad $1,400 to $1,600 estimate: about 3,300 km each way, assumption: cad $0.22 per km camping, food and one motel night each way, 12 days, two people cad $1,200 to $1,800 estimate total, split two ways about cad $2,300 to $2,900 each estimate, opinion, at roughly 1.35 cad per usd, assumption - a guided black bear trip, with flights, is a different budget again. three quotes before you decide. opinion. taking firearms there leaving canada - cbsa (canada border services agency): you do not have to stop at a cbsa office when leaving with a non restricted firearm (85%). - recommended: cbsa form bsf407, identification of articles for temporary exportation, listing the serial number, so you can show the rifle was not bought abroad (85%). formerly form y38 (85%). - rcmp (royal canadian mounted police): an export permit from global affairs canada \"may be required\" for firearms, parts or ammunition taken out temporarily for hunting (80%). call global affairs, 343 203 4331, and ask: verify whether it applies to a hunter's own rifle. entering the us - atf (bureau of alcohol, tobacco, firearms and explosives) form 6nia: \"any nonimmigrant alien temporarily bringing firearm(s) and/or ammunition into the united states for legitimate hunting or lawful sporting purposes must first obtain an approved atf form 6nia\" (85%). - the application needs a copy of a valid us state hunting licence. your alaska licence satisfies this (85%). - processing about 4 to 6 weeks. approval valid one year (85%). - at the border, cbp (customs and border protection): present the approved 6nia plus the hunting licence (85%). - the form covers the tikka .308, the 12 gauge and the ammunition you list. one form per hunter: your brother files his own (80%). - form fee: verify, believed none. coming home - cbsa: residents may re import non restricted firearms by presenting a valid pal (possession and acquisition licence) for that class (85%). - declare the firearms. have the bsf407 card ready (85%). - leftover us ammunition coming back: declare it. limits: verify on the cbsa page. !warn this is the complicated part. start the 6nia in june for a september trip. no approved form, no rifle across the line. the guide can rent you one, but ptarmigan with a borrowed gun is not the trip you planned. bringing meat, hides and antlers home alaska's export rules - all brown or grizzly and black bears must be sealed by fish and game before they leave alaska (85%). - sheep rams from listed units must be sealed, and horns may not leave the state before sealing (85%). - sealing is done within 30 days of the kill, or sooner if the permit says so (85%). - a cites (convention on international trade in endangered species) permit is required to ship hides, skulls, meat or other parts of bears, wolves or lynx out of the us. issued by usfws (us fish and wildlife service) offices in anchorage and fairbanks (85%). - usfws form 3-177, declaration of importation or exportation of fish and wildlife, may be required for meat, hides, skulls or trophies leaving the us (85%). canada's import rules - cfia (canadian food inspection agency): a hunter harvested game carcass from the us may enter if the hunter's licence accompanies it and you bring it yourself, not by courier. no cfia quantity limit (80%, search preview). canada.ca was not reachable: verify on the cfia page linked below. - wild game birds you shot in the us are permitted with the hunting permit (80%). bird flu rules change fast: verify before the trip. - cbsa declares everything. keep the alaska licence and tags with the meat (80%). bc's import rule (cwd) - cwd (chronic wasting disease) has not been found in alaska, but bc's rule does not care where the animal came from (80%). - from the bc synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful item 50 (99%): - \"to possess any part of a cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose), that was killed outside of british columbia, other than (1) the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck, and ribs, or (2) the hide, antlers, and skull plate if, before being brought into british columbia, the part is cleaned in a manner that removes all tissue.\" (99%) - so an alaska caribou or sitka deer comes home boned out, antlers on a skull plate scraped clean. item 51 also bans using any out of province cervid part for hunting in bc (99%). - you also drive through yukon, which bans cervid carcasses and parts except cleaned skull caps with antlers, cleaned teeth, meat detached from head and backbone, finished mounts and tanned hides (85%). blaze orange rules - \"alaska does not require wearing hunter orange clothing, but investigators consistently have found that it reduces hunting accidents.\" (85%, alaska hunter education page) - some youth hunts require blaze orange vests or jackets (85%). - my lean: orange hat at minimum. you will be hunting the same ptarmigan slopes as locals in september. before you go !rule paperwork is part of the hunt. a trip that starts with a refused rifle at the border ends there. !mistake - thinking \"nonresident\" rules apply to you. canadians are nonresident aliens and need a guide for all big game. - applying for the 6nia before buying the alaska licence. atf wants the licence copy with the form. - packing a caribou head for the taxidermist. yukon and bc both say no. !field ptarmigan hold in willow above treeline in september. walk the brush with the 12 gauge, modified choke, 6 shot, and watch for bears on every berry slope. sources - alaska big game guide requirements (https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=hunting.guidereqs) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: nonresident alien rule, 100 yards, kindred. - alaska licence, stamp and tag prices (https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=huntlicense.prices) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: $630, tag fees, waterfowl stamp. - alaska licence pricing list (https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=license.pricinglist) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - alaska waterfowl hunting requirements (https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=waterfowlhunting.requirements) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - shipping or transporting game from alaska (https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=hunting.shipping) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: sealing, cites, form 3-177. - alaska hunter education page (https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=hunting.huntered) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: hunter orange. - 2026 to 2027 alaska hunting regulations pdf (https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/static/regulations/wildliferegulations/pdfs/regulations_complete.pdf) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - atf form 6nia page (https://www.atf.gov/firearms/docs/form/form-6nia-applicationpermit-temporary-importation-firearms-and-ammunition) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - atf 6nia information collection notice (https://www.atf.gov/rules-and-regulations/form-and-information-collection-notices/application/permit-temporary-importation-firearms-and-ammunition-nonimmigrant-aliens) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: 4 to 6 weeks, one year, licence copy. - cbp: bringing a firearm into the us for hunting (https://www.help.cbp.gov/s/article/article-1204?language=en_us) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - cbsa: firearms and weapons border requirements (https://www.cbsa-asfc.gc.ca/import/iefw-iefa-eng.html) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: pal on re entry, bsf407, no stop on exit. canada.ca blocked: verify. - cbsa memorandum d19-13-2 (https://www.cbsa-asfc.gc.ca/publications/dm-md/d19/d19-13-2-eng.html) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. - rcmp: exporting firearms (https://rcmp.ca/en/firearms/importing-and-exporting/exporting) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: export permit may be required. - cfia: bringing food into canada for personal use (https://inspection.canada.ca/en/food-safety-consumers/bring-food-personal-use) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01. site blocked from this environment: verify. - yukon wildlife health and cwd (https://yukon.ca/en/wildlife-health) (search preview), checked 2026-10-01: yukon import rule. - bc hunting and trapping regulations synopsis 2026 to 2028, page 12, it's unlawful items 50 and 51, read in full, checked 2026-10-01. - note: us and federal pages were read through search previews, so facts are capped at 85%. quotes are shown with hyphens removed to fit the app style. fees in us dollars. "}],"glossary":{"ACTS":"Firearm safety rules. Assume every firearm is loaded. Control the muzzle direction at all times. Trigger finger off the trigger and out of the trigger guard. See that the firearm is unloaded: PROVE it safe <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","Antlerless":"A deer with no antlers. Usually a doe or a fawn. Some seasons only allow antlerless.","ATV":"All terrain vehicle. A quad.","Backstop":"What is behind your target that will stop the bullet. Earth is good. Sky is never a backstop.","Bag limit":"Most animals of a kind you may take in one day (birds) or one year (big game).","Bore sight":"Lining up the scope with the barrel before shooting, to get on paper fast.","CORE":"Conservation and Outdoor Recreation Education. BC hunter education course.","Crown land":"Public land owned by the province. Hunting is often allowed unless closed.","CWD":"Chronic Wasting Disease. A fatal brain disease in deer, elk and moose.","ECCC":"Environment and Climate Change Canada. Sets migratory bird rules.","FSR":"Forest Service Road. Gravel resource road on Crown land.","FWID":"Fish and Wildlife ID. Your 9 digit BC hunter number. Replaced the old hunter number card.","Glassing":"Scanning country slowly with binoculars to find animals.","LEH":"Limited Entry Hunting. A draw for special hunts.","MGBHP":"Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit. Federal permit to hunt ducks and geese. Includes the habitat stamp.","MU":"Management Unit. A numbered hunting area, like 3 17. Rules differ by MU.","PAL":"Possession and Acquisition Licence. Federal licence to own and buy firearms.","Patterning":"Shooting a shotgun at paper to see how the shot spreads.","PFD":"Personal flotation device. A life jacket.","Picatinny rail":"A standard mounting strip with slots, used to attach scope rings.","Species licence":"The BC \"tag\" for a big game animal. Paper, notched when you kill the animal.","Synopsis":"The BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis. Plain language summary of the law.","WILD":"Wildlife Information and Licensing Data. BC online system to buy licences.","Zero":"The distance where your bullet hits exactly where the crosshair points.","4 point buck":"A buck with 4 or more points on one antler, not counting the brow tine. Each point at least 2.5 cm long and longer than wide (80%, <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the synopsis).","Bachelor group":"A small summer group of bucks (2 to 4) that breaks up in fall.","Brow tine":"The short point near the base of the antler, just above the eye.","Browse":"Twigs, buds and leaves of shrubs and trees that deer eat. Also the sign left when they eat them.","Button buck":"A male fawn with small bumps instead of antlers. Counts as antlerless.","Flag":"A white tail's raised, waving white tail when it runs from danger.","Forb":"A small leafy flowering plant that is not a grass.","Metatarsal gland":"A scent patch on the outside of a deer's hind leg. Long on mule deer, small on white tails.","Pre rut":"The weeks before the rut. Bucks spar, rub trees and start checking does.","Rattling":"Clacking antlers together to sound like fighting bucks.","Riparian":"The green strip of land along a river or creek.","Rub":"Bark scraped off a sapling by a buck's antlers.","Rut":"The breeding season. Mule deer mid November to mid December. White tails peak in November.","Scrape":"A pawed bare patch of dirt under a low branch, made by a buck around the rut.","Spot and stalk":"Find the animal with binoculars from far away, then sneak within range.","Stand hunting":"Sitting in one spot, such as a tree stand or ground blind, on a trail and waiting.","Still hunting":"Walking very slowly through cover, stopping often to look and listen.","Stot":"The mule deer bounce. All 4 feet hit the ground and push off together.","Thermals":"Air sliding up a slope as it warms (morning) and down as it cools (evening). They carry your scent.","Tine":"A branch of an antler. Also called a point.","Winter range":"The lower, sunnier slopes where deer spend the winter.","PROVE":"Firearm safety steps. Point the firearm in the safest available direction. Remove all cartridges. Observe the chamber. Verify the feeding path. Examine the bore <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","Zone of fire":"The slice of ground in front of a hunter where they may shoot, about 45 degrees. Never swing past it.","Cold shock":"The gasp and fast breathing in the first minute in cold water.","1 10 1 principle":"A name for cold water stages: about 1 minute of cold shock, 10 minutes of useful movement, 1 hour to unconsciousness. A guide, not exact timing.","HELP":"Heat Escape Lessening Posture. Knees up and arms crossed in the water to slow heat loss.","PCOC":"Pleasure Craft Operator Card. Proof of competency to run a boat fitted with a motor.","FVPA":"Firearm Violence Prevention Act. BC law in force 1 October 2026 on loaded firearms and shooting from vehicles and boats.","STOP":"Stop, Think, Observe, Plan. What to do when lost, disoriented or hurt.","Trip plan":"A written plan of who, where, route and return time, left with someone at home.","Satellite messenger":"A device that sends SOS and texts by satellite where there is no cell signal.","RAPP":"Report All Poachers and Polluters. BC line to report wildlife and environmental violations: 1 877 952 7277 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","Tourniquet":"A band tightened above a wound on an arm or leg to stop life threatening bleeding.","SOFTT":"Special Operations Forces Tactical Tourniquet. A commercial windlass tourniquet.","Stalking":"Closing the distance on an animal you have already seen.","Record of Receipt":"A written note that must go with game carried by someone other than the hunter who killed it.","Evidence of sex":"The body part that must stay naturally attached to show if the animal was male or female.","Edible portions":"Meat you must not waste. Big game: neck, ribs, all 4 quarters and the loins. Game birds: both breasts <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","Loaded":"In BC, live ammunition in the chamber or in a magazine attached to the firearm <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","Road allowance":"The strip along a road where hunting and shooting are banned <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. See the BC rules session.","No Shooting Area":"A mapped area where firing a firearm is banned.","Motor Vehicle Closed Area":"An area where some or all motor vehicles are banned.","Ecological reserve":"Protected land. No hunting and no firing a firearm or bow <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","Enclosed land":"Under the Trespass Act, land with a lawful fence, a natural boundary, or trespass signs at each normal access point.","Non toxic shot":"Shot that is not lead, such as steel, bismuth or tungsten. Required for ducks and geese.","Magazine plug":"A filler that limits a shotgun to 3 shells in total. Must not come out unless the gun is taken apart.","Compulsory reporting":"A required report of a kill within 30 days, for listed species and areas.","Field dressing":"Removing the guts soon after the kill.","Gutless method":"Taking the meat off without opening the body cavity.","Conservation officer":"BC wildlife law enforcement officer.","Puddle duck":"A duck that feeds by tipping up in shallow water and jumps straight up to fly. Mallard, teal, wigeon.","Diver":"A duck that dives for food and runs along the water to take off. Goldeneye, bufflehead.","Jump shooting":"Sneaking up on ducks on small water and shooting as they flush.","Pass shooting":"Shooting ducks as they fly over between roost and feeding areas.","Decoy spread":"The pattern you set decoys in.","J hook":"A decoy spread shaped like a J, with an open landing pocket in front of the hunter.","Landing zone":"The open water in a decoy spread where you want ducks to land.","Steel shot":"Non toxic shotgun pellets made of steel. Legal for ducks.","Choke":"The narrowing at the end of a shotgun barrel that controls the spread of shot. Must be rated for steel to shoot steel.","Power boat":"Under BC rules, a boat with a motor ready to use.","Covey":"A fall or winter flock of quail.","ORV":"Off Road Vehicle. An ATV, dirt bike or side by side under BC law.","Cultivated land":"Farmed land, orchards included. You need permission to hunt it.","Grazing lease":"Crown land leased for livestock. You need the lease holder's OK while livestock are on it.","Non resident Canadian":"A Canadian hunting in a province or territory where they do not live. Each place defines it a little differently.","Outfitter":"A licensed business that guides paying hunters. Required for most non resident big game hunts in Yukon, Alberta and Alaska.","Hunter host":"An Alberta resident with a Hunter Host Licence who accompanies up to 2 non resident hunters a year, unpaid <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","WIN":"Wildlife Identification Number. Alberta's lifetime hunter number.","Wildlife Certificate":"Alberta's base licence, bought before any hunting licence.","HAL":"Hunting, Angling and Trapping Licence system. Saskatchewan's online licence account.","GHA":"Game Hunting Area. Manitoba's numbered hunting zones.","WMU":"Wildlife Management Unit. Alberta's numbered hunting zones.","GMU":"Game Management Unit. Alaska's numbered hunting zones.","Nonresident alien":"Alaska's term for a hunter who is not a US citizen and does not live in the US. Needs a guide for every big game animal <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","Locking tag":"Alaska's paid big game tag that locks onto the animal after the kill.","Sealing":"An agency placing a seal on a bear hide or skull, or sheep horns, before export.","CBSA":"Canada Border Services Agency. Canadian customs at the border.","CBP":"Customs and Border Protection. US customs at the border.","ATF":"Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives. US agency that approves Form 6NIA.","ATF Form 6NIA":"US permit a non US hunter must have approved before bringing a firearm and ammunition into the US <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.","BSF407":"CBSA card listing serial numbered goods you take out of Canada, so you can prove they were not bought abroad.","CFIA":"Canadian Food Inspection Agency. Sets rules for food and animal products entering Canada.","CITES":"Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species. Permit needed to export some animal parts across borders.","Skull plate":"The piece of skull the antlers grow from, kept with the antlers when the rest of the head is left behind."},"regs":{"edition":"BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028 (in force 1 July 2026 to 30 June 2028)","lastChecked":"2026-10-01","note":"Values marked from search extracts were read in search previews of official pages, not the full page. They are capped at 85% until the full source is read. Blank values are VERIFY.","links":[{"name":"BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/regulations-synopsis"},{"name":"BC species licences","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences"},{"name":"BC Fish and Wildlife ID (FWID)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/wild-system/fish-wildlife-id"},{"name":"BC hunting FAQ","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/frequently-asked-questions"},{"name":"Federal Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit","url":"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/permit.html"},{"name":"ECCC BC migratory bird regulations summary","url":"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html"}],"hours":{"bigGame":{"beforeSunriseMin":60,"afterSunsetMin":60,"certainty":80,"text":"No hunting from 1 hour after sunset to 1 hour before sunrise. Applies to big game and upland birds (all wildlife except migratory game birds)."},"migratory":{"beforeSunriseMin":30,"afterSunsetMin":30,"certainty":85,"text":"Ducks and geese: half an hour before sunrise to half an hour after sunset (south of 60 degrees north)."}},"items":[{"group":"licence","key":"fwid","label":"Hunter number","value":"Now called FWID (Fish and Wildlife ID), 9 digits. Old hunter number plus 3 digits at the front. No physical card.","certainty":85,"source":"BC Fish and Wildlife ID page (search extract)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/wild-system/fish-wildlife-id","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"licence","key":"carry","label":"What to carry","value":"Photo ID. Know your FWID number. All paper species licences for the year (used and unused). Federal bird permit (paper or phone) for ducks and geese.","certainty":85,"source":"BC hunting FAQ; ECCC permit page (search extracts)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/frequently-asked-questions","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"licence","key":"huntlic","label":"Resident hunting licence","value":"$25 + $7 surcharge. Electronic only. Bought in WILD online or at Service BC / FrontCounter BC.","note":"GST: VERIFY at checkout.","certainty":80,"source":"BC hunting licences page (search extract)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/hunting-licences","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"licence","key":"year","label":"Licence year","value":"1 April to 31 March","certainty":85,"source":"Hunting Licensing Regulation, s.1 (search extract)","url":"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/8_99","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"licence","key":"muledeer","label":"Mule deer species licence","value":"$12 + $3 surcharge","certainty":80,"source":"BC species licences page (search extract)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"licence","key":"wtdeer","label":"White tailed deer species licence","value":"$12 + $3 surcharge","certainty":80,"source":"BC species licences page (search extract)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"licence","key":"print","label":"Buying species licences","value":"Cannot be printed at home. Online orders are mailed (about 14 days). Hunting soon: buy in person.","certainty":85,"source":"BC species licences page (search extract)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"licence","key":"wait","label":"Waiting period after buying","value":"","note":"A 2 day waiting period exists for some species. Does it cover deer? VERIFY.","certainty":50,"source":"BC species licences page","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"licence","key":"upland","label":"Quail and grouse licence (residents)","value":"No species licence. BC hunting licence only.","certainty":80,"source":"BC species licences page (search extract)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/species-licences","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"migratory","key":"mgbhp","label":"Federal Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit","value":"$17 + tax, stamp included. 2026 e permit valid 1 Aug 2026 to 30 Jun 2027. Buy online, carry on phone or paper.","certainty":80,"source":"ECCC permit page (search extract)","url":"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/permit.html","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"safety","key":"dwelling","label":"Shooting near buildings","value":"No hunting or shooting within 100 m of a church, school, playground, regional park, or a dwelling house or farm or ranch building occupied by people or domestic animals. The owner exemption covers slaughtering livestock only, not hunting.","certainty":85,"source":"BC synopsis 2026 to 2028 and B.C. Reg. 190/84 (search preview)","url":"https://www.bclaws.gov.bc.ca/civix/document/id/complete/statreg/190_84_01","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"safety","key":"companion","label":"Unlicensed companion","value":"May come along and watch, and help pack meat out, if not carrying a firearm or weapon and not hunting. Licensed hunter must stay with the meat unless a transport record is filled in.","certainty":80,"source":"BC hunting FAQ; Wildlife Act s.1 (search extracts)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/sports-culture/recreation/fishing-hunting/hunting/frequently-asked-questions","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"deerR3","key":"r3bag","label":"Region 3 deer bag limit","value":"","note":"Search extract says 3 combined (1 mule deer, 2 white tailed). VERIFY in Region 3 synopsis.","certainty":60,"source":"Region 3 synopsis (search extract)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-3-thompson.pdf","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"deerR3","key":"r3mu","label":"MU (Management Unit) for Heffley Creek and Sun Peaks","value":"","note":"VERIFY on official MU map.","certainty":0,"source":"Region 3 synopsis map","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"deerR3","key":"r3mule","label":"Region 3 mule deer season (your MU)","value":"","note":"VERIFY dates and antler rules.","certainty":0,"source":"Region 3 synopsis","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"quail","key":"r8quail","label":"Region 8 California quail","value":"1 Oct to 30 Nov. Daily bag 10, possession 30. MUs 8-1 to 8-15 and 8-21 to 8-26.","certainty":70,"source":"Region 8 synopsis (search extract)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis-region-8-okanagan.pdf","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"quail","key":"r3quail","label":"Region 3 California quail","value":"","note":"Is there a season near Heffley Creek? VERIFY.","certainty":0,"source":"Region 3 synopsis","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"safety","key":"fvpa","label":"BC Firearm Violence Prevention Act","value":"In force 1 October 2026. Offences include transporting loaded firearms in a vehicle and shooting from vehicles and boats. Hunting exemptions come by regulation.","note":"Exact hunting exemptions: VERIFY.","certainty":85,"source":"BC news release (search preview)","url":"https://news.gov.bc.ca/releases/2026PSSG0037-000460","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"safety","key":"rapp","label":"Report poaching (RAPP)","value":"1 877 952 7277, 24/7, can stay anonymous","certainty":85,"source":"BC RAPP page (search preview)","url":"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/environment/natural-resource-stewardship/natural-resource-law-enforcement/conservation-officer-service/cos-rapp","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"migratory","key":"ducksR3","label":"Duck season near Kamloops","value":"","note":"Search previews gave 3 different date ranges. VERIFY in the ECCC BC summary for your district.","certainty":0,"source":"ECCC BC summary","url":"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"migratory","key":"duckbag","label":"Duck daily bag (BC)","value":"8 ducks a day; at most 4 pintails, 4 canvasbacks, 2 goldeneyes, 2 harlequins. Possession 24.","note":"VERIFY for your district.","certainty":70,"source":"ECCC BC summary (search preview)","url":"https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/migratory-game-bird-hunting/regulations-provincial-territorial-summaries/british-columbia.html","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"migratory","key":"plug","label":"Shotgun for ducks","value":"10 gauge or smaller, max 3 shells (chamber + magazine) with a plug that needs disassembly to remove. Non toxic shot only.","certainty":85,"source":"Migratory Birds Regulations, 2022 (search preview)","url":"https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-2022-105/FullText.html","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"migratory","key":"boat","label":"Ducks from a boat","value":"No hunting migratory birds from a boat moving under motor or sail. Beached, anchored or tied to a fixed blind is OK. A motorboat may be used to retrieve birds.","certainty":80,"source":"Migratory Birds Regulations, 2022 s.41; BC synopsis (search preview)","url":"https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/regulations/SOR-2022-105/FullText.html","checked":"2026-10-01"},{"group":"quail","key":"chukar","label":"Chukar (Regions 3 and 8)","value":"","note":"Preview suggests 1 Oct to 30 Nov, bag 5. VERIFY both regions.","certainty":60,"source":"Synopsis (search preview)","checked":"2026-10-01"}]},"bases":[{"id":"heffley","name":"Heffley Creek","lat":50.86,"lon":-120.27,"region":"Region 3 Thompson","note":"Approximate centre point. Seconds of difference only."},{"id":"sunpeaks","name":"Sun Peaks","lat":50.88,"lon":-119.89,"region":"Region 3 Thompson","note":"Approximate village centre."},{"id":"mission","name":"Mission","lat":49.13,"lon":-122.31,"region":"Region 2 Lower Mainland","note":"Approximate city centre."},{"id":"okanagan","name":"Okanagan (Kelowna)","lat":49.89,"lon":-119.49,"region":"Region 8 Okanagan","note":"Approximate city centre."}],"field":{"defaults":{"firstHunt":"2026-10-15"},"legalFlow":[{"q":"Do I have my licences on me?","help":"Photo ID, FWID (Fish and Wildlife ID) number, species licence for this animal (deer), federal bird permit (ducks and geese).","stop":"No licence, no shot. Go get it."},{"q":"Is it within legal hours right now?","help":"Check the Legal light card. Ducks and geese have shorter hours than deer.","stop":"Too early or too late. Wait."},{"q":"Is the season open for this species, sex and antler type in this MU (Management Unit) today?","help":"Check the synopsis table for your exact MU. Antler rules like 4 point count.","stop":"Not open for this animal here today. Let it walk."},{"q":"Am I 100% sure what it is?","help":"Species, sex, and antler points counted. For ducks: species identified before you shoot.","stop":"Not sure means no. Watch it go and learn from it."},{"q":"Am I off the road, out of the vehicle, and not shooting along or across a road?","help":"Engine off, feet on the ground. VERIFY exact road rules in the synopsis.","stop":"Get off the road and out of the vehicle first."},{"q":"Am I more than 100 m from any occupied house, farm or ranch building?","help":"BC rule. Includes your brother's house. 85% (search preview of the synopsis).","stop":"Too close to a building. No shot."},{"q":"Am I allowed to hunt on this land?","help":"Crown land, or written or clear permission from the owner. Not a park or no shooting area.","stop":"No permission or closed area. No shot."},{"q":"Is there a safe backstop and nothing behind it?","help":"Earth behind the animal. No skyline shot. No people, roads, livestock or buildings beyond.","stop":"No safe backstop. Bullets travel kilometres. No shot."},{"q":"Is it a clean, ethical shot within my tested range?","help":"Broadside or quartering away, still animal, steady rest, inside your practised distance.","stop":"Wait for a better angle or get closer. A wounded animal is the worst outcome."}],"cards":[{"id":"emergency","title":"Emergency","sub":"Call, location, bleeding","hot":true,"html":"<h2>Emergency</h2><ol><li><strong>Call 911.</strong> If no signal, use a satellite messenger or iPhone Emergency SOS via satellite (if your model has it).</li><li>Say where you are: GPS (Global Positioning System) coordinates from your map app, nearest road, landmark.</li><li>Severe bleeding: firm direct pressure. Tourniquet high and tight on an arm or leg if pressure fails. Note the time.</li><li>Stay together. Keep warm. Stay put once help is called.</li></ol><h3>Numbers</h3><ul><li>Emergency: 911</li><li>RAPP (Report All Poachers and Polluters): 1 877 952 7277 <span class=\"cert c-mid\">85%</span></li><li>FrontCounter BC (licences): 1 877 855 3222 <span class=\"cert c-mid\">80%</span></li></ul><p class=\"muted\">Write your brother's number and the land address here on paper too.</p>"},{"id":"tag","title":"Tag it","sub":"Steps at the kill","html":"<h2>Right after the kill</h2><ol><li>Confirm the animal is dead. Approach from behind, rifle ready.</li><li><strong>Cancel (notch) the species licence immediately, before you touch or move the animal.</strong> <span class=\"cert c-mid\">85%</span></li><li>Follow the notch instructions printed on the licence itself (date and month). <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> exact fields.</li><li>Keep evidence of sex attached: antlers on the head for bucks, or as the regulation lists. <span class=\"cert c-lo\">75%</span></li><li>Take photos: animal, licence, location.</li><li>Check if your MU needs compulsory reporting or inspection. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span></li></ol>"},{"id":"dress","title":"After the shot","sub":"Wait, mark, track","html":"<h2>After the shot</h2><ol><li>Stay still. Watch where it runs. Listen.</li><li>Mark the spot you shot from and where it stood (phone map pin).</li><li>Wait at least 30 minutes before following (tip, opinion).</li><li>Go to where it stood. Look for blood, hair, tracks.</li><li>Follow the trail slowly. Mark the last blood each time.</li><li>Lost the trail? Grid search from the last blood. Get help.</li></ol><p class=\"muted\">Full field dressing steps come in Session 13.</p>"}],"checklists":[{"id":"licences","title":"Licences and paperwork","items":["Find my FWID (Fish and Wildlife ID) in WILD online","BC resident hunting licence 2026 to 2027 bought","Mule deer species licence bought IN PERSON (paper)","White tailed deer species licence bought IN PERSON (paper)","Federal Migratory Game Bird Hunting Permit (e permit on phone)","PAL (Possession and Acquisition Licence) card in wallet","Photo ID in wallet","Brother: his own hunting licence, deer licences and bird permit bought"]},{"id":"before","title":"Before every hunt","items":["Check in season regulation changes (official synopsis page)","Check area closures and fire restrictions (BC Wildfire Service)","Check road and FSR (Forest Service Road) closures","Check CWD (Chronic Wasting Disease) zones and rules","Check the season is open for my MU (Management Unit), species and sex","Legal light times for today","Trip plan left with someone (where, when back)","Weather checked","Phone charged, offline maps downloaded","Licences, ID, permit on me"]},{"id":"after","title":"After the kill","items":["Licence notched before touching the animal","Photos taken","Evidence of sex left attached","Field dressed fast","Meat cooling (open cavity, shade, game bags)","Compulsory reporting checked (VERIFY for my MU)","Journal entry written"]}]},"flashcards":[{"id":"id-mule-vs-wt","deck":"Species ID","front":"Which deer is which? Name the clues.","diagram":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 420\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"420\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"410\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 6\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mule deer</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">White tail</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Antlers (one side, seen from front)</text>\n  <!-- mule deer antler: forks that split again -->\n  <g stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M100 190 L100 160\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 160 L70 110\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 160 L130 110\"/>\n    <path d=\"M70 110 L55 72\"/>\n    <path d=\"M70 110 L85 75\"/>\n    <path d=\"M130 110 L118 74\"/>\n    <path d=\"M130 110 L148 76\"/>\n    <path d=\"M100 178 L86 168\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Forks that fork again</text>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(\"Y\" on a \"Y\")</text>\n  <!-- whitetail antler: one main beam, tines up -->\n  <g stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M250 190 Q240 120 300 100 Q345 90 360 120\"/>\n    <path d=\"M262 140 L258 112\"/>\n    <path d=\"M285 108 L282 72\"/>\n    <path d=\"M315 98 L318 64\"/>\n    <path d=\"M343 102 L350 76\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"212\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">One main beam,</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">single tines up</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Tail and rump (seen from behind)</text>\n  <!-- mule deer rump -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"330\" rx=\"48\" ry=\"48\" fill=\"#f3ead8\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"94\" y=\"290\" width=\"12\" height=\"62\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f3ead8\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"94\" y=\"340\" width=\"12\" height=\"18\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#23261f\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"396\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Big white rump,</text>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"413\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">thin tail, black tip</text>\n  <!-- whitetail flag -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"300\" cy=\"345\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"34\" fill=\"#b08a5e\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M300 340 C270 320 268 285 285 275 L300 270 L315 275 C332 285 330 320 300 340 Z\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"300\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">flag up</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"396\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wide brown tail,</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"413\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">white below, waved up</text>\n</svg>\n","back":"Left: mule deer. Forked antlers, big ears, thin white tail with a black tip, big white rump. Right: white tailed deer. One main beam with single tines, wide brown tail flagged white when alarmed."},{"id":"id-stot","deck":"Species ID","front":"A deer bounces away on all 4 feet at once. Which species?","back":"Mule deer. The bounce is called stotting. White tails gallop with the tail up."},{"id":"id-duck-shapes","deck":"Duck silhouettes","front":"Name the 4 ducks by shape and flight.","diagram":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 440\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four simple duck flight silhouettes: mallard large with broad wings, teal small and fast in tight flocks, wigeon with white wing patch, goldeneye diver with wings set far back and white sides.\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"440\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"430\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <line x1=\"10\" y1=\"220\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"220\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <!-- Mallard: big body, broad wings -->\n  <g>\n    <path d=\"M30 95 L100 70 L170 95 L100 88 Z\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"38\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"56\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <rect x=\"66\" y=\"84\" width=\"16\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#4a5fa8\"/>\n    <rect x=\"118\" y=\"84\" width=\"16\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#4a5fa8\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"168\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mallard</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"188\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Big. Slow wingbeats.</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"205\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Loose groups.</text>\n  </g>\n  <!-- Teal: small, flock of three -->\n  <g fill=\"#23261f\">\n    <path d=\"M240 80 L268 68 L296 80 L268 77 Z\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"268\" cy=\"84\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"17\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"268\" cy=\"64\" r=\"5\"/>\n    <path d=\"M300 110 L328 98 L356 110 L328 107 Z\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"328\" cy=\"114\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"17\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"328\" cy=\"94\" r=\"5\"/>\n    <path d=\"M250 130 L278 118 L306 130 L278 127 Z\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"278\" cy=\"134\" rx=\"7\" ry=\"17\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"278\" cy=\"114\" r=\"5\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"168\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Green winged teal</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"188\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Small. Very fast.</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"205\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Tight flock twists.</text>\n  <!-- Wigeon: white shoulder patch, pointed tail -->\n  <g>\n    <path d=\"M40 305 L100 282 L160 305 L100 298 Z\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"308\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"32\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"94,338 106,338 100,352\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"270\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <rect x=\"96\" y=\"264\" width=\"8\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"72,293 90,288 90,296 72,300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"128,293 110,288 110,296 128,300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"378\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wigeon</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"398\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">White wing patch.</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"415\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Whistle call.</text>\n  </g>\n  <!-- Goldeneye / diver: wings set back, short neck, white sides -->\n  <g>\n    <ellipse cx=\"300\" cy=\"300\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"34\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <path d=\"M244 318 L300 300 L356 318 L300 312 Z\" fill=\"#23261f\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"262,312 282,307 282,314 262,318\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <polygon points=\"338,312 318,307 318,314 338,318\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"262\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"305\" cy=\"266\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"378\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Goldeneye (diver)</text>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"398\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wings set far back.</text>\n    <text x=\"300\" y=\"415\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wings whistle.</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n","back":"Mallard: big, slow wingbeats. Green winged teal: small, very fast, flock twists. Wigeon: white wing patch, whistles. Goldeneye: a diver, wings set far back, wings whistle."},{"id":"id-puddle-diver","deck":"Duck silhouettes","front":"Puddle duck or diver: how do they take off?","back":"Puddle ducks (mallard, teal, wigeon) jump straight up. Divers (goldeneye, bufflehead, scaup) run along the water to get airborne."},{"id":"tr-deer","deck":"Tracks","front":"Deer track: which way did it walk, and what are the 2 small dots behind it?","diagram":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 320\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"320\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer track (front foot)</text>\n  <!-- two toes (halves of heart), point = toe tips at top -->\n  <path d=\"M195 70 C180 75 160 110 158 160 C156 200 168 225 185 228 C195 229 197 215 197 200 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M205 70 C220 75 240 110 242 160 C244 200 232 225 215 228 C205 229 203 215 203 200 Z\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- dewclaws -->\n  <ellipse cx=\"170\" cy=\"262\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <ellipse cx=\"230\" cy=\"262\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"6\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <!-- direction arrow -->\n  <g stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"#e8590c\">\n    <line x1=\"320\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"90\"/>\n    <path d=\"M320 70 L308 94 L332 94 Z\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"252\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Travel</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\">direction</text>\n  <!-- labels -->\n  <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Pointed tips</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">= front</text>\n  <line x1=\"160\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">2 toes</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">(split hoof)</text>\n  <line x1=\"185\" y1=\"228\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"215\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"210\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Round heel</text>\n  <line x1=\"162\" y1=\"262\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"270\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">Dewclaws:</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">deep mud, snow,</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"300\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\">or running</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">About 6 to 10 cm long</text>\n</svg>\n","back":"The pointed tips point the way it walked. The 2 dots are dewclaws, which show in soft mud, snow or when running."},{"id":"tr-hare-vs-deer-browse","deck":"Tracks","front":"A twig is bitten off. Clean 45 degree cut, or ragged and torn. Who ate it?","back":"Clean angled cut: hare or rabbit (they have upper incisors). Ragged, torn tip: deer, moose or elk (no upper front teeth, they tear)."},{"id":"th-morning","deck":"Field craft","front":"Morning on a sunny slope. Which way does your scent go, and where should you be?","diagram":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 520\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"th-arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n  <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"520\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Morning: sun warms slope, air rises</text>\n  <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"62\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#e8590c\" fill-opacity=\"0.6\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M10,240 L120,200 L260,90 L300,80 L390,150 L390,240 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M90,190 L150,160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M160,148 L215,108\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M225,96 L262,68\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <circle cx=\"275\" cy=\"76\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"275\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">You</text>\n  <ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"212\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"62\" y=\"186\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deer</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"230\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Be ABOVE. Scent goes up, away.</text>\n  <line x1=\"20\" y1=\"262\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"262\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"292\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Evening: slope cools, air sinks</text>\n  <circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"322\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M10,500 L120,460 L260,350 L300,340 L390,410 L390,500 Z\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <path d=\"M245,352 L195,390\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M185,400 L135,437\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M120,450 L70,470\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#th-arrow)\"/>\n  <ellipse cx=\"270\" cy=\"338\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"8\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"320\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deer</text>\n  <circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"470\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"448\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">You</text>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"485\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Be BELOW. Scent sinks, away.</text>\n</svg>\n","back":"Warming air rises uphill. Be above the deer in the morning, below them in the evening when cooling air sinks."}]};